"date-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Why counting days on a calendar goes wrong</h2>
<p>Adding days to a date sounds like arithmetic, but it is really geography: you are travelling across a map of months that refuse to be the same size. January gives you 31 days, February gives you 28 — 29 in a leap year — and April only 30. A count of "90 days from 15 November" crosses three month boundaries and a year boundary, and every crossing is a chance to land a day early or a day late. That one day is rarely harmless. A visa overstayed by a day, a contract notice served a day late, a warranty claimed a day after expiry — the rules written as "within N days" are enforced to the day, not to the nearest week.</p>
<p>The weekday matters almost as much as the date itself. Deadlines that land on a Saturday or Sunday often roll to the next working day in one office and expire on the spot in another, which is why this calculator always shows the weekday next to the landing date: seeing "Sunday, 14 March" in the result is the prompt to check the rule before you rely on it. And when the rule is written in working days rather than calendar days, the honest tool for the job is the <a href="/business-days-calculator/">Business Days Calculator</a>, which skips weekends as it counts.</p>
<p>One more distinction saves endless confusion: this calculator counts calendar days the way laws, visas and contracts count them — every day on the calendar, weekends included. If a result ever looks one day off, the usual cause is inclusive counting (counting both the first and the last day). The standard here excludes the start day and counts each day after it, which matches how "90 days from today" is meant everywhere from passport offices to probation terms.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Worked examples: adding and subtracting days</h2>
<p>Three calculations, worked from one fixed starting date — <strong>Tuesday 17 November 2026</strong> — so you can check the tool above against them, any day you visit:</p>
