"days-between-dates": '''<section class="section"><h2>How the count between two dates is actually used</h2>
<p>The gap between two dates is one of those numbers that quietly runs adult life. Employers count it for probation periods and notice clauses. Landlords count it for tenancy lengths. Couples count it for anniversaries, parents count it for a baby's age in weeks, and anyone who has ever booked a refundable ticket has counted the days left in the cancellation window. The arithmetic looks trivial — subtract one date from the other — until February, leap years and month lengths get involved, which is exactly where mental maths starts dropping days.</p>
<p>The question that causes the most arguments is whether to count the first and last day. This calculator gives the standard difference: the number of full days you must travel to get from the first date to the second, not counting the start day itself. That is the count used by contracts, airlines and age calculations. If your situation genuinely needs both endpoints included — some event and rental counts work that way — add one to the result and note that you did; the important thing is to know which convention your rule uses before you act on the number.</p>
<p>The result here also breaks the gap into weeks and months, because humans plan in those units even when rules are written in days. "In 84 days" means little until it becomes "12 weeks from now" — and for the reverse job, starting from today and landing on the date a set number of days away, the <a href="/date-calculator/">Date Calculator</a> is the companion tool.</p></section>
