"business-days-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Working days are a different currency</h2>
<p>When a supplier promises delivery "in 10 working days" or a bank says a transfer clears "within 3 business days", the calendar on the wall is the wrong measuring stick. Business days skip Saturdays and Sundays, so 10 working days is normally 14 calendar days — and longer again across a holiday week. Quoting a customer the calendar count when the contract means working days is one of the most common small-business mistakes, and it is made by smart people because the two counts feel interchangeable. They are not: over a month, the difference is eight to ten days.</p>
<p>This calculator counts working days between two dates with weekends removed, and it can also work the other way — start from a date, add a number of business days, and land on the real deadline. That second direction is the one project plans are built on: "the designer gets 5 working days from Monday" becomes a date you can put in a calendar, not a guess.</p>
<p>The honest limitation is holidays. Public holidays differ by country, state and even city, and no single list can be right for everyone — so this tool removes weekends only and says so plainly. If your range crosses a public holiday where you work, subtract it from the count shown. For most contract, banking and delivery purposes, weekend-only counting plus that one manual adjustment matches how the other side will count it too.</p></section>
