"age-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>What "exact age" really means</h2>
<p>Ask someone their age and they give you a year count. Ask a form, a school or an insurance company, and they want the exact figure: years, months and days, worked out by borrowing across real month lengths. That borrowing is where hand calculations fail. If your birthday is in March and today is in January, you have not yet had this year's birthday, so the year count drops by one and the leftover months and days are counted from your last birthday — through months of 28, 30 and 31 days, with a leap day hiding in some of them. This calculator does that borrowing exactly, the same way an official would.</p>
<p>The totals underneath the headline age are the part people screenshot: your age in total days, weeks and months. They power the milestone culture of the internet — the 10,000th day of your life is a real date worth celebrating, and it can only be found by counting days exactly. Total weeks explain why a baby's age is quoted in weeks long after adults switch to years: at small scales, weeks are simply the honest unit.</p>
<p>Everything is calculated on your device. A date of birth is exactly the kind of personal detail that should never be typed into a server-side form for a ten-second calculation, so this page does the arithmetic in your browser and the date goes nowhere. If you need an age on a past or future date rather than today — "how old will she be when she starts school?" — the <a href="/age-on-date/">Age on a Specific Date</a> calculator answers that version.</p></section>
''',
"age-difference-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Age gaps change meaning across a lifetime</h2>
<p>A two-year gap is enormous between a four-year-old and a six-year-old — one is starting school while the other is halfway through it — and almost invisible between a 44-year-old and a 46-year-old. The numbers in this calculator do not change, but their weight does, which is why the same tool serves such different visitors: parents spacing siblings, couples checking a gap they have never actually measured, and families building trees where a "generation" turns out to be 22 years in one branch and 38 in another.</p>
<p>School systems quietly run on this arithmetic. Cohort cut-offs ("must be five by 1 September") mean two children born weeks apart can land a full school year apart, while children born eleven months apart share a classroom. The exact gap in years, months and days — not the rough difference in birth years — is what decides it, and the same precision matters for eligibility windows in sport, where youth leagues are split by birth date, and for benefits and pensions keyed to a spouse's age.</p>
<p>For the family historians: the total-days figure is the one to record. Years and months are how people talk, but days are how records agree — two sources that disagree about a birth date by a few days will show it instantly in the day count, while a years-only comparison hides it completely. As with every calculator here, the birth dates are crunched in your browser and never sent anywhere.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Reading the gap in months — and one more worked example</h2>
<p>Years and months are how people talk about a gap, but months alone are often the number a form or a rule actually wants: "at least 18 months apart", "born within two years of each other". The months figure is simple once you have the years: multiply the full years by 12 and add the leftover months. A gap of 4 years and 3 months is 51 months; the worked example below, 3 years and 7 months, is 43 months.</p>
<table>
<tr><td><strong>Person 1</strong></td><td><strong>Person 2</strong></td><td><strong>Exact age gap</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>5 January 2000</td><td>23 August 2003</td><td>3 years, 7 months, 18 days (1,326 days apart)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Notice what the year subtraction alone would have said: 2003 − 2000 = 3 years, which happens to match the full years here only because the younger person's birthday (August) had already passed in the comparison year. Had the second birthday fallen in November instead, the true gap would have been 3 years and 10 months — ten months more than the year subtraction suggests. Full dates, borrowed properly, are the only honest count.</p></section>
''',
"age-on-date": '''<section class="section"><h2>The eligibility question: "how old will they be on…?"</h2>
<p>Most real age questions are not about today. They are about a fixed date in the future with a rule attached to it: will my daughter be five by the school entry cut-off? Will I be 18 by election day, 21 by the flight, 60 by the retirement date, 16 by the exam? The answer decides applications, bookings and plans, and it cannot be worked out from today's age alone — you need the age on that specific date, with the birthday's position in the year taken into account. That is the whole job of this calculator: pick any date, past or future, and read the exact age on it.</p>
<p>The past direction earns its keep too. "How old was I when we moved house?" "How old was Grandad in this 1962 photograph?" Family history, timelines for memoirs and legal statements, and simple curiosity all run on ages at past dates, and the borrowing method — full years, then months, then days across real month lengths — is identical in both directions.</p>
<p>One practical warning: cut-off rules are usually written to the day and enforced to the day, and a child or applicant who misses the date by 24 hours is treated exactly like one who misses it by a month. If the answer here lands within a few days of a cut-off that matters, double-check the rule's exact wording — "by 1 September", "on or before 31 August" and "in the September of the year they turn five" are three different rules wearing similar clothes.</p></section>
''',
"compress-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>Why PDFs get big — and what shrinking one really does</h2>
<p>A PDF grows heavy for one main reason: pictures. A scanned page is a photograph wearing a document's clothes, and a ten-page scan from a phone can sail past 10 MB while looking like "just a document". Text, by contrast, weighs almost nothing — a full novel as pure text is smaller than one phone photo. So when a form demands a PDF under 200KB or 100KB, the squeeze is applied to the images inside: each picture is re-encoded at a smaller size until the whole file fits the limit — the pages themselves are never re-drawn.</p>
<p>That is worth knowing because not every compressor works this way. Older tools re-draw each page as a picture and rebuild the PDF from those page images — the file shrinks, but its text is no longer selectable or searchable. This tool negotiates only with the images and leaves the text, links and page sizes untouched, so the compressed file stays a real document: for the office receiving your application nothing changes, and for your archive copy nothing is lost either.</p>
<p>The limits themselves are real-world, not arbitrary: 200KB is the standard ceiling on exam, job and government upload forms, and 100KB appears on the stricter ones. This tool works down through gentler settings first, so a file that only needs a small squeeze keeps as much sharpness as possible — it does not flatten everything to the harshest setting on the first pass. And because the work happens in your browser, the document — often the most private file on your phone — is never uploaded to do it.</p></section>
''',
"merge-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>Merging is really about the order of pages</h2>
<p>Nobody merges PDFs for fun. They merge them because an application portal wants one file: the passport scan, the bank statement and the signed form, in that order, under one upload button. Or because a report lives in four exports that a manager wants as a single document. In every case the real work of merging is not joining the files — any tool can stack pages — it is getting the sequence right, because the reader will judge the document by its flow: cover page first, evidence after, signature where it belongs.</p>
<p>That is why this tool lets you arrange the files before you commit, and why it is worth a slow minute checking the order in the list before pressing Merge. The merged file takes the pages of each PDF in order, so if one source document itself is out of order, merging will faithfully preserve the mistake. A quick scroll through each source first is the professional habit that separates a clean submission from a returned one.</p>
<p>Two more things matter in practice. First, merging does not compress: ten files of 1 MB become one file of roughly 10 MB, so if the portal also has a size limit, run the result through the <a href="/compress-pdf/">PDF Compressor</a> afterwards. Second, privacy: merged bundles are usually your most sensitive documents gathered in one place — identity papers, statements, certificates. This merger runs entirely in your browser, so the bundle is assembled on your device and never passes through a server on its way.</p></section>
''',
"pdf-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>When a page needs to become a picture</h2>
<p>PDF is the format of documents, but half the modern world runs on images: slide decks, social posts, messaging apps, form builders that accept a photo but not a document, and clients who will open a picture instantly but let a PDF sit unopened for a week. Converting each page of a PDF into a JPG turns the document into something that can be dropped into a presentation, posted, printed as a poster or attached where only images are allowed — with the layout frozen exactly as designed, because a picture cannot reflow, substitute fonts or break.</p>
<p>Sharpness is the decision that matters. A page rendered at its natural size can look soft when zoomed or printed, so this converter renders at 2× — each page comes out with four times the pixels, crisp on a slide or a phone screen. If the images are only for a quick preview or a chat message, that extra size is harmless; if you are converting a 60-page report, expect 60 separate downloads, one per page, in order — keep the ones you need and discard the rest.</p>
<p>Choose JPG for photographs, slides and anything destined for screens — the files stay small. If a page is mostly crisp text and line art and you need every edge perfect, or the page has transparency to preserve, the <a href="/pdf-to-png/">PDF to PNG</a> converter is the lossless sibling. Either way the conversion runs on your device: the document never leaves your browser, which matters when the PDF is a contract, a statement or anything else you would not email to a stranger.</p></section>
''',
"jpg-to-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>One photo is a picture; several photos are a document</h2>
<p>The phone camera has become the world's scanner, and its output is a pile of separate photos: three pages of a contract, both sides of an ID, a sequence of receipts. The offices on the receiving end do not want a pile — they want one PDF, in the right order, that opens as a single document. This converter takes your JPGs (and PNGs) and binds them into exactly that: one page per photo, sequenced the way you arrange them.</p>
<p>Order is the craft here. Add the photos in the sequence the reader needs — title page first, then the pages in reading order, ID front before ID back. If a photo arrives in the wrong place, remove it and re-add it in position rather than rebuilding the whole set; the preview list is the document's table of contents, and it is worth reading once before you convert, the way you would check a stapled stack before handing it over.</p>
<p>Two practical notes. The PDF keeps each photo at its own quality, so a sharp photo makes a sharp page — but the file also inherits the photos' weight, and camera photos are heavy. If the finished PDF must fit under an upload limit, compress the photos first with the <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a> or shrink the finished file with the <a href="/compress-pdf/">PDF Compressor</a>. And as everywhere on ToolPixi, the binding happens in your browser: photographs of your documents are assembled on your device, never uploaded.</p></section>
''',
"split-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>Splitting is a privacy tool as much as a convenience</h2>
<p>The obvious reason to split a PDF is size or relevance: pull pages 4–7 out of a 90-page report and send only the chapter that matters. The less obvious reason is privacy, and it is the stronger one. A bank statement PDF often covers three months when the landlord asked for one. A medical file bundles every visit when the insurer needs a single report. Sending the whole document because it arrived as one file hands over far more of your life than the request asked for — splitting lets you send exactly the pages requested and nothing else.</p>
<p>This tool works two ways: extract a page range into a single new PDF, or save every page as its own file. Ranges suit the "send pages 4–7" job; every-page mode suits breaking a scanned bundle — the classic 40-page scan of mixed receipts, letters and forms — back into individual documents you can file, name and send separately.</p>
<p>A housekeeping habit makes split files useful instead of chaotic: rename them as they land. "statement.pdf (pages 3–4)" means nothing in a downloads folder a month later; "bank-statement-march.pdf" files itself. And if the extracted pages must also fit an upload limit, the <a href="/compress-pdf/">PDF Compressor</a> finishes the job. The split itself happens entirely in your browser — the full document, the parts you send and the parts you keep all stay on your device.</p></section>
''',
"image-compressor": '''<section class="section"><h2>File size is a gate, and forms hold the key</h2>
<p>Nobody compresses a photo because smaller is beautiful. They compress it because a form refused the original: "file must be under 200KB" on a visa application, "photo must be 50KB or less" on an exam entry, "image too large" on a job portal that never says what it actually wants. The camera on a modern phone produces 3–8 MB photographs as a matter of routine, so nearly every official upload in life now begins with this exact problem — a perfectly good photo that is simply too heavy for the gate it has to pass through.</p>
<p>There are two ways a photo gets lighter, and this tool uses the honest one first: re-encoding at a quality level matched to your target. Quality is a dial, not a cliff — a photo saved at 80% quality is usually indistinguishable from the original on a screen at half the bytes. Only when a very tight target (20KB, say) demands it does the tool also reduce dimensions. The preview matters more than the number: always look at the compressed result before downloading, because a face on an ID photo has to stay recognisably a face, and the right target is the smallest size that still reads clearly.</p>
<p>Keep the untouched original somewhere safe. The compressed copy is a sending copy, made for one form; the original is the photograph. And because the compression happens in your browser, the photo of your passport page or your child's school form is never uploaded to make it smaller — the gate-check happens entirely on your device.</p></section>
''',
"compress-image-to-50kb": '''<section class="section"><h2>The 50KB habit: compress the sending copy, keep the original</h2>
<p>A photo shrunk to 50KB is a sending copy, made for one form — not a replacement for the photograph. The original stays on your phone or computer at full quality, and the 50KB version is the one that passes the portal's gate. That split is worth keeping deliberate: if the same photo is needed again for a different form with a 100KB or 200KB cap, compress the original again rather than re-compressing the 50KB copy, because every re-save of an already-compressed JPEG throws away a little more detail. One original, as many sending copies as there are forms — each made fresh, each at exactly the size its form demands.</p></section>
''',
"compress-image-to-100kb": '''<section class="section"><h2>100KB is the comfortable middle</h2>
<p>100KB sits in a sweet spot that 50KB cannot reach: double the bytes, and roughly double the detail budget. A head-and-shoulders portrait at 100KB looks close to the original on screen; a scanned certificate keeps its small print legible; a group photo stops turning faces into smudges. That is why so many older government portals and job sites settled on 100KB as their cap — strict enough to keep their storage sane, generous enough that an ordinary photo survives the squeeze looking like itself. Where 50KB is a squeeze you feel, 100KB is a trim you barely notice.</p>
<p>The practical rule this page is built on: send at the size the form asks for, not smaller. There is no prize for fitting a 50KB cap when the form allows 100KB — the smaller file only throws away detail nobody asked you to lose. And the reverse holds too: if the form says 100KB, a 200KB file will be refused just as surely as a 5 MB one, so "close enough" is not a strategy. Match the number on the instruction page exactly, check the preview before downloading, and keep the original safe for the next form.</p></section>
''',
"compress-jpg-to-100kb": '''<section class="section"><h2>100KB in JPG: the size every upload box understands</h2>
<p>JPG is the one format every upload box, inbox and social site accepts without a second thought, and 100KB is the size those boxes keep asking for. The pair works because JPG was built for exactly this trade: photographs compress gracefully, losing detail the eye barely registers, while the file shrinks to a fraction of its original weight. A 100KB JPG is light enough to attach five at a time to an email, small enough for any profile-photo slot, and sharp enough that the person looking at it sees the photograph, not the compression.</p>
<p>The habit that makes this page useful is the same one the other size pages teach: the 100KB file is a sending copy. Keep the untouched original — the full-resolution JPG from the camera — and make a fresh 100KB copy for each place that asks for one. Re-compressing a 100KB copy for the next form throws away detail twice, while the original compresses cleanly every time. One original, as many sending copies as there are forms — each made fresh, each at exactly the size its destination demands.</p></section>
''',
"image-resizer": '''<section class="section"><h2>Pixels, proportions and the stretch that gives itself away</h2>
<p>Resizing sounds like one job and is actually three. There is resizing to exact pixels, when a platform demands 1200×630 for a link preview or 1080×1080 for a post. There is resizing by percentage, when a photo is simply too large and half the size will do. And there is the quiet third job underneath both: keeping the proportions true. Change the width without the height and faces stretch, logos warp and products look melted — the unmistakable signature of an image resized by someone fighting the tool instead of using it. This resizer locks the aspect ratio by default precisely so that mistake is hard to make.</p>
<p>The other misunderstanding is size in pixels versus size in kilobytes — they are cousins, not twins. Halving a photo's dimensions usually cuts its file size by far more than half, because the pixel count falls to a quarter. So when the real problem is an upload limit like "under 200KB", resizing the dimensions down is often the fastest route, with the <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a> finishing the job to an exact target. When the problem is a platform's required dimensions — a banner, a thumbnail, a profile slot — pixels are the language to speak, and this page speaks it directly.</p>
<p>One rule protects every resize: never enlarge a small image and expect sharpness. A 200-pixel logo stretched to 2000 pixels is the same information spread thinner, and it will look soft no matter what tool does it. Resize down freely; for going up, you need a bigger original.</p></section>
''',
"heic-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>The photo format your iPhone chose for you</h2>
<p>Since 2017, iPhones have saved photos as HEIC by default — a modern format that stores the same picture in roughly half the space of a JPG. On Apple devices this is invisible and wonderful. The trouble starts at the border: a Windows PC that shows blank thumbnails, a web form that rejects the file outright, a photo printer, an older editor, a colleague on Android saying "the photo won't open". HEIC is not a bad format; it is a young one, and the world's forms, offices and older software still speak JPG.</p>
<p>Converting is a translation, not a transformation: the pixels come across whole, and the JPG you get is the photograph you took, in the format everything accepts. What does not always travel is the exotic extras — some depth and live-photo data stays behind — which is why the converted JPG is best treated as the shareable copy while the HEIC original stays in your library as the master. For most photos the visible quality difference is nil; for a form or an email attachment, compatibility beats theory every time.</p>
<p>The conversion here happens in your browser, which matters more for this tool than most. iPhone photos are the most personal files people own — children, homes, documents photographed for safekeeping. Server-based converters ask you to upload them to a stranger's machine and trust the deletion promise. This one never receives them at all: the file is read by your browser, converted on your device, and handed back — nothing to delete because nothing was sent.</p></section>
''',
"percentage-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Three questions hide inside the word "percentage"</h2>
<p>Almost every percentage problem in daily life is one of three questions wearing different clothes. "What is 15% of 240?" — a share of a whole, the tip, the commission, the discount amount. "What percentage is 45 of 180?" — a part measured against its whole, the test score, the completion rate. And "a price rose from 80 to 100 — by what percentage?" — a change measured against where it started, the raise, the inflation figure, the growth rate. Most percentage confusion is simply using one question's method on another question's problem; this calculator separates the three so each gets its own honest method.</p>
<p>The classic trap lives in the third question: percentages are anchored to the starting value, so a rise of 25% followed by a fall of 25% does not bring you home — 100 becomes 125, then falls by 25% of 125 to 93.75. Shopkeepers, headlines and salary letters all benefit when that asymmetry goes unnoticed. The related trap is percentage points: an interest rate moving from 4% to 5% rose by one percentage point, which is a 25% rise in the rate itself. Both statements are true; they answer different questions.</p>
<p>For the change version specifically, the <a href="/percentage-increase-calculator/">Percentage Increase Calculator</a> shows the working in full, and the <a href="/discount-calculator/">Discount Calculator</a> applies the first question to sale prices with tax handled. All of them run in your browser — the numbers you type, from salaries to school fees, are nobody else's business.</p></section>
''',
"percentage-increase-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>The raise, the rent rise and the reversed percentage</h2>
<p>"Prices went up 8%." "Your salary increases by 5%." "The rent is rising from 900 to 990 — is that the 10% the landlord promised?" Percentage increase is the language of every unwelcome letter and half the welcome ones, and verifying it takes one formula: the change divided by the original, times 100. The original is the anchor — every error in this territory comes from dividing by the new number instead, which flatters whoever is presenting the rise.</p>
<p>The skill that separates the fluent from the fooled is running the machine backwards. If a jacket costs 84 after a 30% discount, what was the original price? Not 84 plus 30% of 84 — that overshoots, because the discount was taken from the original, not from 84. The true reversal divides by what remains: 84 ÷ 0.70 = 120. The same reversal recovers a pre-tax price from a tax-included one, and a pre-raise salary from a payslip. This calculator shows the forward answer plainly; once you can see the anchor value in the result, the backward jobs become the same shape.</p>
<p>And a sanity habit worth keeping: equal percentage moves do not cancel. Up 50% then down 50% leaves you at three-quarters of where you started, because the second percentage acted on a bigger base. Whenever a chart, a fund report or a landlord's letter leans on percentages, ask "percent of what, starting when?" — the two questions this page is built to answer.</p></section>
''',
"loan-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>What a monthly payment is really made of</h2>
<p>Every loan payment is two payments fused: a slice of the amount you borrowed, and rent on the money still outstanding — the interest. Early in a loan the balance is large, so interest takes the bigger share of each instalment and the debt itself shrinks slowly; as the balance falls, the same fixed payment bites deeper into the principal. That is why the first year of a mortgage feels like standing still, and why the totals in this calculator — total paid, total interest — are the numbers that tell the truth about a loan. The monthly figure tells you whether you can carry it; the totals tell you what it costs.</p>
<p>The term is the lever with the longest shadow. Stretch a loan from 3 years to 5 and the monthly payment drops kindly — while the total interest climbs steeply, because the balance spends two extra years being rented. Halving the term rarely doubles the payment, yet it can cut the interest by half or more. Run any real loan offer through both terms here before signing: the comparison takes ten seconds and is worth more than any negotiation over the rate's second decimal.</p>
<p>This calculator assumes a fixed rate and equal monthly payments (the EMI pattern). If your offer has a variable rate, fees, or a balloon payment at the end, treat the result as the honest baseline and ask the lender what their additions do to it. The figures you type stay in your browser — loan shopping is another calculation nobody needs to watch you do.</p></section>
''',
"compound-interest-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Interest on interest: the slowest magic in money</h2>
<p>Simple interest pays you for the money you put in. Compound interest pays you for the money you put in plus everything it has already earned — growth feeding on its own past growth. The difference is sleepy for the first few years and theatrical later, because the curve bends upward: each year's gain is calculated on a bigger base than the last. A thousand growing at 8% becomes about 1,080 after one year and roughly 4,660 after twenty — not 2,600, which is where simple interest would have left it. The extra came from earnings earning.</p>
<p>The famous shortcut for feeling this is the rule of 72: divide 72 by the annual rate and you get roughly the years needed to double your money. At 6%, money doubles about every 12 years; at 9%, about every 8. It is approximate and it is enough to compare two offers or two habits at a glance. What the rule hides is frequency: interest compounded monthly grows a touch faster than the same rate compounded yearly, because each month's small gain starts earning sooner. This calculator lets you switch the compounding rhythm and watch the gap open.</p>
<p>The variable that beats all others is time, and it is not close. Starting at 25 instead of 35 gives the curve an extra doubling period — often worth more than doubling the amount saved. These examples are illustrations of the maths, not financial advice or promises about any real investment; markets do not move in straight lines. But the arithmetic of compounding itself never lies, and it runs here in your browser.</p></section>
''',
"discount-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Two discounts are never the discount you think</h2>
<p>"20% off, plus an extra 10% off at checkout!" is not 30% off, and the gap between those numbers is exactly where sale psychology lives. The first discount cuts the price to 80% of itself; the second cuts that reduced price to 90% of what remains — 72% of the original. Your true saving is 28%, not 30%. On a small basket the difference is coins; on a laptop, a sofa or a contractor's "discounted" quote, it is real money quietly staying in the shop's till. This calculator untangles stacked offers by showing the final price and the true saving separately.</p>
<p>Tax is the second act of the same trick. In most places tax is calculated on the discounted price — you are not taxed on money you did not spend — but receipts and quotes are not always written that way, and a "total with tax" typed by hand deserves one verification pass. Enter the price, the discount, and your tax rate here, and the order of operations is handled correctly and shown plainly.</p>
<p>The healthy habit this page encourages is comparing the number that leaves your account, not the percentage that caught your eye. A 40% discount on an inflated "was" price can lose to an honest 25% elsewhere. Percentages are advertising; the final price is shopping. Both figures — what you pay and what you truly saved — are the two lines this calculator puts at the top.</p></section>
''',
"bmi-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>A useful number that was never meant to be a verdict</h2>
<p>Body mass index is a ratio — weight divided by height squared — invented in the 1830s for studying populations, not for judging individuals. It survives because it is genuinely useful at its own job: a fast, free screening number that flags where a closer look might be worthwhile. The standard bands (under 18.5 underweight, 18.5–24.9 the healthy range, 25–29.9 overweight, 30 and above obese) describe statistical risk across millions of people. Applied to one person they are a starting point for a conversation, not a diagnosis.</p>
<p>The known blind spots are worth naming plainly, because this page would rather be honest than flattering. BMI cannot see composition: a rugby player or a dedicated lifter can land in "overweight" on muscle alone, while someone with little muscle and more body fat can sit in the "healthy" band. It does not account for where fat is carried, for age, or for pregnancy — and it is not the right tool for children, whose assessment uses age-and-sex percentiles instead. Several health bodies suggest lower action thresholds for people of South Asian heritage, whose risk rises at lower BMI values; if that applies to you, a clinician can interpret the number in context.</p>
<p>What this page gives you beyond the index itself is the healthy weight range for your exact height — for most people a more usable figure than the index, because it turns an abstract score into a concrete band to discuss with a doctor. Nothing you type is sent anywhere: height and weight are exactly the kind of numbers a person should be able to calculate in private, and here they never leave the browser.</p></section>
''',
"png-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>Why PNG files so often need to become JPGs</h2>
<p>PNG is the format of screenshots, logos and anything with sharp edges or transparency — and it pays for that fidelity with size. A single phone screenshot or exported graphic can weigh several megabytes, because PNG compression is lossless: it refuses to throw away a single pixel. That is exactly right for editing and archiving, and exactly wrong for the places images usually end up. Upload forms cap file sizes, email clients balk at heavy attachments, marketplaces and job portals reject oversized images, and a page full of PNG photographs loads slowly for every visitor.</p>
<p>Converting to JPG trades a small, usually invisible amount of detail for a file that is often a fifth of the size. JPG was designed for photographs — continuous tones, gradients, real-world scenes — and it discards precisely the information the human eye is worst at noticing. The honest catch: JPG has no transparency. A PNG with a transparent background comes out with that background filled in white, which is why logos and stickers should stay PNG, while photos, screenshots for forms and anything headed for a web page almost always travel better as JPG.</p>
<p>The conversion here happens entirely in your browser, on your device. The image is decoded, re-encoded at a quality level chosen to keep it looking like itself, and handed back as a download — nothing is uploaded to a server, so a private document or an unreleased product shot is safe to convert. If you are converting a whole folder of images, each one can be dropped in and saved individually with its original name, ready to upload wherever it was needed.</p></section>
''',
"jpg-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>When a JPG needs to go back to PNG</h2>
<p>JPG is a one-way street: every save discards a little more detail, and editing a JPG over and over slowly turns it to mush — text goes fuzzy, edges grow halos, flat colours develop speckles. Converting a JPG to PNG does not restore anything the JPG already lost, and this page will not pretend otherwise. What it does is stop the bleeding. PNG is lossless, so once the image is a PNG, you can annotate it, crop it, composite it and save it a hundred times without it degrading any further.</p>
<p>That is why the job comes up so often in practice. A photo needs text or arrows added for a report; a screenshot exported as JPG needs to be marked up; a design tool, game engine or document workflow simply demands PNG and refuses anything else. PNG also restores the possibility of transparency going forward: the JPG itself has none, but as a PNG the image can have areas removed and saved see-through, ready to drop over other backgrounds.</p>
<p>Expect the file to grow — often several times over — because PNG stores every pixel faithfully instead of approximating them. That trade is the right one when the image still has work ahead of it, and the wrong one for finished photos headed to an upload form, where JPG stays king. The conversion runs locally in your browser: the file is decoded and re-encoded on your device and never sent to a server.</p></section>
''',
"webp-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>The WebP you downloaded is not broken — just early</h2>
<p>If you have ever saved an image from a website and found a file your computer pretends not to know, you have met WebP. Google created the format to make web pages lighter, and it works: a WebP is typically a quarter to a third smaller than the same picture as a JPG, which is why so many sites now serve nothing else. The problem is the rest of the world. Older photo editors, office software, upload forms, printers and plenty of phones still cannot open WebP, and a picture you cannot open is a picture you do not have.</p>
<p>Converting to JPG moves the image into the most universally supported format in existence — every device, editor, form and messaging app of the last thirty years reads it. The conversion costs a whisper of quality, because JPG is lossy and WebP usually is too, so the picture passes through one more round of compression. At the quality this tool uses, the difference is invisible in normal viewing; what you gain is a file that simply works everywhere it is sent.</p>
<p>There is one honest limitation to know: if the WebP is animated, the JPG can only hold a still — this converter saves the first frame, which is the frame most people want as the cover image anyway. Everything runs in your browser, with the file decoded and re-encoded on your own device, so even images you would never upload to a stranger's server are safe to convert here.</p></section>
''',
"webp-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>Keeping every pixel when WebP has to become PNG</h2>
<p>WebP is a delivery format — brilliant for web pages, awkward for everything after. When a WebP image needs to be edited, printed, dropped into design software or archived somewhere it will still open in twenty years, PNG is the safe destination. PNG is lossless and supported by literally everything, and it preserves the two things WebP images often carry that JPG cannot: transparency, and pixel-perfect detail in graphics, screenshots and text.</p>
<p>That transparency point matters more than it first appears. A product cut-out, a logo or a UI graphic saved as WebP keeps its see-through background, and converting it to JPG would slam a white rectangle behind it. PNG keeps the alpha channel intact, so the converted file drops over new backgrounds exactly as the original did. The same goes for sharp-edged graphics: WebP at web quality settings slightly softens crisp lines and small text, while PNG stores them exactly, which is why screenshots headed for documentation make this trip.</p>
<p>The converted file will be larger — often much larger — because lossless storage is the whole point. Think of PNG as the working and archive copy, and WebP as the shipping copy. The conversion is done by your own browser on your own device: the image is decoded and re-encoded locally and is never uploaded, so unreleased artwork and private screenshots stay private.</p>
<p>Two conversion behaviours are worth knowing before you rely on the result. An animated WebP — a sticker or a short loop — becomes a still PNG of its first frame, since PNG animation is not supported here; and descriptive metadata carried inside the WebP, such as camera details or colour notes, is not carried across, so if the metadata itself matters, archive the original alongside the PNG. What does cross over, exactly, is the picture and its transparency — which for editing and design work is the part that counts.</p></section>
''',
"jpg-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>Why websites ask for WebP and what it saves you</h2>
<p>Every kilobyte of image weight on a web page is paid for twice: once by the visitor waiting for it to load, and again by the site owner in bandwidth and search ranking, because Google measures page speed and images are usually the heaviest thing on the page. WebP exists to cut that bill. At the same visual quality, a WebP typically weighs 25–35% less than a JPG — detailed photographs save a little less, simple graphics save more — and across a gallery or a product catalogue that difference is the gap between a sluggish page and a snappy one.</p>
<p>The format is no longer exotic. Every current browser — Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari, and the mobile browsers built on them — displays WebP natively, which is why WordPress, Shopify and most image CDNs now generate it automatically. Converting your JPGs yourself is for the places that do not: a hand-built site, an email tool that accepts the file, a marketplace that takes uploads as they come. One honest caveat: a handful of older desktop programs and some social platforms still refuse WebP uploads, so keep the JPG originals as your masters.</p>
<p>Batch work is the norm here — rarely does anyone convert a single photo — so you can drop several JPGs in together and each downloads as its own WebP with a sensible quality setting already chosen. Everything is encoded in your browser on your device; the photos are never uploaded, which matters when the images are client work or family pictures you would rather keep to yourself.</p>
<p>One housekeeping habit protects the gain: keep the same filenames when you convert (photo.jpg becomes photo.webp), and a site migration or CMS swap stays a find-and-replace instead of an afternoon of detective work. The originals archive under their own names, the WebPs ship under matching ones, and the mapping between master and copy never has to be reconstructed from memory.</p></section>
''',
"png-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>PNG quality at a fraction of the weight</h2>
<p>PNG's great strength is also its great cost: it stores every pixel perfectly, so a screenshot, logo or interface graphic can weigh megabytes. On a web page that weight is pure delay. WebP was built for exactly this problem — it compresses graphics far harder than PNG while keeping the two things PNG users actually care about: sharp edges and transparency. A transparent logo or product cut-out converted to WebP keeps its see-through background and typically shrinks to a third of the PNG's size, sometimes less.</p>
<p>The savings are biggest precisely where PNG is used most. Flat colours, crisp text and simple shapes — the stuff of screenshots, diagrams, icons and UI captures — compress beautifully in WebP because there is so little unpredictable detail to store. Photographs saved as PNG (it happens more often than people realise, usually from editors and screen-capture tools) shrink even more dramatically. The practical result: pages that keep their crisp look and lose most of their load time, which is why WebP has become the default delivery format across the modern web.</p>
<p>Keep your PNGs as the masters, though. WebP at delivery quality is a shipping format; if the image will be edited again later, the lossless PNG is the one to archive. This converter runs entirely in your browser — the file is re-encoded on your device and never uploaded — and it handles images one by one or in batches, each keeping its transparency and its name.</p>
<p>A refinement for the fidelity-minded: WebP also has a lossless mode, which stores graphics pixel-perfectly while still usually beating PNG's file size — the right choice when a screenshot or diagram must stay exact and small at once. The quality setting on this page targets the practical middle ground for web delivery; where an image is destined for further editing rather than display, keep the lossless master in PNG and treat every WebP as a delivery copy.</p></section>
''',
"heic-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>HEIC is efficient; PNG is forever</h2>
<p>Since 2017, iPhones have saved photos as HEIC by default, because the format stores a photo in roughly half the space of a JPG. The catch arrives the moment a photo leaves the Apple ecosystem: Windows PCs without the right codec, older Android phones, web upload forms and plenty of professional software still cannot open HEIC at all. JPG is the usual escape route — but when a photo matters enough to edit, print or archive, PNG is the safer destination, because PNG conversion is a one-time, lossless step: every pixel Apple captured is preserved, and the file will never degrade through later edits and saves.</p>
<p>That distinction decides which converter to use. Sending a snapshot to a form or a friend? JPG, smaller and universal. Preparing a photo for retouching, pulling stills for print, feeding images into design or machine-learning pipelines that demand PNG, or archiving originals you cannot reshoot? PNG, every time. PNG also future-proofs in a way HEIC does not: it is an open, thirty-year-old standard that every operating system and editor reads natively, with none of the patent and codec baggage that still trails HEIC on non-Apple platforms.</p>
<p>Expect big files — a lossless copy of a modern phone photo can run to ten megabytes or more — because fidelity, not size, is the point of this conversion. It all happens in your browser: your photos are decoded and re-encoded on your own device and are never uploaded to a server, which is exactly how personal photos should be handled. Convert a single frame or work through a batch; each keeps its full resolution.</p></section>
''',
"heic-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>From iPhone-only to web-ready in one step</h2>
<p>HEIC is a storage format: it exists to squeeze more photos onto your iPhone. WebP is a delivery format: it exists to make web pages load fast. When iPhone photos are headed for a website, a blog, an online store or anywhere an audience waits on them, they need to make this trip — because browsers do not display HEIC at all, and uploading the JPG equivalent means carrying files roughly twice the weight for the same picture. WebP typically lands 25–35% smaller than JPG at matching quality, and it is supported by every current browser.</p>
<p>The conversion quietly solves two problems at once. The compatibility problem disappears — the file opens in every browser and CMS — and the performance problem shrinks with it, which matters more than most people expect: image weight is the single biggest contributor to slow pages, and slow pages lose visitors and search ranking. For a product catalogue or a photo essay shot on an iPhone, converting the whole set to WebP is the difference between a page that loads in a blink and one that assembles itself piece by piece.</p>
<p>One decision to make consciously: WebP is a destination, not a master. Keep the original HEIC (or a JPG/PNG copy) in your archive, because WebP's savings come from lossy compression you cannot undo. The conversion itself runs entirely in your browser — your photos are processed on your device and never uploaded — and batches are welcome: drop in the whole shoot and take back web-ready files, each at full resolution with a sensible quality setting.</p></section>
''',
"svg-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>SVG is a drawing, PNG is a photograph of it</h2>
<p>An SVG is not really a picture — it is a set of instructions: draw this line, fill that shape, place this text. That is why an SVG logo can be stretched to billboard size without going blurry, and why the file is often just a few kilobytes. But instructions need an interpreter, and plenty of places cannot follow them: office documents, many email clients, social platforms, older software and most upload forms all want pixels, not recipes. Converting to PNG renders the drawing once, at the exact pixel size you choose, into the most widely supported still-image format there is.</p>
<p>The size you pick is the one real decision, because it cannot be undone afterwards. Render too small and the PNG will look soft when displayed large — the infinite scalability of the original is spent the moment you rasterise. A reliable rule: choose at least the largest size the image will ever be shown at, and double it if it might appear on a retina display or in print. A logo used at 200 pixels wide on a website wants a 400-pixel PNG; a graphic headed for a slide deck wants 1,000 or more.</p>
<p>PNG keeps the SVG's transparency, so a logo designed to sit over any background still does. One behaviour worth knowing: any fonts inside the SVG are rendered as shapes at conversion time, so the PNG looks identical everywhere — no missing-font surprises — but the text in it is no longer editable or selectable. The rendering happens in your browser on your device, so unpublished artwork never leaves your machine.</p></section>
''',
"svg-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>When the vector needs to become a plain photo-style file</h2>
<p>Most SVG artwork — logos, icons, illustrations — eventually meets a system that only accepts JPG: a marketplace listing image, a presentation tool, an older content manager, a form that whitelists JPG and PNG and nothing else. JPG is the safer of the two fallbacks when the destination is a photograph-style slot, because its files are small and every piece of software on earth opens them. The conversion renders the vector drawing at the pixel size you choose, then compresses it the way JPG always does.</p>
<p>Two behaviours define this variant and separate it from SVG to PNG. First, transparency dies here: JPG has no alpha channel, so any see-through part of the artwork is filled with white. For an illustration on a white page that is invisible; for a logo meant to float over a coloured background it is a deal-breaker, and PNG is the right tool instead. Second, JPG's compression is tuned for photos, so razor-sharp edges and flat colour blocks can pick up slight fuzz and speckling at small sizes — the very things SVG artwork is made of. At generous pixel sizes and sensible quality the effect is minimal, but it is why PNG remains the default for graphics and JPG the choice for photo-shaped slots.</p>
<p>Pick your render size generously — at least the largest display size, ideally double — because the vector's infinite scaling is spent in this one render. Everything is processed in your browser; the artwork is never uploaded to a server.</p></section>
''',
"svg-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>The web-native way to ship vector artwork</h2>
<p>Designers love SVG because a logo can weigh three kilobytes and scale forever. But SVG has two quiet costs on a real website: browsers must parse and draw every shape on every page load, and some content systems, ad platforms and social tools simply reject SVG uploads — occasionally for security reasons, since SVG can carry scripts. WebP is the compromise the modern web settled on: render the vector once, at the size actually needed, into a compressed pixel file that keeps transparency and loads like any other image.</p>
<p>Against PNG — the other transparency-keeping option — WebP usually wins on weight by a wide margin for illustrations, often coming in 30–60% smaller, because its compression was designed for exactly this kind of content. Against JPG it wins on fidelity: no white fill behind transparent areas, and no speckling around the crisp edges that vector art is made of. The trade-offs are commitment and compatibility: the render size is fixed forever (keep the SVG as your master), and a few older desktop programs still cannot open WebP, though every current browser can.</p>
<p>Render at double the largest size the graphic will be displayed at, so retina screens stay sharp. The conversion runs entirely in your browser — your artwork is rendered and encoded on your own device and is never uploaded — and the result drops straight into web pages, CMS uploads and ad managers that want a file, not a drawing recipe.</p></section>
''',
"gif-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>One frame, kept as a photo</h2>
<p>A GIF is a tiny film: a stack of frames, each limited to 256 colours, usually looping. Sometimes all you need from it is a single still — the cover image for a post, a thumbnail for a document, a frame to attach where animations are not allowed or not wanted. JPG is the natural container for that still: it is the most compatible image format in existence, and it stores the frame with far more colour subtlety than the GIF's 256-colour palette ever allowed the original to show.</p>
<p>Be clear about what this conversion does and does not do. It takes the GIF's first frame — the frame most platforms themselves use as the GIF's poster image — and saves it as a photograph. It does not animate, and it cannot recover colours or detail the GIF format discarded when the animation was made; banding in gradients and dithering speckles are baked into the source. What it can do is give you a small, universally accepted still at proper JPG quality, ready for upload forms, documents and messaging apps that strip or refuse GIFs.</p>
<p>If the frame you need is not the first one, this is the wrong tool — that job needs a GIF editor that can step through frames. If the first frame is the shot you want, the job takes seconds and happens entirely in your browser: the GIF is decoded on your device, the frame re-encoded, and nothing is uploaded anywhere.</p></section>
''',
"gif-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>A still frame, kept losslessly — transparency included</h2>
<p>Converting a GIF to PNG is about keeping things, not shrinking them. The PNG holds the GIF's first frame as a still image, stored losslessly, with two properties JPG cannot offer: pixel-exact fidelity and transparency. Many GIFs — stickers, reaction images, logos, overlays — carry transparent regions, and those survive this conversion intact, so the still can be composited over new backgrounds, dropped into documents and slides, or fed into design software exactly as intended.</p>
<p>The honest expectations: the PNG will usually be bigger than the GIF's single frame would suggest, because lossless storage is the point, and it cannot add back what GIF threw away. A GIF frame lives inside a 256-colour palette, so gradients may show banding and photos may look dithered — the PNG preserves that faithfully rather than fixing it. What you gain is a still that will never degrade further: edit it, annotate it, save it fifty times, and it stays identical, which is more than any JPG can promise.</p>
<p>Choose this over GIF to JPG when transparency matters or the image has more work ahead of it; choose JPG when the still is a finished photo headed for an upload form. The conversion runs in your browser on your device — the file is decoded and re-encoded locally and never uploaded — and the result is a clean, standard PNG that every editor and platform accepts.</p></section>
''',
"gif-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>The first frame, at web weight</h2>
<p>Websites that still serve GIFs pay a heavy tax in load time, which is why so many have moved their short loops and stickers to WebP. This converter takes a GIF and produces a WebP still of its first frame — the poster frame, the one viewers see before anything plays. That still is exactly what is needed for thumbnails, previews, link cards and placeholders: the recognisable image, at a fraction of the weight, in the format browsers render fastest.</p>
<p>The comparison with its siblings matters. Against GIF to JPG, this keeps the GIF's transparency — stickers and overlays stay see-through instead of gaining a white box — and usually produces a smaller file. Against GIF to PNG, it gives up lossless perfection in exchange for size: the WebP is a delivery copy, noticeably lighter, ideal when the still is finished and headed for a page. And against all of them, one limit is shared: animation does not survive. A multi-frame GIF becomes a single still here; if you need the loop itself preserved in WebP, that is a video-conversion job, not an image conversion.</p>
<p>Everything is processed in your browser, on your device — the GIF is decoded and the frame re-encoded locally, nothing uploaded. For site owners trimming page weight and anyone preparing preview images, it is the fastest honest route from an ageing GIF to a modern still.</p></section>
''',
"avif-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>AVIF is the future; JPG is the present tense</h2>
<p>AVIF is built on the AV1 video codec, and on pure compression it beats everything else in this toolbox — photos at half the size of a JPG with quality to spare, plus HDR colour and transparency. Phones, cameras and screenshot tools have started saving it, and browsers display it happily. The frustration arrives off the web: many photo editors, office suites, upload forms, printers, smart TVs and older devices still stare at an AVIF file and see nothing. A picture that cannot be opened where it is needed might as well not exist.</p>
<p>JPG is the universal translator. Converting AVIF to JPG costs a little — one generation of lossy compression applied to a file that was itself compressed — but at the quality used here the difference is not visible in ordinary viewing, and the result opens absolutely everywhere: every form, every editor, every messaging app, every decade-old laptop. This is the variant to choose when the destination is a form, a document, an email or another person. Choose AVIF to PNG instead when the image will be edited further or carries transparency you must keep, because JPG will fill transparent areas with white and degrade a little with every future save.</p>
<p>The conversion happens entirely in your browser. AVIF decoding is built into modern browsers, so your image is decoded and re-encoded on your own device and never uploaded — unpublished photos stay unpublished until you choose otherwise.</p></section>
''',
"avif-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>Out of the codec, into the vault</h2>
<p>AVIF compresses brilliantly, but it is a young format riding on a video codec, with support that is still patchy outside browsers and a long-term future that depends on the AV1 ecosystem. PNG is the opposite bet: a thirty-year-old open standard, lossless, transparent-capable, and readable by every operating system, editor and archive system in existence. Converting AVIF to PNG is what you do when the image matters more than the megabytes — when it is headed for editing, print preparation, compositing, or long-term storage where "will this open in 2045?" is a real question.</p>
<p>Two things survive this conversion perfectly. Every pixel: PNG is lossless, so the decoded AVIF frame is stored exactly, and from here the image can be edited and re-saved forever without the generational decay that lossy formats suffer. And transparency: AVIF supports an alpha channel, and unlike a conversion to JPG — which slams white behind see-through areas — the PNG keeps it, so cut-outs, product shots and overlays remain usable over any background. Metadata is the exception: camera details and colour notes embedded in the AVIF are not carried across, so if the EXIF record itself matters, keep the original file alongside.</p>
<p>Expect large results — lossless copies of modern photos run to many megabytes — because fidelity is the entire point. The work is done in your browser, decoded and re-encoded on your device; nothing is uploaded, so private images stay private.</p></section>
''',
"avif-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>Modern to modern: trading peak compression for reach</h2>
<p>AVIF and WebP are the two formats fighting to succeed JPG, and converting between them seems odd until you hit the real-world problem: support. AVIF squeezes harder — often 20–30% smaller than WebP at the same quality — but its support map has holes: older Safari versions, some embedded browsers, various CMS and ad-platform uploaders, and tools that accept WebP happily while rejecting AVIF outright. WebP, a decade older, is the safer passport: every current browser displays it, and most of the web's plumbing accepts it without complaint.</p>
<p>So this conversion is a deliberate, small step down in compression efficiency in exchange for a big step up in acceptance — the right trade when a website, store or client pipeline says "WebP yes, AVIF no," and when images are headed for pages where shaving the last kilobyte matters less than loading everywhere, first try. Transparency survives the trip (both formats carry alpha channels), which keeps product cut-outs and overlays intact. What does not survive perfectly is quality at the margins: both formats are lossy at delivery settings, so the WebP is one more generation removed from the original — invisible in practice at the quality used here, but a reason to keep the AVIF or a lossless master in your archive.</p>
<p>The conversion runs in your browser, decoded and re-encoded on your device with nothing uploaded. If your destination is a person or a form rather than a web page, JPG remains the most universal answer; if it is an archive or an editing desk, PNG is. WebP is for the web itself.</p></section>
''',
"bmp-to-jpg": '''<section class="section"><h2>The heavyweight from the floppy-disk era</h2>
<p>BMP is one of the oldest image formats still in circulation, and its design shows its age: it stores essentially every pixel with almost no compression, so a single screenshot or scanned page can weigh tens of megabytes. BMPs still escape into the wild from older Windows software, legacy scanners, industrial and medical equipment, and long-lived internal tools that never got a reason to change. The files are valid — they are just absurdly heavy for anything involving email, uploads or storage at scale.</p>
<p>JPG was invented for precisely this problem. A photograph or screenshot stored as BMP compresses down to a small fraction of its size as a JPG — routinely 90–95% smaller — with no visible difference at the quality used here, because real-world images are full of the gradual tones JPG handles best. The one case to pause on: images with razor-sharp text or line art, like screenshots of documents, can pick up slight fuzz around edges in JPG. If the image is a photo or a general screenshot, convert without a second thought; if every pixel of small text must stay pristine, BMP to PNG is the lossless route instead.</p>
<p>Batch behaviour matters here — nobody has exactly one BMP — so you can convert files one after another and watch a folder of legacy bulk become a set of files that fit through an email limit. Everything runs in your browser on your device; your files are never uploaded to a server.</p></section>
''',
"bmp-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>Lossless to lossless: same pixels, sane size</h2>
<p>BMP and PNG make the same promise — every pixel stored exactly — but they keep it with wildly different efficiency. BMP barely compresses at all, a design from an era of local disks and small images; PNG compresses losslessly with modern algorithms, so the identical image typically lands at a third to a tenth of the BMP's weight. Converting between them changes nothing about the picture itself: every pixel value survives, bit for bit in effect, which makes this the safe route for screenshots of documents, technical captures, UI records and anything with fine text or linework that JPG would fuzz.</p>
<p>Transparency is the other quiet upgrade. BMP in the wild almost never carries a usable alpha channel; PNG supports one properly, so once an image is a PNG it can take part in modern workflows — compositing, design tools, web use — that BMP cannot reach. And while BMP is increasingly a format other software tolerates rather than welcomes, PNG is the universal still-image standard: every editor, browser, document tool and archive reads it without complaint. Converting a legacy BMP library to PNG is, in effect, moving it from a museum format onto the modern shelf without losing a thing.</p>
<p>Do not expect JPG-sized files — lossless fidelity costs megabytes, and a big BMP becomes a merely reasonable PNG, not a tiny one. The conversion is done by your browser on your device, with nothing uploaded, so even screenshots of internal systems stay exactly where they belong: with you.</p>
<p>BMPs have one modern hiding place worth knowing: remote-desktop tools, virtual machines and industrial interfaces still export screenshots as BMP by default, which is why the format keeps arriving in IT inboxes decades after its retirement everywhere else. Converting those captures to PNG preserves every pixel of text — the licence keys, error codes and settings that are the entire content of such screenshots — at a size an email system will actually accept.</p></section>
''',
"bmp-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>Thirty years of format progress in one click</h2>
<p>No conversion on this site spans a wider technological gap. BMP dates from the late 1980s: uncompressed pixels, enormous files, no transparency worth the name. WebP is its opposite in every respect — modern lossy and lossless compression, alpha channel support, and files built for a world of page-load budgets and mobile bandwidth. The BMPs still produced by legacy Windows tools, scanners and industrial software routinely weigh tens of megabytes; the same image as a WebP typically lands under one.</p>
<p>That makes this the right variant when the destination is the web — a site, a CMS, a store — and the source is some ageing system that only speaks BMP. WebP's lossy mode is tuned for photographs and screenshots and, at the quality used here, the savings versus the BMP original are so large (often 95%+) that the slight theoretical quality cost is the easiest trade in imaging. Where the BMP carries crisp line art or text that must stay pixel-perfect, WebP's lossless mode or a PNG will serve better; where the file just needs to become small, modern and uploadable, nothing else comes close.</p>
<p>Compatibility is the one thing to check before committing: WebP opens in every current browser but not in every old desktop program, so keep the BMP originals in the archive — storage of originals is cheap, and this conversion is about delivery copies. All processing happens in your browser on your device; the files are never uploaded to a server.</p></section>
''',
"png-to-ico": '''<section class="section"><h2>An ICO is not one icon — it is a drawer of them</h2>
<p>A Windows .ico file looks like a single icon but is really a container: inside one file sit several versions of the same artwork at different sizes — 16×16 for taskbars and lists, 24 and 32 for desktops, 48 for tiles, and 256 for high-DPI displays and large-icon views. Windows picks the closest size to what it needs and scales that one, which is why a proper ICO looks crisp everywhere while a single-size file looks mushy somewhere. This tool builds that genuine multi-size container from your PNG, generating each size from the original artwork rather than stretching one.</p>
<p>Start with the right PNG: square, at least 256×256, with the artwork large in the frame and simple enough to survive being shrunk to sixteen pixels — fine lines and small text simply vanish at that size, which is why famous icons are bold shapes, not detailed illustrations. Transparency in the PNG is preserved in the ICO, so a logo-shaped icon stays logo-shaped against any desktop background. The result is the file Windows expects for application shortcuts, installer icons, folder customisation and legacy software — and with a rename to favicon.ico, it also serves as a website's browser icon.</p>
<p>The whole build happens in your browser on your device. Drop in the PNG, take back a real .ico — no uploads, no watermarks, and no pretending that a renamed PNG is the same thing, because Windows can tell the difference even when people cannot.</p></section>
''',
"favicon-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>The smallest branding job on the internet</h2>
<p>The favicon is the 16-pixel square that represents your site in browser tabs, bookmarks, history lists and phone home screens — the tiniest piece of branding you own, and the one regular visitors see most often. Browsers expect it as favicon.ico, a special container holding several sizes at once (16, 32 and 48 pixels, plus larger ones for high-DPI screens), so the icon stays sharp from a crowded tab bar to a desktop shortcut. Uploading a lone PNG renamed to .ico sort of works in some browsers and silently fails in others; a genuine multi-size ICO works everywhere.</p>
<p>This generator takes any PNG or JPG — a logo, a monogram, a product mark — and builds the full container properly, rendering each size from your original so text and edges stay as clean as physics allows. Designing for sixteen pixels is its own discipline: bold shapes and one or two letters survive, detailed logos do not, which is why most strong favicons are a simplified mark rather than the full logo. If your source is a busy image, crop tight to the most recognisable element before generating.</p>
<p>Once downloaded, the file goes in your site's root and answers to the name favicon.ico — most browsers find it there automatically, and a one-line link tag in your HTML makes it certain. Generation happens entirely in your browser: your brand artwork is processed on your device and never uploaded to a server.</p></section>
''',
"image-cropper": '''<section class="section"><h2>Cropping is subtraction with a purpose</h2>
<p>Almost every image slot in modern life has a fixed shape: the square profile photo, the 16:9 video thumbnail, the 4:5 feed post, the passport rectangle, the banner strip. A photo that does not match the shape gets cropped by someone — either by you, deliberately, or by the platform, blindly, usually through somebody's forehead. This tool exists so the deliberate version happens: frame the part of the picture that matters, at exactly the aspect ratio or pixel size the destination demands, and export only that.</p>
<p>The preset ratios cover the common destinations, and the custom size exists for the specific ones — application forms and ID systems that want exact pixel dimensions, print shops that work in millimetres at a stated DPI. Two habits separate a good crop from a destructive one. First, crop from the largest original you have; every crop discards pixels, and a tight crop from a small file leaves too few to display well. Second, watch the edges: the rule-of-thirds grid is not decoration — subjects sit more naturally on its lines than dead centre, and a breath of space around a face reads as intentional rather than accidental.</p>
<p>Cropping here is non-destructive to your original file, which never changes and never leaves your device: all the work happens in your browser, and only the cropped copy is downloaded. If a form also imposes a file-size limit — many do — the cropped result can go straight through the Image Compressor afterwards to land under the exact KB cap.</p></section>
''',
"image-to-base64": '''<section class="section"><h2>Turning a picture into text — and when that is smart</h2>
<p>Base64 is a way of writing binary data using only safe, ordinary characters, and it lets an image travel in places only text can go: inside an HTML file as a data URI, in a CSS stylesheet, in a JSON payload, in an email template. For a developer, the payoff is a self-contained file — a single HTML page with its icons and images embedded, no separate files to host, link or lose. For small graphics like logos, icons and signatures, this is a genuinely tidy technique.</p>
<p>It comes with a tax you should know about: Base64 encoding inflates data by about a third. A 30KB icon becomes roughly 40KB of text, and a 500KB photograph becomes a ~670KB lump that makes HTML files unwieldy and JSON payloads sluggish. That arithmetic draws the honest line: embed small, frequently reused graphics; never embed photos or anything large. Browsers also cannot cache an embedded image separately, so the same logo pasted into ten pages is downloaded ten times — the opposite of what you want at scale.</p>
<p>This tool converts your image and shows both forms — the raw Base64 string and the full data URI with the correct MIME prefix (data:image/png;base64,…) — ready to paste, with the encoded size displayed so the trade-off is never a surprise. The encoding runs in your browser on your device: the image never leaves your machine, which matters when the picture is an unreleased asset or a private photo.</p></section>
''',
"base64-to-image": '''<section class="section"><h2>Getting the picture back out of the string</h2>
<p>Base64 image strings turn up everywhere in technical life: returned by APIs as JSON fields, embedded in emails and logs, pasted in forum posts and documentation, stored in databases by apps that treat images as text. The string itself is unreadable — thousands of characters of alphabet soup — but inside it is an ordinary picture, encoded character by character. This tool decodes it back into a viewable image and lets you download it as a proper PNG file.</p>
<p>Paste either form: the full data URI (starting data:image/png;base64, or jpeg, webp, gif) or the bare string on its own. The tool detects the image type from the data itself, renders a preview so you can confirm it decoded what you expected, and then saves a clean PNG — the sensible universal container, lossless and accepted everywhere. This round-trip is also how you rescue images from places with no download button: a profile picture trapped in an app's API response, a chart generated by a script, an attachment visible only as code.</p>
<p>One caution worth the sentence: only decode strings from sources you trust, and let the preview confirm the content before you use the file — a Base64 blob is opaque until decoded, which is precisely why this tool shows you the picture before anything is saved. All decoding happens in your browser on your device; the string you paste — which might come from a private system — is never sent to a server.</p></section>
''',
"image-watermark": '''<section class="section"><h2>A signature that travels with the photo</h2>
<p>A watermark does two jobs at once. It tells everyone who sees the image whose it is — a name, a brand, a handle burned visibly into the picture — and it changes the economics of theft: a clean photo is one right-click from being someone else's listing, portfolio piece or advertisement, while a watermarked one has to be cropped, cloned or explained. Photographers previewing client galleries, sellers whose product shots keep appearing on rival stores, and artists posting work in progress all watermark for the same reason: most image theft is opportunistic, and a visible mark defeats opportunity.</p>
<p>Placement and strength are a craft decision. A small, semi-transparent mark in a corner is polite and easily cropped away; a larger mark across the centre protects completely and ruins the viewing; the working compromise most professionals use is a medium-opacity mark across a lower third or over an area of low detail, visible when you look for it, unobtrusive when you do not. This tool gives you the controls that matter — text, position, size and opacity — with a live preview so the balance is judged on the actual photo, not guessed.</p>
<p>Be honest about the limit: a watermark deters and attributes, it does not make theft impossible — determined editors can remove one, which is why the original unwatermarked file stays your legal proof of ownership. Your photos are processed entirely in your browser and never uploaded, so the only watermarked copies in existence are the ones you choose to download and share.</p></section>
''',
"background-remover": '''<section class="section"><h2>Cut-outs without the upload queue</h2>
<p>Removing a background is the gateway edit of the image economy: the product shot that must sit on a marketplace's white or transparent background, the portrait that needs a new scene, the logo that has to float over a design. Traditionally it meant either Photoshop pen-tool patience or uploading your photo to a website that keeps who-knows-what. This tool runs an AI segmentation model directly in your browser — the photo is analysed on your own device, the subject is separated from the background, and the result comes back as a transparent PNG with nothing uploaded anywhere.</p>
<p>The model's judgement is genuinely good on the subjects that matter commercially: people, products, pets, cars, single clear objects with defined edges. Its honest struggles are the same ones every AI remover has — wispy hair against a busy background, transparent objects like glasses and veils, shadows that could be subject or setting, and scenes with several overlapping subjects. In those cases the cut-out may need a forgiving background choice: a subject placed on a similar-toned scene hides edge imperfections that a stark white background would announce.</p>
<p>For sellers, the practical win is consistency: shoot products on any surface you have, remove the background here, and every listing image matches — the clean, uniform catalogue look that makes a store read as professional. Because processing is local, client photos, unreleased products and family portraits are never exposed to a third-party server, and there is no per-image fee or credit counter quietly shaping how you work.</p></section>
''',
"color-picker-from-image": '''<section class="section"><h2>The exact colour, straight from the pixel</h2>
<p>Every designer, developer and decorator hits the same moment: a colour in a photo — a wall, a product, a brand mark in a client's screenshot — needs to become a colour on a screen, and "a sort of dusty teal" is not a specification. This tool answers with numbers. Upload the image, click the exact pixel, and read its colour as HEX for web work, RGB for screens and code, and HSL for when you need to reason about the colour — lighten it, darken it, find what harmonises.</p>
<p>The craft is in where you click. A photograph is not flat colour: light, shadow, texture and JPEG compression mean neighbouring pixels can differ noticeably, and anti-aliased edges blend two colours into a third that exists nowhere in the original design. For a brand colour, sample the middle of a flat area, not an edge; for a representative tone — the colour a product "is" — sample a mid-tone, avoiding both the highlight and the shadow. Zoom in when the target is small; precision at the pixel level is the entire point of the exercise.</p>
<p>One professional caution: a photo's colours are the colours the camera and lighting produced, not necessarily the object's true colour under neutral light — a white shirt at sunset samples as peach. Treat picked values from photographs as an excellent starting point, then adjust by eye. The image is processed in your browser and never uploaded, so client work and private photos stay exactly that.</p></section>
''',
"mp4-to-mp3": '''<section class="section"><h2>Sometimes the video is just a container for sound</h2>
<p>A surprising amount of the world's audio travels inside video files: the lecture recorded as MP4, the interview saved from a camera, the podcast published to a video platform, the voice memo a phone insisted on filming. Playing video to hear audio wastes storage, battery and screen — and many places audio needs to go (a car stereo, a podcast app, an editor's timeline, a transcription service) want an audio file, full stop. This tool extracts the soundtrack from an MP4 and hands it back as an MP3, the audio format everything accepts.</p>
<p>The extraction is faithful to the source: the MP3 can only ever sound as good as the audio inside the MP4, because that audio was usually compressed when the video was made. A spoken-word recording — a lecture, a meeting, an interview — comes through essentially perfect for its purpose, and at a fraction of the video's size, which is why a two-hour recorded class can shrink from gigabytes to a file that fits anywhere. Music recorded from a phone at a concert will faithfully reproduce a phone recording at a concert; the tool moves sound, it does not restore it.</p>
<p>Everything happens in your browser: the video is decoded on your device, the audio encoded and downloaded, and the file — which might be a private meeting or an unreleased recording — is never uploaded to a server. The obvious copyright note applies with real force here: extract audio from your own recordings and content you have the right to use.</p></section>
''',
"video-compressor": '''<section class="section"><h2>Video files are huge; limits are not negotiable</h2>
<p>Modern phones shoot glorious video and enormous files: a minute of 4K footage can weigh 400MB, while the places video needs to go enforce limits from another era — Discord's free tier balks at 25MB, email attachments cap around 20–25MB, and plenty of upload forms still draw the line at 100MB. Compression is the negotiation between the video you shot and the limit you face: trade resolution, frame rate and bitrate — intelligently, in that order of least regret — until the file fits.</p>
<p>The trade-offs have a logic worth knowing. Dropping 4K to 1080p quarters the pixels and is invisible on most screens the video will actually be watched on; dropping to 720p quarters them again and still looks fine on phones. Bitrate is the second lever and the one that controls texture: too low, and motion turns blocky and faces smear. The sane strategy this tool follows is to aim at your target size with resolution first and bitrate second, preserving the original frame rate and audio — because butchered sound annoys viewers more than slightly softer pictures.</p>
<p>Because the compression runs in your browser, big files take real time on your device — a long 4K clip is a genuine computing job, and a plugged-in laptop will chew through it faster than a phone. The privacy payoff is total: the video is never uploaded, so private, client or unreleased footage is compressed without ever leaving your machine, and there is no queue, no watermark and no size-based paywall — just physics.</p></section>
''',
"pdf-to-png": '''<section class="section"><h2>Pages as pictures, at print-grade sharpness</h2>
<p>A PDF page is a set of drawing instructions, and plenty of the places a page needs to go — a slide deck, a social post, a document that accepts images only, a preview thumbnail — cannot follow instructions. Converting to PNG renders each page into a high-resolution image: text stays razor sharp, diagrams keep their edges, and the result opens in every image viewer, editor and platform in existence. PNG is the right target when fidelity is the point, because it is lossless: unlike JPG, it never speckles small text or smudges line art.</p>
<p>Two properties define this variant. The render scale: pages are drawn at 2× size, so a page that looks crisp on screen also survives being zoomed, projected or printed at modest sizes — a 1× render would soften the moment anyone enlarges it. And the background: pages are rendered onto transparency rather than a forced white sheet, so a page whose content has its own colour scheme or transparent elements composites cleanly over other backgrounds — something neither a screenshot nor a JPG conversion can offer. Each page becomes its own file, named in page order, so a 40-page document arrives as a tidy sequence rather than one unwieldy strip.</p>
<p>Choose PNG when the pages contain text, tables or diagrams that must stay legible; choose PDF to JPG when the pages are essentially photographs and file size matters more. Everything renders in your browser using PDF.js on your own device — the document, which might be a contract or a payslip, is never uploaded to a server.</p></section>
''',
"pdf-to-webp": '''<section class="section"><h2>Document pages, at the weight the web wants</h2>
<p>When PDF pages are headed for a web page — a menu, a catalogue, a document preview, an online portfolio — the classic options both miss. PNG renders beautifully and weighs a fortune, because lossless storage of a full page of text and graphics is expensive. JPG is light but treats page text the way it treats photos, softening the crisp edges that make documents readable. WebP was designed in the gap between them: substantially smaller than PNG at the same sharpness, cleaner around text and line art than JPG, and natively supported by every current browser.</p>
<p>Pages here are rendered at 2× scale before encoding, which is the detail that decides whether the result looks professional. A page rendered at screen size and then displayed larger — on a retina display, a zoomed view, a big monitor — goes soft no matter the format; the 2× render keeps text pin-sharp in real use while WebP's compression keeps the file itself lean. Each page becomes its own WebP, in order, ready to drop into a page or a CMS.</p>
<p>The honest boundary is audience: WebP is a browser format, so if the images are headed for print, email or desktop publishing software, PNG is the safer destination. For anything that will be viewed on the web, this is the efficient route. Rendering happens in your browser on your device — the document never leaves your machine — so private paperwork can be converted without a second thought about where it went.</p></section>
''',
"rotate-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>The sideways scan problem, fixed at the source</h2>
<p>Scanners and phone cameras are optimists: they capture pages in whatever orientation the paper happened to be, and the PDF they produce opens with half its pages sideways or upside down. Every viewer lets you rotate the display, but that fix evaporates the moment you close the file — the next person, the archive, the printing service all meet the same tilted pages. This tool fixes the document itself: each page's rotation is set permanently, in 90° steps, so the PDF opens upright everywhere, forever, for everyone.</p>
<p>The job matters more than it looks. A rotated page is not just an annoyance — text in a sideways page cannot be selected, searched or read by accessibility tools in the right order, printers spit it out sideways, and a client or case officer receiving a bundle with mixed orientations reads it as carelessness. Rotating the whole document in one pass is the common case — a scanner that fed every page the same wrong way — and the angles cover the full compass: 90° for portrait pages lying on their side, 180° for the upside-down stack, 270° for the other side.</p>
<p>One thing this tool deliberately does not do: it does not guess. Automatic orientation detection is a research demo that gets confident wrong answers on sparse pages; here you choose the rotation, preview the result, and save — a ten-second human judgement that is simply more reliable. The file is processed in your browser and never uploaded, so the contracts, statements and ID documents that most often need rotating stay on your device.</p></section>
''',
"pdf-to-text": '''<section class="section"><h2>Getting the words out of the document</h2>
<p>PDF is where text goes to be looked at, not used. The words in a PDF are locked in a fixed layout — hard to search across documents, awkward to quote, impossible to feed into a spreadsheet, a translation tool or a text editor. Extracting the text sets it free: the document's content becomes a plain-text file you can search, copy, count, analyse, paste into other tools or hand to an AI assistant as context. Contracts get summarised, reports get quoted, long documents get searched for the one clause that matters.</p>
<p>Understanding what extraction can and cannot do saves real frustration. It reads the PDF's text layer — the actual character data the file carries — and it reads it well: multi-page documents, reports, ebooks and exported files yield clean, ordered text. But a scanned document has no text layer at all; it is a photograph of a page wearing a PDF coat. Extraction from a scan returns nothing, or next to nothing, because the words exist only as pixels. That job needs OCR — a different technology that reads the picture — and no honest extractor pretends otherwise.</p>
<p>Layout is the other expectation to set: plain text keeps the words and their reading order, not the columns, tables and typography. What you get is the content, ready to work with — which for searching, quoting and analysing is exactly the point. Extraction runs in your browser on your device; the document is never uploaded, so the contract or statement you are mining for its own words stays private.</p></section>
''',
"add-page-numbers-to-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>Small numbers that make documents navigable</h2>
<p>Page numbers look like decoration until a document is used by more than one person. Then they become infrastructure: the judge's bundle referenced by page, the thesis whose contents page must match reality, the report where "see page 14" is the difference between a ten-second find and a ten-minute scroll, the contract exhibit that has to be cited precisely. Any document long enough to be discussed is long enough to need numbers — and documents assembled from scans, exports and merged files almost never have them.</p>
<p>This tool stamps the numbers into the PDF itself, on every page, in the position and style you choose — bottom centre for the classic document look, corners for bundles and exhibits, with formats from a plain "3" to "Page 3 of 24", the style court bundles and formal reports prefer because it proves completeness as it navigates. Because the numbers are written into the file, they print with it, survive forwarding, and match on every screen and every paper copy — unlike a viewer's on-screen indicator, which vanishes the moment the file is printed.</p>
<p>Placement deserves one practical thought: keep numbers clear of existing content — most documents carry their margin furniture at the very bottom, so a bottom-centre number occasionally needs the footer style instead. The stamping happens in your browser, with the document processed on your device and never uploaded, which is the right way to handle documents formal enough to need page numbers in the first place.</p></section>
''',
"sign-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>Your signature on the page, without the printer</h2>
<p>The old ritual — print the form, sign it with a pen, scan it back, email the scan — survives mostly out of habit. This tool skips it: draw your signature with a mouse, trackpad or finger, or type your name in a signature style, and stamp it onto the exact page and position the document asks for. The signed PDF that comes back is the document itself with your mark embedded, ready to email to the landlord, the school, the client or the HR department that sent the form.</p>
<p>One distinction matters and this page states it plainly: this is a visual signature — an image of your signature placed on the page, which is what the overwhelming majority of everyday forms, agreements and acknowledgements actually accept. It is not a cryptographic digital signature of the kind banking and government PKI systems use, where a certificate proves identity mathematically and seals the document against alteration. If a counterparty specifically requires a certified digital signature, they will name the platform; for everything else — the everyday paper trail of forms and letters — a visual signature on the PDF is the accepted, expected artefact.</p>
<p>Your signature is personal in the deepest sense, so the process is built accordingly: everything happens in your browser, the document and your signature are processed on your device, and nothing — not the file, not the signature image — is uploaded or stored anywhere. Sign, download, close the tab, and no copy exists outside your hands.</p></section>
''',
"pdf-to-word": '''<section class="section"><h2>Unlocking the document you need to edit</h2>
<p>PDF is a final-form format: brilliant for sharing, deliberately hostile to editing. But documents refuse to stay final. The contract needs one clause changed and the original Word file is lost; the report exported last year needs this year's figures; the form must become a template. This tool converts a text-based PDF back into an editable Word document — real paragraphs of real text in a .docx you can open, rewrite and re-export, instead of retyping pages by hand or pasting mangled fragments out of a viewer.</p>
<p>Set expectations by the source. A PDF that was born digital — exported from Word, Google Docs or a publishing tool — carries a clean text layer, and it converts into a genuinely workable document: paragraphs, headings and lists in editable text, with layout approximated closely. A scanned PDF is a photograph in a PDF coat; it has no text layer to convert, so the honest result is little or nothing until the pages go through OCR elsewhere. And even with a clean source, treat the conversion as a strong draft rather than a facsimile: multi-column layouts, exotic fonts and intricate tables may need a tidy-up pass, because PDF stores where words sit, not what they mean structurally.</p>
<p>The conversion runs in your browser on your device — the document is never uploaded to a server. That matters precisely because the PDFs people most need to edit are contracts, statements, applications and drafts: documents whose contents are nobody else's business.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-christmas": '''<section class="section"><h2>The one countdown that runs the calendar</h2>
<p>No other date bends the year around itself like 25 December. Retailers plan their survival by it, schools and offices close for it, postage deadlines, travel prices and family logistics all key off it — and the shoppers' rule is as true as it is ignored: the people who enjoy December are the ones who started in October. A live countdown turns the vague sense of "plenty of time" into a number, which is either a comfort or a spur depending on the month you are reading it in.</p>
<p>The counter at the top of this page ticks in real time — days, hours, minutes and seconds to midnight at the start of Christmas Day — and it is right about the date every year, because Christmas is one of the few fixed points in the calendar: always 25 December, no lunar maths, no movable feast. What does move is the weekday, and the weekday changes the shape of the holiday: a Friday Christmas means a long weekend and a gentle run-up; a Wednesday Christmas splits the week in two and eats annual leave. The countdown page tells you the day-count; planning the leave, the travel day and the last-post deadline around it is the genuinely useful part.</p>
<p>Everything runs in your browser with no sign-up and nothing stored — open the page in December and it is simply there, ticking. Counting down to a different date entirely — a birthday, a wedding, a deadline? The Days Until Any Date counter builds the same live countdown to any day you choose.</p></section>
''',
"christmas-countdown": '''<section class="section"><h2>Why a live countdown beats a calendar square</h2>
<p>A date circled on a calendar is easy to ignore; a number that shrinks every second is not. That is why this countdown shows more than days: the hours, minutes and seconds underneath turn "some time in December" into something you can feel approaching, and the weeks-and-weekends reading turns it into a plan — five weekends left means five shopping trips, five visiting slots, five chances to book the train before the prices climb.</p>
<p>For children the honest unit is sleeps, and the counter answers that too: the days figure is the sleeps figure, one night at a time. For everyone else the useful trick is to read the countdown backwards — fix the day everything must be finished by, count the full weekends the timer says are left, and give each weekend one job. December stops being a surprise that way. And because the timer runs on your own device clock, it agrees with the midnight your household will actually celebrate — open it on Christmas Eve and it counts down to the start of the day itself.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-new-year": '''<section class="section"><h2>Midnight, one timezone at a time</h2>
<p>New Year is the only countdown the whole planet shares — and it arrives in waves. The moment 1 January begins in Auckland, it is still the morning of 31 December in London and the small hours in New York; the new year sweeps westward around the globe across a full day, timezone by timezone, which is why the live counter at the top of this page is set to your timezone: the midnight that matters is the one where you are standing.</p>
<p>The counting itself is simple but the planning around it is not. Year-end is a thicket of deadlines wearing party clothes: tax years, insurance renewals, contracts and subscriptions that expire "at year end", the last postage dates, the final working day. The counter ticks days, hours, minutes and seconds to the start of 1 January, and New Year's Day 2027 falls on a Friday — a detail worth knowing, because it means the new year opens with a long weekend in most countries and the first real working day of 2027 is Monday the 4th.</p>
<p>And then there are resolutions, whose failure is famously a planning failure rather than a character flaw: "from January" is a wish, while "in the 41 days left, three days a week" is a plan. Watching the exact count is a small, honest way to make the deadline real. The countdown runs entirely in your browser, live to the second, with nothing to install and nothing stored.</p>
<p>The wave has a first and a last stop. The Line Islands of Kiribati, at UTC+14, are the first inhabited places to greet the new year — a full day ahead of Honolulu, among the last, which is still finishing 31 December for a full hour after Sydney's fireworks are a memory. It is a fitting oddity for the festival of fresh starts: for roughly 26 hours, the planet holds three calendar dates at once, and somewhere it is always just about to be next year.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-ramadan": '''<section class="section"><h2>A countdown to a date written in the sky</h2>
<p>Ramadan does not have a fixed date on the solar calendar — it begins with the sighting of the new crescent moon that opens the ninth month of the Islamic lunar year. Because the lunar year is about eleven days shorter than the solar one, Ramadan travels: across a lifetime it visits every season, bringing short winter fasts in some decades and long summer ones in others. The expected start for 2027 is 7 February, and the live countdown at the top of this page counts to it — with the understanding held by everyone who observes the month that the confirmed date follows the moon sighting, and neighbouring countries occasionally differ by a day.</p>
<p>The countdown matters because Ramadan is prepared for, not just arrived at. The month rearranges sleep, work, meals, travel and training around the fast from dawn to sunset, and the practical preparations — adjusting schedules, stocking a kitchen, planning leave for the last ten nights — all run backwards from the start date. The month then runs 29 or 30 days until the next crescent opens Shawwal and Eid al-Fitr arrives, expected on 9 March 2027; our Eid countdown tracks that day separately.</p>
<p>Whether you are counting down to observe, to support family and colleagues who do, or to plan travel and business around the month, the counter runs live in your browser — days, hours, minutes and seconds — with nothing to sign up for and nothing recorded. Ramadan Mubarak when it comes.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-eid": '''<section class="section"><h2>The feast at the end of the fast</h2>
<p>Eid al-Fitr — the Festival of Breaking the Fast — falls on 1 Shawwal, the first day of the month that follows Ramadan, and it is earned the way few festivals are: after 29 or 30 days of dawn-to-sunset fasting. The expected date for 2027 is Tuesday 9 March, set by the sighting of the Shawwal crescent moon, which is why the countdown at the top of this page describes itself as expected: the celebration is confirmed by the moon, and some countries mark it a day apart from their neighbours in some years.</p>
<p>The countdown serves the festival's real logistics. Eid is a visiting festival — family rounds, sweets and Eidi gifts for children, new clothes bought in advance, food prepared in quantity, and in much of the world a public holiday whose exact days employers and schools confirm late. Counting the days makes the planning honest: the outfit ordered in time, the leave requested, the travel booked before prices understand what week it is. For the many people counting down simply because the fast is long and the feast is sweet, the ticking number from the end of Ramadan onwards needs no justification at all.</p>
<p>The counter runs live in your browser — days, hours, minutes and seconds to the expected morning — and asks for nothing: no account, no stored data. If you are counting to the year's other great festival, the Eid al-Adha countdown sits alongside this one. Eid Mubarak when it arrives.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-eid-al-adha": '''<section class="section"><h2>The Festival of Sacrifice, and the pilgrimage at its heart</h2>
<p>Eid al-Adha falls on 10 Dhul Hijjah, the tenth day of the final month of the Islamic lunar year, at the climax of the Hajj pilgrimage to Makkah. It commemorates Abraham's willingness to sacrifice his son and the ram provided in his place, and it is marked by prayer, charity and the Qurbani sacrifice whose meat is shared between family, neighbours and those in need. As a lunar festival it moves about eleven days earlier each solar year; the expected date in 2027 is in mid-May, confirmed — as with all these dates — by the sighting of the new moon.</p>
<p>The countdown earns its place in the practical arrangements. Eid al-Adha is a major public holiday across the Muslim world and widely observed beyond it: livestock and Qurbani arrangements are booked ahead, travel peaks, offices and schools close for days at a time, and family gatherings on this scale do not improvise themselves. Knowing the count — and therefore the likely holiday window — is how leave gets requested, flights get booked before the seasonal surge, and businesses plan their quietest week of the year with their eyes open.</p>
<p>The live counter at the top of this page ticks days, hours, minutes and seconds to the expected date, entirely in your browser, with nothing stored and nothing to sign up for. Those counting to the year's other Eid will find the Eid al-Fitr countdown alongside. Eid Mubarak when it comes.</p></section>
''',
"days-until": '''<section class="section"><h2>Any date becomes real when you can see it coming</h2>
<p>There is a psychological trick hidden in every countdown: a date in a calendar is abstract, but "63 days, 4 hours and 12 minutes" is a fact you can feel. That is why countdowns work where resolutions fail. The exam, the wedding, the visa expiry, the product launch, the baby, the flight home — each has a date that deserves better than being vaguely "in a few months". This tool builds a live countdown to any date you give it, ticking every second, so the distance between today and the day is always exact and always visible.</p>
<p>The uses sort themselves into two families. Anticipation: birthdays, holidays, reunions, release dates — the countdown as pleasure, shared with children and group chats. And obligation: deadlines, renewals, probation ends, submission dates — the countdown as discipline, because work planned backwards from a ticking number ("eleven days, so the draft exists by day six") gets finished, while work planned "soon" gets an apology. Either way, precision beats estimation: people are chronically wrong about how much time is left, in both directions, and the counter simply removes the guessing.</p>
<p>The countdown is built and run entirely in your browser. Pick the date and the counter starts; bookmark the page and it is there when you return, still counting. Nothing is stored on any server and there is nothing to sign up for — the date you are counting to is your business, which is exactly how a countdown to a personal day should work.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-halloween": '''<section class="section"><h2>31 October, the fixed point of autumn</h2>
<p>Halloween never moves: 31 October, every year, the eve of All Saints' Day, with roots running back to the Celtic festival of Samhain, when the boundary between the living and the dead was thought to thin. The modern version — costumes, jack-o'-lanterns, trick-or-treating, horror marathons — is a layering of that old festival, immigrant customs and a century of enthusiastic commercial invention, and it now anchors the entire autumn season for retailers, party venues and fancy-dress shops.</p>
<p>The countdown at the top of this page ticks live to the start of 31 October, and its real usefulness starts in September: costumes that need ordering, parties that need booking, pumpkins that need carving close to the day (a carved pumpkin is a countdown of its own, and not a long one). The weekday Halloween falls on changes its character completely — a Saturday Halloween is a full weekend of parties, a Tuesday Halloween is a school-night negotiation — and knowing the day-count early is how the celebration gets planned around work, school and the half-term break rather than crashing into them.</p>
<p>Everything runs in your browser — no sign-up, nothing stored, the counter simply ticks. If the date you are really watching belongs to a different festival, the family of countdowns alongside this one covers the year's other fixed points and feasts.</p>
<p>A word on the evening itself: trick-or-treating runs on local custom rather than law, with most neighbourhoods working the hours between dusk and about 9 p.m., which in late October means the smallest visitors start in near-darkness — another reason the countdown matters, because costumes involving visibility, and routes planned in daylight, are decided in the calm weeks before. Halloween has grown into the second-largest commercial festival of the American year after Christmas; the pumpkins, though, keep their own schedule: carve in the final two or three days, or the lantern grins at the pavement instead of the guests.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-thanksgiving": '''<section class="section"><h2>The fourth Thursday, and the busiest table of the year</h2>
<p>American Thanksgiving is a movable feast by rule rather than by moon: the fourth Thursday of November, fixed by Congress in 1941 after decades of drifting dates. (Canada celebrates its own Thanksgiving on the second Monday of October, a separate harvest festival with the same name — the countdown here follows the US date.) The rule produces a subtle variation worth planning around: in some years there are nearly five weeks between Halloween and Thanksgiving, in others barely three and a half, and the Christmas season expands or compresses accordingly.</p>
<p>No date in the American year moves more people: the days around Thanksgiving are consistently among the busiest travel days in the country, flights and trains price accordingly, and the dinner itself — ordered turkeys, borrowed chairs, the logistics of twelve people's dietary requirements — rewards anyone who counts backwards honestly. The live counter at the top of this page gives the exact distance to the day, which the practically minded use for the real deadlines: the turkey order, the flight booking, the work handover before the longest weekend of the autumn.</p>
<p>And Thanksgiving is a hinge date: the day after it is Black Friday, whose countdown sits alongside this one. The counter runs live in your browser, days, hours, minutes and seconds, with nothing to sign up for — gratitude, as it turns out, needs no account.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-diwali": '''<section class="section"><h2>A festival dated by the new moon</h2>
<p>Diwali, the festival of lights, falls on the new-moon day — Amavasya — of the Hindu month of Kartik, which lands between mid-October and mid-November on the solar calendar. The main day, when Lakshmi, goddess of prosperity, is honoured and homes fill with diyas and lamps, is the centre of a five-day festival that begins with Dhanteras and ends with Bhai Dooj. Because the date is set by the lunar calendar, it must be computed afresh every year — which is precisely what the live countdown at the top of this page is for.</p>
<p>The counting drives real arrangements. Diwali is India's great season of travel, gifting and gold-buying: trains and flights sell out, sweetshops and jewellers plan their year around it, businesses close, and the exchange of gifts and mithai between families and colleagues runs on a schedule set by the festival days. For the diaspora it is also the year's strongest pull homeward, and school and office closures — Diwali is a public holiday in countries far beyond India — are planned around the computed date.</p>
<p>The counter ticks live in your browser, days, hours, minutes and seconds, with nothing stored and nothing to sign up for. And since the festival spans five days, knowing the main date is knowing the anchor: the celebrations unfold on either side of the day this page counts to. Happy Diwali when it arrives.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-easter": '''<section class="section"><h2>The most computed date in the calendar</h2>
<p>Easter has no date — it has a formula, the oldest continuously used one in the calendar: the first Sunday after the first ecclesiastical full moon falling on or after 21 March. The result wanders between 22 March and 25 April, a five-week window that reshuffles school terms, holiday prices and spring plans every year. The countdown at the top of this page does the computus for you and ticks live to Easter Sunday — the Western date, used by Catholic and Protestant churches. Orthodox Easter follows the older Julian calendar and a slightly different rule, so in many years the two Easters fall on different Sundays, occasionally a month apart.</p>
<p>The wandering date is why the countdown matters more for Easter than for fixed feasts. A "late Easter" pushes school holidays into warmer weather and stretches the spring term before it; an "early Easter" compresses everything — and retailers, holiday companies and anyone planning around Good Friday and Easter Monday closures plan backwards from a date that refuses to sit still. Knowing the exact day-count early is the difference between booking the break and discovering the break.</p>
<p>The counter runs entirely in your browser, live to the second, and rolls forward each year to the next Easter when one passes. Nothing is stored, nothing is asked. For countdowns to the year's other great festivals, fixed and movable alike, the family of counters alongside this one covers the calendar.</p>
<p>The weekend around the day carries its own planning weight: Good Friday and Easter Monday are public holidays across much of the world — though not uniformly, and not both everywhere — so the four-day weekend Easter creates in many countries is precisely why its wandering date reshuffles travel prices and workplace cover. Count the days here, then check which of the surrounding days your own calendar actually gives you.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-valentines-day": '''<section class="section"><h2>14 February: the deadline romantics ignore</h2>
<p>Valentine's Day is fixed — 14 February, every year — and yet it ambushes more people than any movable feast, because nothing about January feels like a warning. The live countdown at the top of this page exists to make the distance honest. In 2027 the day falls on a Sunday, a detail with real consequences: restaurants stretch the celebration across the whole weekend, the Friday and Saturday tables book out first, and a Sunday Valentine's is a brunch-and-flowers affair rather than a dinner one — plan the version the calendar is actually offering.</p>
<p>The history is older than the card industry: a Roman-era feast day for Saint Valentine, folded by Chaucer's century into the season when birds were said to choose their mates, and industrialised in the nineteenth century into the festival of cards, flowers and chocolate we know. The practical deadlines are the ones the countdown serves — restaurant reservations that vanish weeks ahead, florists whose prices climb as the day approaches, postage dates for anything mailed. Romance may be spontaneous; its logistics are not, and the romantics who look effortless in February are the ones who counted in January.</p>
<p>The counter ticks live in your browser — days, hours, minutes and seconds — with no sign-up and nothing stored. Counting to an anniversary of your own instead? The Days Until Any Date counter builds the same live countdown to any day that matters to you.</p>
<p>The scale of the day rewards the early. Valentine's is the second-biggest card-sending occasion of the year after Christmas, with well over a hundred million cards exchanged, and the fresh-flower trade moves a significant share of its annual roses through this single week — which is why the same bouquet follows a price curve that climbs as the countdown falls. None of this is an argument against the gesture; it is an argument for making it while the counting is still in double digits.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-mothers-day": '''<section class="section"><h2>The second Sunday of May — mostly</h2>
<p>The Mother's Day this page counts to is the American one: the second Sunday of May, established in 1914 and now kept on that rule across much of the world. The rule makes the date drift between 8 and 14 May, and the drift has a planning consequence people rediscover annually — in early-May years the day arrives barely a week after the month turns, ambushing the unprepared; in late-May years there is a fortnight of grace. (Britain keeps its older Mothering Sunday instead, on the fourth Sunday of Lent — a different date entirely, tied to Easter, so a family split across the Atlantic gets two chances and two chances to forget.)</p>
<p>The countdown at the top of this page ticks live to the day, and its honest use is logistical: flowers ordered before the seasonal surge pricing, a card posted to arrive rather than to apologise, a table booked at the restaurant that fills every year, a call scheduled across time zones — the World Clock alongside helps when the call crosses oceans. Mother's Day has become one of the biggest flower and greeting-card days of the year, and every supplier in that chain prices by proximity to it; counting the days is how the same gesture costs less and lands better.</p>
<p>Everything runs in your browser with nothing stored and no sign-up. The counter rolls to next year's date when this one passes, because the second Sunday of May is a debt that comes due annually.</p>
<p>The day's own history carries an irony the countdown cannot fix but should record: Anna Jarvis, who campaigned the holiday into existence in memory of her mother, spent her later years campaigning against its commercialisation — appalled by the card and flower industries she had inadvertently enriched. The gesture she defended was a handwritten letter and a visit. A century on, the countdown's best use might be hers: not merely ordering earlier, but writing the actual words while there is still time to write them properly.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-fathers-day": '''<section class="section"><h2>The third Sunday of June, and the card bought in time</h2>
<p>Father's Day follows the American rule this page counts to: the third Sunday of June, first proposed in 1910 and a US federal holiday since 1972. The rule drifts the date between 15 and 21 June, which is just enough movement to catch people who remember it as "mid-June sometime". Much of the world keeps the same date — but not all: Australia and New Zealand celebrate on the first Sunday of September, and several Catholic countries keep 19 March, Saint Joseph's Day, which is why internationally scattered families live by the countdown rather than by habit.</p>
<p>The counter at the top of this page ticks live — days, hours, minutes and seconds — and its work is humble but real: the card posted to arrive on time (postage across oceans is measured in weeks, and Father's Day cards are the year's great late-arrival genre), the gift ordered before the day, the call planned across time zones with the World Clock alongside. The third-Sunday rule also places the day in the year's hinge: a week of the solstice, the start of summer holidays, barbecue season — the celebration the calendar designed is an outdoor one, weather permitting, and knowing exactly which Sunday it is decides the booking.</p>
<p>The countdown runs entirely in your browser, nothing stored, no account — and rolls forward to next year's third Sunday when this one has been properly celebrated.</p>
<p>The day has a founder worth knowing: Sonora Smart Dodd of Spokane, Washington, who campaigned for it from 1910 after hearing a Mother's Day sermon and thinking of the widowed father who had raised six children alone. It took until 1972 to become a permanent US holiday — over sixty years of campaigning — which may be the most fatherly possible origin story: necessary, unglamorous and eventually done. Retail surveys still record Mother's Day outspending Father's Day by a wide margin every year; the countdown is your chance to at least make the smaller number arrive on time.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-independence-day": '''<section class="section"><h2>July 4: the fixed star of the American summer</h2>
<p>Independence Day is immovable: 4 July, the anniversary of the Declaration of Independence adopted in 1776 — though the vote for independence itself passed on 2 July, a two-day head start John Adams always insisted was the real date. The celebration ignores him, as it has for two and a half centuries: fireworks, parades, cookouts, and the effective opening of high summer in the United States. When the 4th falls near a weekend the holiday stretches; in the middle of the week it becomes an island, and the countdown at the top of this page is how the leave days around it get planned honestly.</p>
<p>The ticking counter serves the day's real economy. Fireworks displays, campsite and beach-house bookings, town parades and the great summer sales all run backwards from the date, and travel prices understand the calendar perfectly — the days around the 4th rank with Thanksgiving and Christmas for crowded roads and full flights. Knowing the exact distance to the holiday is the difference between booking the lake house and reading about other people's.</p>
<p>The counter runs live in your browser — days, hours, minutes and seconds to the start of 4 July — with no sign-up and nothing stored. It rolls to next year when the fireworks fade, because July 4, like the republic, recurs annually.</p>
<p>One wrinkle the countdown helps you catch early: when 4 July falls on a Saturday, the federal holiday is observed on Friday 3 July, and when it falls on a Sunday it is observed on Monday 5 July — so the day off work and the day of the fireworks are not always the same day. Banks, post offices and government services follow the observed date, while the parades and parties keep the fourth itself. Knowing which year-shape you are in is the difference between booking the long weekend and booking the wrong one.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-black-friday": '''<section class="section"><h2>The day after Thanksgiving, and the start of the season</h2>
<p>Black Friday is defined by another festival: it is the Friday immediately after US Thanksgiving — the fourth Friday of November in effect, though the Thursday rule means it can fall anywhere from 23 to 29 November. Born in Philadelphia slang for the traffic chaos of the day, rebranded by retailers as the moment the year's accounts move into the black, it has grown from a day into a season that now starts in October — yet the day itself remains the peak: the deepest discounts, the doorbuster queues, the moment the stock and the prices are best and worst at once.</p>
<p>The countdown at the top of this page ticks live to it, and the strategic shopper uses it backwards: the wish-list priced in advance, so a "40% off" claim can be checked against what the item cost in October (a meaningful share of Black Friday discounts are measured against prices nobody ever charged); the budget fixed before the adrenaline; the decision about which two or three purchases actually matter. Retailers count down to this date with rather more at stake — for many it is the single largest revenue day of the year — and the prices on the day move by the hour, which is why knowing exactly how long remains, to the minute, is a tool rather than a curiosity.</p>
<p>The counter runs entirely in your browser, with nothing stored and nothing to sign up for. It pairs naturally with the Thanksgiving countdown one step earlier in the same week.</p>
<p>And the season no longer ends at midnight: Cyber Monday follows three days later, born when office connections were the fastest ones and now simply the second peak of the same wave. Retailers increasingly publish their headline prices ahead of the day — which quietly changes how to use this countdown: the days before are for verifying which offers are real, because a discount announced early can be checked against October's price long before the Friday adrenaline arrives.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-hanukkah": '''<section class="section"><h2>Eight nights, beginning at sundown</h2>
<p>Hanukkah begins on 25 Kislev in the Hebrew calendar, which lands anywhere from late November to late December on the solar one — a five-week window that makes the festival impossible to plan by habit. The first candle of the menorah is lit at sundown on the first evening, and one more candle is added each night for eight nights, commemorating the rededication of the Temple in Jerusalem and the single day's oil that burned for eight. The countdown at the top of this page counts to that first candle, and rolls each year to the next 25 Kislev.</p>
<p>The drifting date drives the practical side: in an early year Hanukkah is finished before the Christmas rush begins; in a late year the two festivals overlap entirely, school holidays align differently, and the travel, gifting and gathering plans of a family — latkes, dreidels, gelt, and eight evenings of lighting times that arrive with the early winter sunset — shift by weeks. Knowing the count early is how those plans get made while choices still exist, and how colleagues and schools understand which evenings matter.</p>
<p>The counter ticks live in your browser — days, hours, minutes and seconds — with nothing stored and no sign-up. As a lunar-calendar festival, Hanukkah shares its travelling nature with the year's other moon-set feasts, whose countdowns sit alongside this one. Happy Hanukkah when it comes.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-st-patricks-day": '''<section class="section"><h2>17 March: Ireland's day, the world's party</h2>
<p>Saint Patrick's Day is fixed at 17 March, the feast day of Ireland's patron saint, falling in the middle of Lent — a placement that historically paused the fast for a day, which may explain some of the celebration's enthusiasm. What began as a religious feast is now one of the world's most exported festivals: parades in Dublin and New York (New York's is among the largest on earth), rivers dyed green in Chicago, and pubs full on every continent, whether or not a drop of Irish blood is present.</p>
<p>The countdown at the top of this page ticks live to 17 March, and its value is in the shape of the week: a Patrick's Day on a Friday or a weekend is a city-wide event with bookings to match; on a weekday it is a more local affair with an early finish for the wise. The day also functions as the year's unofficial spring marker in the northern hemisphere — four days before the equinox, the first festival of the year that expects to be celebrated outdoors, weather's opinion notwithstanding.</p>
<p>Everything runs in your browser: no account, nothing stored, the counter simply ticks to the day. For the year's other fixed feasts and travelling festivals alike, the family of countdowns alongside this one keeps the same watch.</p></section>
''',
"timer": '''<section class="section"><h2>A countdown that ends with a sound, not a surprise</h2>
<p>The humble timer is the most used time tool in daily life and the one people improvise worst — the phone buried in a pocket, the oven clock nobody hears, the mental note that evaporates. This timer takes hours, minutes and seconds (or one tap on a preset), counts down on screen in large, legible figures, and sounds an alarm at zero that is impossible to confuse with a notification. Alongside it sits a lap stopwatch for the opposite job, counting up — so the page covers both directions of kitchen, classroom, gym and laboratory time.</p>
<p>The uses have a pattern: they are all moments when attention is somewhere else. Cooking, where the difference between twelve and fifteen minutes is dinner; workouts and interval training, where the rest period is the discipline; study techniques like Pomodoro, where twenty-five focused minutes only work if somebody trustworthy watches the clock; exams, presentations, speeches, naps, children's screen time, darkroom and laboratory processes. A big visible countdown also does social work — a class, a meeting or a game that can see its remaining time behaves differently from one guessing at it.</p>
<p>Once the page has loaded, the timer runs entirely on your device: no connection needed while it ticks, no account, nothing recorded. Set it, put it where you can see it, and let it be the thing that remembers — that is the entire job, done properly.</p></section>
''',
"stopwatch": '''<section class="section"><h2>Counting up, to the millisecond, with laps</h2>
<p>A stopwatch is a timer pointed the other way: not how long is left, but how long it took. The difference sounds trivial and is not — the stopwatch's defining feature is the lap, the ability to mark time at intervals without stopping the clock, and it is laps that make it a measuring instrument rather than a novelty. Coaches time splits, swimmers and runners pace by them, scientists and technicians log process stages against them, and anyone running a meeting, a rehearsal or a test learns more from the laps than from the total.</p>
<p>This stopwatch times to the millisecond and keeps a clean lap history you can read while it runs: each lap's split and the running total, so the pattern — a strong first lap, a fading third, the stage of the process that eats the time — is visible in the numbers rather than remembered wrongly. Millisecond precision sounds excessive until it is needed: photo-finishes in school sports days, reaction-time experiments, code and process benchmarks, the hundredths that separate finalists. The clock is your device's own, so its accuracy is the accuracy of the machine in your hand.</p>
<p>It runs entirely in your browser after the page loads — no connection, no account, nothing uploaded or stored. Open it, start it, and it simply keeps honest time until you tell it to stop: the oldest job in computing, done where you can see it.</p></section>
''',
"world-clock": '''<section class="section"><h2>What time is it there — the question remote life runs on</h2>
<p>Every distributed team, diaspora family and international friendship now runs on a question that had no daily meaning a generation ago: what time is it there, right now? This clock answers it at a glance — the live time in the world's working cities, New York to Tokyo, each updating every second, each with its date, because the truly disorienting discovery of remote life is that it is not merely a different time elsewhere; for half the planet it is a different day.</p>
<p>The traps are seasonal, and they catch professionals every year. Daylight saving does not change on the same day in different countries — for a few weeks each spring and autumn, the gap between, say, London and New York is four hours instead of the usual five — and the southern hemisphere's seasons run backwards, so Sydney moves its clocks the opposite way at the opposite time of year. Add the half-hour zones — India sits at UTC+5:30, Nepal at +5:45 — and "let's meet at 9" becomes a small research project. A live clock, reading the real zone rules from your own device, cuts through all of it: no mental arithmetic, no remembered offsets, just the time, now, there.</p>
<p>The clock runs in your browser and stores nothing. For fixing a specific meeting rather than reading the present moment — converting a stated time from one zone to another, daylight saving included — the Time Zone Converter alongside does that job directly.</p></section>
''',
"sunrise-sunset-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>The day's true edges, computed for any place on Earth</h2>
<p>Sunrise and sunset are not properties of a date; they are properties of a date in a place. The same Saturday that gives London sixteen hours of daylight gives Sydney ten, and a few degrees of latitude or a valley to the west moves the evening by minutes that matter — to photographers chasing the golden hour, to runners and cyclists timing a route, to farmers, sailors, astronomers, and everyone whose prayers, fasts or workdays are keyed to the sun. This calculator computes sunrise, sunset and total day length for any location and any date, from the astronomy itself: the Earth's tilt, the date's position in the orbit, and your coordinates on the globe.</p>
<p>The numbers have edges worth understanding. What is computed is the moment the sun's upper edge crosses the horizon, with atmospheric refraction included — the bending of light that lets us see the sun for a few minutes when it is technically already below the horizon. Twilight stretches beyond both ends (civil, nautical and astronomical), and at the extremes of latitude near the solstices the sun may not rise or set at all: the midnight sun and the polar night are the calculator honestly reporting that on that date, in that place, there is no such time.</p>
<p>The results reward planning: the photographer's blue hour, the traveller's arrival before dark, the gardener's true season length. All of it is calculated on your device from public astronomy — your location is used for the computation and stored nowhere.</p></section>
''',
"weekday-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Which day of the week was that? Every date has an answer</h2>
<p>It sounds like a parlour trick — naming the weekday of any date in history — but the perpetual calendar underneath this calculator is serious infrastructure. Weekdays decide things: contracts that specify working days, school and court schedules, the anniversary that must be celebrated on the right day, the historical document dated only "Tuesday week" that a researcher needs to pin to a real date. And the most-asked question of all is personal: what day of the week was I born? (Enter your birth date and find out — it is the classic use of this tool, along with the weekdays of weddings, first meetings and other dates a family keeps.)</p>
<p>The arithmetic hides real subtlety. The Gregorian calendar repeats exactly every 400 years, leap years follow a rule with exceptions inside exceptions (century years are not leap years unless divisible by 400 — 2000 was, 1900 was not), and any date calculation crossing those boundaries will punish naive counting. This calculator applies the full rules, so a date in 1752 and a date in 2152 are both answered correctly, and future dates are as reliable as past ones: planning an event two years out, you can know its weekday today, which is how venues and registrars already think.</p>
<p>Everything is computed in your browser with nothing stored. Dates are the most ordinary personal data there is, and there is no reason a weekday lookup should send yours anywhere.</p></section>
''',
"pregnancy-due-date-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Two hundred and eighty days, counted honestly</h2>
<p>The arithmetic behind this calculator is nearly two centuries old and still the standard first estimate: Naegele's rule, which counts 280 days — forty weeks — from the first day of the last menstrual period. Enter that date and the calculator returns the estimated due date, how many weeks along the pregnancy is today, and which trimester it is in (first: to week 13, second: weeks 14–27, third: week 28 to the birth). It is the same calculation made at the first midwife or doctor appointment, offered here without the waiting room.</p>
<p>What the estimate deserves is honest framing, because the due date is widely misunderstood as an appointment. It is the centre of a window: only about one baby in twenty arrives on the exact date, while the large majority arrive within a fortnight either side, and a pregnancy is considered full-term across a span of weeks, not a day. The date is genuinely useful — for planning leave, timing scans and purchases, and understanding the pregnancy's stage — provided it is held the way clinicians hold it: as a landmark, not a promise. Cycles longer or shorter than 28 days shift the estimate by days, and a dating scan, not any calculator, has the final word.</p>
<p>Dates this personal are computed entirely in your browser and stored nowhere. And this page is information, not medical advice — for anything about an actual pregnancy, the midwife and doctor who know it outrank every calculator on the internet, including this one.</p></section>
''',
"zodiac-sign-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Your sign, its element, and why the dates sometimes argue</h2>
<p>Enter a birthday and this calculator returns the tropical zodiac sign whose date range contains it, the sign's element — fire, earth, air or water — and the exact range it used, so the answer can be checked rather than merely believed. The tropical zodiac is the system used by Western astrology: twelve signs of thirty degrees each, anchored to the seasons, beginning with Aries at the March equinox. It is a framework nearly two thousand years old in its current form, and its date ranges are the ones printed in every newspaper column — which is why this calculator's ranges match the ones you will find across Western sources.</p>
<p>Two honest sources of disagreement are worth knowing. The changeover days — the cusps — are rounded differently by different sources, sometimes by a day, so a birthday on a boundary can be one sign here and the neighbour sign there; on a cusp date, the exact range shown on this page tells you which side of the line you fell. And the tropical system is not the only one: Vedic astrology uses the sidereal zodiac, tied to the constellations themselves, which has drifted about 24 degrees from the tropical signs as the Earth's axis slowly precesses — so the same birthday is often one sign earlier in the Indian system. Neither is an error; they are two different maps.</p>
<p>Your birth date is computed on your device and sent nowhere. Astrology's claims are a matter of belief, but its calendar is a matter of fact — and the calendar part, this page gets exactly right.</p></section>
''',
"time-zone-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>The meeting is at 9 — but whose 9?</h2>
<p>Converting a time between zones is the conversion people get wrong most confidently. The naive method — remembering that New York is five hours behind London — fails for weeks every year, because daylight saving begins and ends on different dates in different countries, so the gap breathes: five hours, then four, then five again. It fails completely in the southern hemisphere, where summer is December and the clocks move the other way, and it never even starts in the half-hour and forty-five-minute zones — India at +5:30, Nepal at +5:45 — that no round-number rule can produce.</p>
<p>This converter does the job the reliable way: pick the time, the date (the date matters — it decides which side of a daylight-saving change you are on) and both zones, and it applies the real rules from the international time zone database, the same data your phone's world clock uses. The result is the exact corresponding local time, including the awkward truth when the converted meeting lands on a different day entirely — a Tuesday morning in Tokyo that is still Monday evening in Los Angeles, the discovery that has embarrassed more than one calendar invitation.</p>
<p>The professional habit this enables is simple: always quote the meeting with its zone, and convert on the actual date rather than the usual offset. The conversion runs in your browser, stores nothing, and pairs with the World Clock alongside — one shows the time everywhere now, the other converts the time that matters to you.</p></section>
''',
"time-duration-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Time arithmetic is harder than it looks — base 60 sees to that</h2>
<p>Subtracting clock times defeats mental arithmetic for a structural reason: time is not decimal. Borrowing across hours means borrowing sixties, not hundreds, and the moment a shift crosses midnight the whole calculation changes species. How long was the night shift from 22:40 to 06:15? How many hours between a 09:17 start and a 17:43 finish, minus forty-five minutes of lunch? These are timesheet, payroll, travel and study questions that arrive weekly in working life, and they are exactly the questions people answer wrongly with total confidence.</p>
<p>This calculator works in both directions. Between two times, it gives the duration — handling the overnight wrap correctly, because a finish "earlier" than the start means the next day, not a negative shift. And it adds durations to clock times: start at 14:30, add three hours forty minutes of driving and a stop, and know the arrival time without counting on fingers. The results come back in hours and minutes and in decimal hours too, because payroll and invoicing think in decimals (7.5 hours) while humans think in clock time (7 hours 30) — and converting between those two is its own small trap this page quietly removes.</p>
<p>Everything is computed in your browser and nothing is recorded. Your hours are your own business; the calculator's only job is to make them add up.</p></section>
''',
"unix-timestamp-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>Seconds since 1970: the number underneath the internet</h2>
<p>Almost every system you have ever used keeps time as a single number: the count of seconds since midnight on 1 January 1970, UTC — the Unix epoch. It is in your log files, your database rows, your API responses and your analytics, usually looking like 1761187200 and conveying nothing to a human reader. This converter translates in both directions: a timestamp becomes a readable date and time, and any date and time becomes its timestamp, with the current epoch ticking live at the top of the page for the copy-paste jobs that start most debugging sessions.</p>
<p>The classic traps deserve their fame. The first is units: Unix time is seconds, but JavaScript's clock runs in milliseconds, so real-world data contains both ten-digit and thirteen-digit numbers, and feeding one kind where the other is expected produces dates in 1970 or the year 56000 — the single most common timestamp bug in working software. The second is time zones: the timestamp itself is zone-free (it is always UTC underneath), while every human-readable rendering of it depends on where you are standing, which is why the same number is two different clock times in London and Lahore. And for the archivists: 32-bit systems run out of seconds in January 2038, the one deadline the whole industry has agreed to worry about later.</p>
<p>All converting happens in your browser — timestamps from private logs and production systems are decoded on your own machine and sent nowhere.</p></section>
''',
"word-counter": '''<section class="section"><h2>The count that essays, applications and editors actually enforce</h2>
<p>Word limits are never decorative. The personal statement allows 500 words and rejects the 501st; the article brief says 1,200 and the editor counts; the grant application truncates at exactly its stated maximum, mid-sentence if necessary. This counter tracks the number as you type — words, characters with and without spaces, sentences, paragraphs and estimated reading time — because the time to discover you are 200 words over is while the text is still on your screen, not after submission.</p>
<p>Knowing what the numbers mean improves the writing, not just the compliance. Reading time at an average adult pace of roughly 200 words a minute tells a blogger whether the post is a three-minute read or a commitment; sentence and paragraph counts expose the texture problems writers cannot feel from inside — the paragraph that is secretly nine lines long, the stretch of forty sentences without a break. And the character counts serve the platforms: the post that must fit a character limit, the meta description with about 160 characters to earn a click, the form field that stops accepting input at its cap.</p>
<p>Your text is counted in your browser, as you type, and is never uploaded or stored anywhere — which matters, because the texts people count are drafts, statements, applications and manuscripts: precisely the writing that is nobody else's business until its owner decides otherwise.</p>
<p>The speaking estimate deserves its own sentence, because writers consistently over-trust it: a text that reads as five minutes at reading speed takes six to seven minutes spoken aloud, since speech runs near 140 words a minute against silent reading's 200-plus. If the words are headed for a mouth — a talk, a video, a ceremony — plan by the speaking figure, and the audience will hear the ending at the moment you promised it.</p></section>
''',
"qr-code-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>A QR code is a promise: point a camera, arrive somewhere</h2>
<p>The QR code's comeback was total: menus, payments, Wi-Fi passwords on café walls, event tickets, product packaging, the sign on a market stall. Underneath the squares is a genuinely robust piece of engineering — a QR code stores its data with Reed–Solomon error correction, which is why a code can be scuffed, partly covered or printed imperfectly and still scan: depending on the correction level, up to 30% of the code can be damaged without losing the message. Enter a link or text here and the generator draws the code and hands you a clean PNG, ready for a poster, a label, a table tent or a document.</p>
<p>Two decisions decide whether a printed code actually works. Size and contrast: a code crammed below about 2cm square, or printed in pale colours on a tinted background, defeats phone cameras in real lighting — black on white at a sane size scans from arm's length, which is the whole game. And data length: a long URL makes a dense, fragile code, while a short link makes a bold, forgiving one — shorten the destination first and the code gets easier to scan everywhere. The cardinal rule costs nothing: test the final printed code, at final size, with more than one phone, before the print run.</p>
<p>Your code is generated on your device — the link or text you enter is never sent to a server. That matters more than it might seem: the destination of an unreleased product page or a private document should stay yours until the moment you choose to print it.</p></section>
''',
"random-number-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Fair draws need better randomness than a shaken phone</h2>
<p>Picking a number "at random" by gut feeling is anything but — humans favour middle values, avoid repeats that are actually legitimate, and cluster in ways any statistician can spot. This generator takes the job seriously: it draws from your device's cryptographic random source, the same class of randomness your browser uses for security, so every number in your range is equally likely and no pattern is hiding in the sequence. Set the minimum and maximum, choose how many numbers you want, and the draw is made — sorted into order if that helps, and with a no-repeats option when duplicates would spoil the purpose.</p>
<p>The no-repeats mode is the one with real work to do: raffles and giveaway winners (where drawing the same entrant twice is a scandal in miniature), lottery-style picks, classroom cold-calling, draft orders, random samples for surveys and testing. With repeats allowed, each draw is independent — the right model for dice-like games, randomised trials and generating test data. The distinction sounds pedantic until the moment a duplicated raffle winner has to be explained to a room; getting it right in advance is the entire point of using a tool instead of a hat.</p>
<p>The draw happens locally on your device, using the browser's secure random generator, and nothing is recorded anywhere. If the draw will ever need to be defended — a public giveaway, a work allocation — screenshot the result with its settings; a fair draw you can show beats a fair draw you can only describe.</p></section>
''',
"case-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>Case is formatting, and formatting is meaning</h2>
<p>The same words in different case are different artefacts. UPPER CASE is a shout and a headline convention; Title Case is the shape of book titles, headlines and proper names; Sentence case is how prose breathes; lower case is the dialect of code, URLs and filenames — and retyping a passage to move between them is the kind of chore that introduces errors precisely because it is mindless. This converter transforms a whole text between the standard cases in one tap: paste it in mangled, take it out shaped, with every word handled consistently — including the edge cases humans botch, like the small words (a, the, of) that headline style keeps lower.</p>
<p>The cases earn their keep in different worlds. Writers and editors move between Title Case headlines and sentence-case subheads all day. Developers live in the stricter dialects — camelCase, snake_case, kebab-case — where case is not style but syntax, and a variable renamed wrongly is a bug. Social media and design work has its own conventions, and the all-caps announcement still has its uses provided it is a choice rather than a caps-lock accident. The unifying truth: readers judge correctness by consistency, and a text whose case is uniformly, deliberately one thing reads as professional before its first sentence is understood.</p>
<p>Your text is transformed in your browser, character by character, and never leaves your device — drafts, manuscripts and internal documents get reformatted without being uploaded anywhere.</p>
<p>A note from the research on all-caps, since it is the conversion most often requested: text set entirely in capitals is measurably slower to read — roughly a tenth slower in classic legibility studies — because word shapes flatten into uniform rectangles. Capitals are for labels, warnings and single lines of emphasis. Anything longer that arrives in shout-case is a candidate for Sentence case and a calmer page, which is most of what this converter is ever asked to do.</p></section>
''',
"password-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Length beats cleverness: the maths of a strong password</h2>
<p>Human-chosen passwords fail in predictable ways — names, dates, a capital letter at the front and a digit at the end — because attackers' software tries the predictable first, at billions of guesses a second. A generated password wins by being unmemorable in the technical sense: drawn truly at random from a large alphabet, so no pattern exists to exploit. The maths is dominated by length: a random 16-character password mixing cases, digits and symbols carries around 100 bits of entropy, a space so large that brute force stops being a plan and becomes a geology project.</p>
<p>This generator builds passwords on your own device, using your browser's cryptographic random source — the passwords are never sent anywhere, stored anywhere or seen by anyone, which is the only safe way a password generator can work. Choose length first (16 or more for anything that matters), keep the symbol and digit sets on unless a site's rules forbid them, and generate one per account: uniqueness matters as much as strength, because the realistic attack is not guessing your password but reusing one leaked from a breached site against all your others.</p>
<p>The necessary companion habit: generated passwords are not meant to be remembered, so let a password manager remember them — that is the system working as designed. What you must never do is generate a password on any tool that sends it across a network; this one does the whole job locally, in the tab, and forgets everything when you close it.</p></section>
''',
"text-diff-checker": '''<section class="section"><h2>Two versions, and the exact words that moved</h2>
<p>Every profession has the moment: the contract came back "with minor changes", the editor returned the draft, the terms were updated, the code was copied and altered. Reading two long texts against each other by eye is slow and unreliable, because the changes that matter are crafted to sit quietly among the unchanged — a "shall" become "may", a figure altered, a clause missing. This tool puts two texts side by side and highlights every addition and removal, so the review becomes verification instead of detection: you read the changes, not the documents.</p>
<p>The comparison works the way diff tools have worked since the earliest days of computing: it finds the longest common structure between the two texts and reports everything outside it as changed. Pasted prose, contract clauses, essays, policies, config files and code snippets all diff cleanly; the highlighting distinguishes text that was removed from the first version and text added in the second, so the direction of every change — who gave, who took — is visible at a glance. For documents with legal or financial weight, that direction is the story.</p>
<p>Both texts are processed in your browser and never uploaded — an essential property, because the texts people diff are drafts, contracts, unreleased work and internal documents, the exact material that should not be pasted into strangers' servers. Compare locally, decide locally.</p></section>
''',
"json-formatter": '''<section class="section"><h2>JSON is written for machines and debugged by humans</h2>
<p>JSON runs the modern web — every API response, config file and data export wears it — but in its natural state it is a single unbroken line of brackets that no human can audit. Formatting it properly, with honest indentation, turns a wall of syntax into a structure you can read: objects nested inside objects, arrays in order, the value you actually came for visible at a glance. And when JSON is broken, this tool does the second essential job: it validates strictly and reports the error with its line and column, instead of leaving you to bisect a thousand characters by eye.</p>
<p>The usual suspects are worth knowing, because they cause nearly every failure: trailing commas after the last item (legal in JavaScript, illegal in JSON), single quotes where JSON demands double, unquoted keys, comments smuggled into a format that forbids them, and the invisible one — a mismatched brace three hundred lines from where the parser finally gave up. A formatter that validates first and formats second turns all of those from a debugging session into a ten-second fix. The minify direction matters too: the same data, stripped to one compact line, for payloads where every kilobyte of whitespace is a real cost.</p>
<p>Everything is parsed on your device. API responses and config files routinely contain tokens, internal URLs and customer data in their natural state — exactly the material that should never be pasted into a server-side formatter — so the parsing happens in your browser, and nothing is transmitted or kept.</p></section>
''',
"dice-roller": '''<section class="section"><h2>From d4 to d100: the dice bag, digitised</h2>
<p>Tabletop gaming runs on polyhedral dice, and its notation is a small language of its own: a d20 is a twenty-sided die, 2d6 means roll two six-siders and add them, and the +3 after it is a modifier applied to the total. This roller speaks that language — dice from the d4 pyramid to the d100 percentile pair, any number of them, with modifiers, and a running history of every roll so the record of the session is never in dispute.</p>
<p>Digital dice earn their place honestly. The physical bag fails in mundane ways: the one die the roll needs is missing, lost or still in another city; a roll of 8d6 for a fireball takes a minute of gathering and adding while the table waits; statistics exercises and game design need hundreds of rolls, not twelve. A roller that shows each die's result alongside the total keeps the transparency of the table — the critical hit was a natural 20 and everyone saw it — while the arithmetic happens instantly. And the history matters more than new players expect: probability stops being a theory when you can scroll your own last fifty rolls.</p>
<p>The rolls are generated on your device with the browser's random source, and nothing is recorded anywhere outside the page. No dice were harmed; several goblins, statistically, were.</p></section>
''',
"lorem-ipsum-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Placeholder text with a two-thousand-year pedigree</h2>
<p>Lorem ipsum is not gibberish — it is a scrambled passage from Cicero's De Finibus, a first-century-BC treatise on ethics, mangled somewhere in the sixteenth century into the dummy text typesetters have used ever since. Its genius is what it refuses to do: it looks like real prose — real word lengths, real sentence rhythms — without meaning anything, so a design can be judged on its layout, typography and spacing without a single reader being distracted by the content. That neutrality is exactly what a mock-up, a template or a page still waiting on its copywriter needs.</p>
<p>Generate it here by the unit the job needs — paragraphs for article layouts, sentences for cards and sidebars, words for headlines and labels — and the eternal cautions apply. Lorem ipsum is a scaffolding, not a building: the classic professional humiliation is the site, brochure or app that shipped with the placeholder still in it, so it should be the last thing replaced and the first thing searched for before anything goes live. And while it mimics the shape of text, it does not mimic your text — real copy in the audience's language, at its real lengths, will break a layout that lorem ipsum flattered, which is why the final check should always use the genuine words.</p>
<p>The generator runs in your browser, endlessly patient and entirely local — Cicero, scrambled on your own machine, with nothing uploaded and nothing stored.</p></section>
''',
"base64-encoder-decoder": '''<section class="section"><h2>The encoding that is everywhere — and is not encryption</h2>
<p>Base64 exists because the early internet spoke text, and data that was not text — images, files, raw bytes — needed a disguise to travel through systems built for words. It re-writes binary data using 64 safe characters (letters, digits, + and /), at a cost of about 33% more size. It is inside your email attachments, your data URIs, your API tokens and your embedded images. This tool encodes text to Base64 and decodes it back, safely in both directions: Unicode is handled properly, so accented letters, emoji and non-Latin scripts — the input that breaks naive encoders — survive the round trip exactly, and a URL-safe variant is offered for the places (addresses, tokens, filenames) where + and / cause trouble.</p>
<p>The misunderstanding to retire: Base64 is an encoding, not a lock. Anyone can decode a Base64 string in a second — this very page will do it — so it hides nothing from anybody; it is an envelope for transport, never a safe. Passwords, keys and secrets do not belong in it unless a real encryption step happened first, a distinction whose confusion has leaked credentials from a thousand codebases. Used for its actual purpose, though, it is indispensable plumbing: moving binary through JSON, embedding assets in text files, debugging what an API really sent.</p>
<p>Encoding and decoding happen in your browser, on your device. The strings people decode are often lifted from private systems and logs — they are decoded here and sent nowhere.</p></section>
''',
"url-encoder-decoder": '''<section class="section"><h2>Why URLs speak in percent signs</h2>
<p>A URL is only allowed a small alphabet: letters, digits and a handful of symbols. Everything else — spaces, ampersands, question marks, accented letters, entire alphabets — must be translated into percent-encoding, where each unsafe byte becomes a % followed by two hex digits: a space is %20, an ampersand %26, and a single Arabic or Chinese character becomes a chain of three encoded bytes. This tool encodes and decodes in both of the modes that matter, and knowing the difference between them is the difference between a working link and a broken one.</p>
<p>Component mode encodes everything reserved, making a piece of text safe to sit inside one part of a URL — a search query, a parameter value, a filename — so a user's search for "fish & chips" cannot accidentally become two parameters. Full-URL mode preserves the structural characters (://, /, ?, &) that hold an address together and encodes only the unsafe parts, for cleaning up a complete link someone pasted with spaces in it. The classic bugs live in the confusion: double-encoding a value (%2520, a percent sign that got encoded twice) or decoding a whole URL when only its query was meant to be read.</p>
<p>All of it runs in your browser, on your device — because URLs in the wild carry tracking IDs, reset tokens and internal addresses, and there is no reason for a stranger's server to see the one you are untangling.</p></section>
''',
"uuid-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>An identifier so unique it needs no one's permission</h2>
<p>A UUID solves a coordination problem: how do many systems, offline and unconsulted, mint identifiers that never collide? The version 4 answer is randomness at scale — 122 bits of it, formatted as the familiar 8-4-4-4-12 pattern of hex digits. The space is so large that generating a billion UUIDs a second for a century would still leave a collision more unlikely than most events physics bothers to name, which is why databases, APIs, devices and files all use UUIDs as primary keys and reference IDs without a central registry to ask.</p>
<p>Generate one at a time for the everyday jobs — an ID for a record, a filename, a test fixture — or a bulk list of up to a hundred when seeding data, provisioning resources or preparing identifiers in advance. The generator uses your browser's cryptographic random source, because a UUID is only as unique as its randomness is honest: a weak generator quietly narrows that astronomical space into something collidable, which defeats the entire construction. Properly generated, a v4 UUID reveals nothing about when, where or by whom it was made — the version digit and variant bits are the only structure in it.</p>
<p>Everything is generated on your device and nothing is stored or transmitted. Identifiers minted here exist only where you paste them — which, given that their entire purpose is to be unguessable points in an infinite space, is exactly as it should be.</p></section>
''',
"hash-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>A fingerprint for data: same input, same hash, always</h2>
<p>A hash function compresses any input — a character, a novel, a file — into a fixed-length fingerprint with three magical properties: it is deterministic (the same input always yields the same hash), it is one-way (the fingerprint cannot be run backwards into the input), and it avalanches (change one letter and the fingerprint changes completely). Type here and watch MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256 and SHA-512 fingerprints update as you type — the working demonstration of how downloads are verified, how file integrity is checked, and how systems confirm data has not been tampered with.</p>
<p>The family has a hierarchy the honest page should state. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for security purposes — collisions have been constructible for years — and survive legitimately only as fast checksums for spotting accidental corruption. SHA-256 and SHA-512 remain the working standards for integrity and signatures. And one misuse towers over the rest: a plain hash is not password storage. Hashes are fast by design, and fast means guessable at billions of attempts per second; real password systems use slow, salted constructions built for that single job. Use this tool to verify, fingerprint and learn — not to protect secrets.</p>
<p>Your text is hashed on your device, in your browser, and goes nowhere — the right arrangement, since the things people fingerprint are so often the things they least want transmitted.</p></section>
''',
"slug-generator": '''<section class="section"><h2>The last part of the URL is a sentence in uniform</h2>
<p>A slug is the readable tail of a web address — /how-to-choose/ rather than /p?id=4815 — and it quietly does three jobs: it tells a human what the page is before they click, it tells a search engine the same thing, and it survives being read aloud, pasted into print and shared in a chat without breaking. Making one by hand invites the classic messes: spaces that become %20, capital letters, punctuation that URLs cannot carry, underscores where convention demands hyphens. This generator takes any title or headline and returns the clean form in one tap: lowercase, words joined by hyphens, everything a URL cannot digest stripped away.</p>
<p>The craft rules are few and worth following. Keep it short — the words that identify the topic, not the whole headline; "best-running-shoes" outlives "the-10-best-running-shoes-for-beginners-in-2026" in every way that matters. Use the words people would search for, in the order they would say them. And once a slug is published, treat it as permanent: changing a URL orphans every link, bookmark and ranking the page has earned, which is why a minute spent choosing the slug now is worth an afternoon of redirects later.</p>
<p>The conversion happens in your browser, instantly, with nothing stored — paste tomorrow's headline, take back its address. Small tool, permanent consequences, done properly.</p></section>
''',
"remove-duplicate-lines": '''<section class="section"><h2>Every list you inherit is full of doubles</h2>
<p>Lists accumulate duplicates the way harbours accumulate barnacles: the email list merged from three sources, the keyword set combined from two tools, the inventory pasted from four spreadsheets, the log distilled from a week's files. Working with a duplicated list means mailing people twice, counting things wrongly and chasing entries that were never two things at all. Paste the list here and the repeated lines are stripped out — the first occurrence of each line is kept and the original order is preserved, because the list you get back should be your list, cleaned, not a stranger's idea of it.</p>
<p>The details that matter are the ones this tool gets right by design. Order preservation means a ranked list stays ranked and a sequence stays a sequence. First-occurrence keeping means the position a thing earned in your list is the position it keeps. And the comparison is exact, line by line, which is both the strength and the thing to know: lines differing by a trailing space or a capital letter are different lines to a computer. For lists where those near-duplicates matter, a sort first makes them sit together where your eye can catch the variants — the Sort Lines tool alongside exists for exactly that pairing.</p>
<p>Your list is processed on your device and never uploaded. That matters because lists are so often the crown jewels in miniature: customer emails, subscriber addresses, internal inventories — data whose cleaning should not require handing it to a server.</p></section>
''',
"sort-lines": '''<section class="section"><h2>Order is a feature: alphabetical, by length, or deliberately not</h2>
<p>An unsorted list hides everything: duplicates sit apart from each other, the item you need is somewhere in the middle, and patterns that would be obvious in order stay invisible. Paste a list here and sort it the way the job demands — A to Z for the directory, glossary or name list; Z to A when the newest or largest should lead; by length, the undersung option that surfaces your shortest and longest entries at a glance (the keyword list whose monsters and runts both need attention); or shuffle, which destroys order on purpose for randomised running orders, draws and test sequences.</p>
<p>One trap deserves its warning label, because it catches everyone once: computers sort text as text, so numbers sorted alphabetically line up as 1, 10, 100, 2, 20 — correct by the alphabet's rules and useless as numerics. If your lines begin with numbers you intend numerically, the honest workarounds are zero-padding (002, 010, 100) or sorting lines by the part of the text that is not the number. Case and leading spaces also vote in alphabetical order — a capital letter can pull a line above its lowercase neighbours — which is why a sort sometimes looks one line "wrong" while being perfectly faithful to what it was given.</p>
<p>Sorting happens on your device, instantly, with the list never uploaded. Lists of customers, keywords, files and names get ordered where they live: with you.</p></section>
''',
"reverse-text": '''<section class="section"><h2>Backwards by letter, by word, or by line — three different tricks</h2>
<p>Reversing text sounds like one job and is actually three, and the difference matters. Reverse by character and the whole string turns around letter by letter — "stressed" becomes "desserts", the palindrome-maker's view of the world. Reverse by word and each word stays intact while their order flips, the trick behind backward speech effects, certain puzzles and a classic way to proofread a sentence's rhythm. Reverse by line and a list inverts top to bottom — the newest entry becomes the first, the countdown reads upwards, the pasted stack becomes a queue. This tool does all three, because the people who arrive asking for "reverse" are reliably asking for different things.</p>
<p>The serious uses hide among the playful ones. Reversed text is a debugging instrument: reading code output or log order backwards exposes assumptions about sequence; checking a palindrome or an anagram starts here; language learners and puzzle setters work in reversed text constantly; and reading your own prose backwards, word by single word, remains a proofreader's old trick for seeing what is actually written instead of what you meant. The party uses need no defence — mirror-writing challenges, upside-down-text pranks and social posts that make people stop scrolling are legitimate currency.</p>
<p>Your text is flipped on your device and never leaves it — drafts, lists and private messages get their backwards moment in private, exactly as they should.</p></section>
''',
"json-minifier": '''<section class="section"><h2>Whitespace is for humans; payloads travel without it</h2>
<p>Every space, tab and newline in a pretty-printed JSON file is a courtesy to human readers — and a cost to anything that has to store or send it. Minifying strips the lot: the same data, the same structure, one compact line, typically a quarter to a third smaller. That saving is real money and real milliseconds in the places JSON travels at scale — API responses served millions of times, configuration embedded in a page, data packed into a token, a file that must fit a size limit. Paste JSON here, in any state of formatting, and get back the compact form, ready to paste where it is going.</p>
<p>Two properties separate a minifier from a text mangler. It validates as it works: the output is produced by actually parsing your JSON, so invalid input — the trailing comma, the single quotes, the unquoted key — is reported rather than silently passed into a "minified" file that fails later, further from the cause. And it changes nothing but the whitespace: keys, order, values, number formats and strings emerge identical, because the data is the cargo and only the packaging is removed. The round trip is safe — anything minified here can be re-formatted for reading with the JSON Formatter, the same tool wearing its other hat.</p>
<p>The parsing happens on your device. JSON in the wild carries tokens, customer records and internal addresses, and none of that should be pasted into a stranger's server for the sake of smaller whitespace.</p></section>
''',
"csv-to-json": '''<section class="section"><h2>Spreadsheet rows in, structured data out</h2>
<p>CSV is the spreadsheet world's native tongue and the API world's foreign language. The moment spreadsheet data needs to meet a program — a database import, a web app, a script, an API that speaks only JSON — somebody has to translate rows and columns into objects. This tool does it in one paste: your CSV's header row becomes the keys, each following row becomes an object in a JSON array, and the result is ready to copy into code, a request body or a file. The catalogue, the price list, the exported contacts — the everyday exports of business life — make this trip constantly.</p>
<p>The details that decide whether the output is usable: quoting is handled properly, so a field containing a comma or a quote ("Smith, John" or a description full of punctuation) stays one field, as the CSV standard intends — naive splitting on commas is the bug this tool exists to prevent. And one truth to design around: CSV carries no type information, so every value arrives in JSON as a string — "42" the text, not 42 the number. That is not a flaw in the conversion; it is the honest state of the source data, and any system consuming the JSON should cast its numbers and dates deliberately rather than inherit them by assumption.</p>
<p>The conversion runs on your device, with nothing uploaded — the right treatment for exported customer lists, sales data and internal tables, which should be translated where they live.</p></section>
''',
"json-to-csv": '''<section class="section"><h2>API data, opened in Excel where it belongs</h2>
<p>JSON is how programs talk; a spreadsheet is how people look. When data arrives as a JSON array — an API response, a database export, a log distilled into records — the human questions (how many, how much, sort by this, filter to that) all live in Excel or Google Sheets. This tool makes the crossing: paste an array of objects and get back a CSV whose columns are the objects' keys and whose rows are the records, ready to download and open as a table.</p>
<p>The craft is in the escaping, because real data is hostile to grids. Values containing commas, quotes or newlines — descriptions, addresses, notes — are quoted exactly as the CSV standard requires, so a sentence full of punctuation stays inside its cell instead of exploding across six columns. Objects with differing keys are reconciled into one column set, with blanks where a record lacks a field, because real-world JSON is rarely as uniform as its documentation promises. Nested objects and arrays cannot flatten honestly into a cell; they are included as their JSON text, visible and intact, rather than silently dropped.</p>
<p>Everything converts on your device — API responses carrying customer data and internal records are flattened locally and never uploaded. From programs to people, without the data taking a detour through a stranger's server.</p></section>
''',
"markdown-to-html": '''<section class="section"><h2>Write in Markdown, ship in HTML</h2>
<p>Markdown won the writing war by being invisible: a hash for a heading, asterisks for emphasis, brackets for a link — formatting you type at the speed of thought and read without decoding. It is the native format of READMEs, documentation, notes, forums and half the world's drafts. But browsers do not speak it: the web runs on HTML, and sooner or later Markdown must become tags. This tool converts as you write — type Markdown on one side, watch the rendered result in a live preview and lift the clean HTML from the other, ready for a CMS, an email template, a static site or any system that wants markup.</p>
<p>Seeing both views at once teaches the mapping faster than any tutorial: emphasis becomes em, strong becomes strong, a fenced code block becomes pre and code with its contents safely escaped. That escaping is the quiet correctness that matters — angle brackets in your text stay text, they do not become accidental tags — and the headings, lists, links, images, blockquotes and code you actually used emerge as the semantic HTML a browser, a screen reader and a search engine all understand. Where a platform accepts Markdown natively you never needed this page; where it accepts only HTML, this page is the bridge.</p>
<p>Your writing is converted in your browser and sent nowhere. Drafts — unpublished posts, internal docs, the README for the unreleased thing — stay drafts until you decide otherwise.</p></section>
''',
"text-to-speech": '''<section class="section"><h2>Hearing your words is a different kind of reading</h2>
<p>Text read aloud does work that silent reading cannot. It proofreads with a stranger's ears: the missing word, the sentence that runs out of breath, the repeated phrase — all invisible on the page and obvious in the air. It makes text accessible to readers for whom print is a barrier, turns articles into listening for commutes and kitchens, and lets language learners hear the rhythm of what they are studying. Paste text here, pick a voice, set the speed and pitch, and your device reads it to you.</p>
<p>The voices are your device's own — this tool uses the speech voices built into your browser and operating system, which is why the selection differs between a phone, a Windows PC and a Mac, and why the best voice on the list is worth a minute's exploring: modern systems ship with several, including natural voices in many languages and accents, and quality varies enormously between them. Speed is the control with the most range — slow for proofreading and language study, brisk for skimming familiar material — and a long text can be consumed in sittings, because ears, like eyes, have a working day.</p>
<p>Because the speech is generated locally by your own device, your text is never uploaded to a synthesis server. The manuscript, the private letter, the internal document get read aloud in the room you are in, and nowhere else.</p></section>
''',
"morse-code-translator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Dots, dashes, and the original digital language</h2>
<p>Before text messages there were dots and dashes. Morse code — developed in the 1840s for the telegraph and standardised internationally a generation later — encodes every letter and digit as a short pattern of signals, and it ran the world's communications for a century: shipping, railways, wars and weddings all travelled as beeps. This translator works in both directions, text to Morse and Morse back to text, and it plays the code aloud as real beeps, because Morse was always a heard language first and a written one second — operators recognised whole words by rhythm, the way you recognise a voice.</p>
<p>The system's elegance is in its timing. A dot is one unit, a dash is three, the gap inside a letter is one, between letters three, between words seven — a rhythm anyone can learn to feel. Frequency shaped the code itself: the commonest letters got the shortest patterns (E is a single dot, T a single dash), an optimisation designed in the age of the printing press's type cases. And its most famous sequence needs no introduction: ··· −−− ···, SOS, chosen not as initials but because it is unmistakable — three, three, three, a pattern no natural message produces by accident.</p>
<p>Everything translates and plays on your device, nothing recorded. Amateur radio operators, scouts, puzzle hunters and the simply curious are all welcome; the code has outlived every network built to replace it, and it is not done yet.</p></section>
''',
"character-counter": '''<section class="section"><h2>Every platform has a limit; this page knows where yours ends</h2>
<p>Character limits are the invisible walls of the internet: the post that stops at 280, the headline field that truncates, the bio that takes 150 and not one more, the meta description with about 160 characters to win a click from a search page, the SMS that becomes two messages — and two charges — at the 161st character. This counter tracks your text in real time: characters with spaces, characters without, words, sentences, and — the number that matters — how much room is left before the wall of whichever limit you are writing against.</p>
<p>The two character counts serve different masters and both catch people out. With spaces is the count most platforms enforce, because it is the count of what is actually transmitted. Without spaces is the translator's and typesetter's measure, the basis of per-character pricing for localisation and the honest size of the content itself. The SMS trap deserves its fame: a message typed with a single smart quote, emoji or non-Latin character switches the whole message to Unicode encoding, and the per-segment budget collapses from 160 characters to 70 — which is why one emoji can triple the cost of a text, a fact this counter makes visible before sending rather than after billing.</p>
<p>Your text is counted on your device as you type and never leaves it — drafts, posts and private messages measured in private, as they should be.</p></section>
''',
"reading-time-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>How long is this, really — in minutes, not words</h2>
<p>"2,000 words" means little to a reader deciding whether to start; "about 10 minutes" is a promise they can plan around. This calculator converts any text into time: how long it takes to read — at slow, average and fast paces — and how long it takes to speak aloud, which is the number a different set of people desperately needs. Paste the text and the estimate is instant, because the conversion is a well-measured human constant: adults read silently at roughly 200–250 words a minute, and speak at a slower, steadier 130–150.</p>
<p>The speaking time is the one with professional stakes. A 900-word speech is not "about five minutes" — it is six to seven, and the speaker who learns that from a calculator rather than from a restless audience learns it kindly. Podcast scripts, video narration, wedding speeches, conference talks, sermons, lessons: all are written in words and delivered in minutes, and the gap between the written estimate and the spoken reality is where overruns live. Reading time earns its keep in the other direction — the article labelled honestly at seven minutes gets finished; the one disguised as a quick read and discovered to be a marathon gets abandoned, and the reader remembers who mislabelled it.</p>
<p>Your text is timed on your device and never uploaded — speeches, scripts and manuscripts are measured in private, ahead of the audience rather than in front of a server.</p></section>
''',
"unit-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>Two systems, one world, and the cost of confusing them</h2>
<p>The world measures in two dialects — metric and imperial — and the border between them runs through kitchens, workshops, gyms and airports rather than along any map. This converter translates across four everyday categories — length, weight, temperature and distance — in both directions, instantly. Its conversions are built on definitions, not approximations: since 1959 the inch has been exactly 2.54 centimetres by international agreement, the pound exactly 0.45359237 kilograms, the mile exactly 1,609.344 metres — so a conversion done here is not an estimate but an identity, the same value any standards laboratory on earth would give you.</p>
<p>Precision has limits worth knowing, and temperature is the famous one: it is the only common conversion with an offset as well as a scale, which is why °C to °F is a formula rather than a ratio, and why doubling a Celsius temperature does not double the Fahrenheit one. Unit mix-ups are also the rare comedy with real casualties — aircraft have run out of fuel and a Mars orbiter was lost to pound-versus-newton confusion — which is the serious argument beneath the everyday one: convert deliberately, with the units named, whenever money, safety or machinery is attached to the number.</p>
<p>For the conversions people make daily, the dedicated pages alongside go deeper — kilograms to pounds, centimetres to inches, the temperature pair, each with its own table and worked examples. This page is the generalist: every unit in those four categories, both directions, computed on your device with nothing stored.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Worked examples: one from each category</h2>
<p>Four conversions, one per category the tool covers — each checked against the exact definitions the converter uses (1 inch = 2.54 cm, 1 lb = 0.45359237 kg, 1 mile = 1,609.344 m):</p>
<table>
<tr><td><strong>Category</strong></td><td><strong>Conversion</strong></td><td><strong>Result</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Length</td><td>10 cm → inches</td><td>3.94 inches</td></tr>
<tr><td>Length</td><td>5 feet 7 inches → cm</td><td>170.18 cm (67 inches × 2.54)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight</td><td>70 kg → pounds</td><td>154.32 lb</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight</td><td>150 lb → kg</td><td>68.04 kg</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temperature</td><td>20°C → °F</td><td>68°F</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temperature</td><td>37°C → °F</td><td>98.6°F (body temperature)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Distance</td><td>10 km → miles</td><td>6.21 miles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Distance</td><td>26.2 miles (a marathon) → km</td><td>42.16 km</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Three habits keep these conversions honest. First, temperature is the only category with an offset as well as a scale — doubling a Celsius figure does not double the Fahrenheit one, so use the formula (°F = °C × 9/5 + 32) rather than a ratio. Second, heights are two numbers, not one: convert feet and inches to total inches first (5 feet 7 inches is 67 inches), then convert the inches — reading 5′7″ as 5.7 feet is the classic error. Third, round only at the end: the converter keeps full precision internally, and the table above shows two decimals, which is plenty for luggage, recipes and road signs.</p></section>
''',
"tip-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Tipping is arithmetic wrapped in etiquette</h2>
<p>The tip is the only price in daily life the customer sets after the fact, and the calculation arrives at the worst possible moment: the end of a meal, in company, with mental arithmetic as the entertainment. This calculator ends the performance — enter the bill, choose the percentage, split it between however many people are paying, and read the tip, the total and each person's share, exact to the cent. No rounding drift, no one quietly overpaying because the division got awkward.</p>
<p>The etiquette underneath varies and the page should be honest about it. In the United States, 15–20% of the bill is customary at sit-down restaurants, with more for excellent service — the convention exists because service wages are structured around it. In much of Europe a service charge is often already inside the bill and a small addition is a courtesy, not an obligation; in Japan tipping can be refused as politely confusing. Inside the US system, two refinements matter: the percentage is traditionally figured on the pre-tax subtotal, and a large group's automatic gratuity, when already added, should not be tipped on top of itself by reflex — check the bill the calculator is helping you split.</p>
<p>The maths runs on your device; the judgement — what this service, in this place, deserves — remains entirely, properly yours.</p></section>
''',
"calorie-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Your body has a base rate, and everything else is activity</h2>
<p>Every calorie question splits in two, and this calculator answers both halves in order. The first is your BMR — basal metabolic rate — the energy your body spends in a day at complete rest, keeping you warm, breathing and alive. It is estimated here with the Mifflin–St Jeor equation, the formula dietitians currently trust most, built from your age, sex, height and weight: bodies are furnaces of different sizes, and BMR is the size of yours. The second figure, TDEE, multiplies that base by your activity level — from sedentary desk life to heavy daily training — to estimate the calories that maintain your weight as you actually live it.</p>
<p>From the maintenance figure, the strategy people actually use follows: eat modestly below it to lose weight, modestly above to gain, and treat the traditional 500-calorie daily adjustment — roughly half a kilogram a week — as the outer edge of sensible rather than a starting point. The honesty that matters: these are population-level estimates, and individual metabolism varies by a real margin either side of any formula — the number is a well-founded starting hypothesis, and the scale over three honest weeks is the experiment that corrects it.</p>
<p>Your figures are computed in your browser and stored nowhere. And a boundary worth stating once: this is general information for healthy adults, not medical advice — pregnancy, illness, eating-disorder history or serious training goals deserve a professional who knows the person, not a formula that knows the average.</p></section>
''',
"grade-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>The question behind every grade: what do I need on the final?</h2>
<p>Most courses are not one grade but a weighted mixture — homework worth a tenth, quizzes a fifth, a midterm a quarter, a final carrying the rest — and the weighted average is unintuitive in a way that makes students misjudge their position all term. This calculator does the honest arithmetic two ways. It computes where you stand now: each category's average multiplied by its weight, summed into the grade you currently hold. And it answers the question students actually lose sleep over — given the target and the weight of what remains, exactly what score on the final exam lands the grade?</p>
<p>That second calculation is algebra with a temperament worth understanding. When the final carries a heavy weight, the required score can come out startlingly achievable — or mathematically impossible, a number above 100 that is the calculator's blunt way of saying the target cannot be reached through this exam alone, better known in week twelve than in the exam hall. When it comes out low, it is permission to distribute effort to the other courses competing for the same week. Either way, the number replaces dread with a fact, and facts can be planned around.</p>
<p>Your grades are computed on your device and recorded nowhere — a student's standing is private business. Use the answer early enough for it to be a strategy rather than an epitaph.</p></section>
''',
"binary-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>Counting in twos: the only language computers speak natively</h2>
<p>Every number in every computer is stored in binary — base two — because a switch has two states and a billion switches are still just switches. This converter translates between binary, decimal and hexadecimal, and it shows the step-by-step working rather than just the answer, because binary is one of those topics that is opaque until the place values click and trivial forever after: where decimal columns are worth 1, 10, 100, binary columns are worth 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 — doubling each step — and the number 1101 is simply 8 + 4 + 0 + 1 = 13, read off the columns that hold a 1.</p>
<p>The working matters because binary is a working tool, not a museum piece. Programmers meet it in bitmasks and permissions (the Unix 111101101 that chmod renders as 755), in network addresses and subnet masks, in the RGB colour channels that are each eight bits wide, and in every "why is the limit 255 / 65,535 / 2,147,483,647" question ever asked — those odd ceilings are binary numbers wearing decimal clothes: 2⁸−1, 2¹⁶−1, 2³¹−1. Seeing a decimal number's binary form, and watching it built column by column, is how those limits stop being trivia and become arithmetic you can predict.</p>
<p>Conversions are computed on your device, instantly, in both directions. Learn it here once and every system limit you ever meet reads like a signpost instead of a riddle.</p></section>
''',
"hex-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>Sixteen digits: where programmers and colours meet</h2>
<p>Hexadecimal is base sixteen — digits 0–9 plus letters A–F standing for ten through fifteen — and it exists for one elegant reason: sixteen is two to the fourth power, so exactly four binary digits make one hex digit, making hex the compact human handwriting for binary data. A byte, eight bits, is always exactly two hex digits, from 00 to FF. This converter moves between hex, decimal and binary in every direction, so the same value can be read in whichever base the job speaks.</p>
<p>Its two great workplaces reward fluency in both. In computing, hex is the language of memory addresses, error codes, machine-level data and network hardware — your router's MAC address is six hex bytes wearing colons. In design, the same notation paints the web: a colour like #FF5733 is three hex bytes, one per channel — red at FF (255, full), green at 57 (87), blue at 33 (51) — and this converter shows the RGB breakdown alongside, bridging the programmer's base and the designer's palette. Two habits make hex effortless: learn the landmarks (00 is nothing, 80 is exactly half, FF is everything), and remember that hex counts in sixteens, so 20 is thirty-two and 99 is one hundred and fifty-three.</p>
<p>Values are converted on your device, both ways, with the workings available. However the number arrived — an address, an error, a colour — it leaves readable.</p></section>
''',
"salary-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>An hourly wage and a salary are the same money in different clothes</h2>
<p>Job offers arrive in whichever unit makes them hardest to compare: one quoted hourly, one as an annual salary, a third as a monthly figure — and the comparison everyone actually lives by, what lands in a month of ordinary life, is buried three conversions deep. This converter translates between hourly, daily, weekly, monthly and annual pay in both directions, built on the standard full-time skeleton: 40 hours a week, 52 weeks a year, 2,080 working hours — the arithmetic employers themselves use, so an hourly rate of 25 becomes the 52,000 salary it truly is, and a 60,000 salary reveals itself as 28.85 an hour.</p>
<p>The honest caveats are where the value hides. The figures are gross — before tax, pension and every deduction — and the gap between gross and take-home is its own separate calculation that varies by country and circumstance. Hours are the other trap: a salary divided by a nominal 40-hour week is a fiction for anyone whose role actually runs at 50, and converting a salary back to an hourly rate at your real hours is sometimes an uncomfortable, clarifying exercise. Part-time and contract work need the hours adjusted to match — the converter takes your actual weekly hours as the basis, because a conversion is only honest when its assumptions are yours.</p>
<p>Your pay figures are computed on your device and stored nowhere. What you earn is precisely the kind of number that should never need to visit a server to be understood.</p></section>
''',
"vat-calculator": '''<section class="section"><h2>Adding VAT is easy; removing it is where the money leaks</h2>
<p>VAT — value added tax, called GST in several countries — sits inside most prices you see, and working with it correctly is a daily business skill: pricing a product net and quoting it gross, or taking a receipt's total and recovering the net amount and the tax portion for your accounts. This calculator works both directions at any rate — the common ones are preset (the UK at 20%, EU member states between 17% and 27%, many others at 5–15%) and a custom rate covers everywhere else — showing the net, the tax and the gross as three clear figures.</p>
<p>The removal direction contains the expensive trap, and it catches real businesses: to strip 20% VAT out of a gross price you divide by 1.20 — you do not subtract 20%. Subtracting 20% from £120 gives £96; the true net is £100, because the 20% was calculated on the net, not on the gross. Four pounds of phantom discount per hundred, repeated across an invoice book, is how VAT returns go wrong. The calculator exists largely to make that error impossible — and to make the tax element itself visible, since the VAT inside a pile of receipts is money a registered business reclaims, and knowing its exact sum is the difference between a return filed from figures and one filed from guesses.</p>
<p>Amounts are computed on your device and never stored. Your turnover, your receipts and your tax position are your own — the calculator just keeps the arithmetic honest.</p></section>
''',
"color-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>One colour, three languages — and when to speak each</h2>
<p>Every screen colour is one fact expressible three ways, and this converter moves between them live: HEX, the six-digit code of web design (#1A73E8); RGB, the raw recipe of light — so much red, green and blue, each from 0 to 255; and HSL, the human arrangement — hue as an angle around the colour wheel, saturation as intensity, lightness as brightness. Type or pick a colour (the eyedropper at the top of this page lifts one from anywhere on your screen) and all three update together beside a preview swatch, because converting colour is never an end in itself — it is always on the way to using the colour somewhere that speaks a different dialect.</p>
<p>Knowing which dialect a job wants saves real fumbling. CSS and design tools trade in HEX for finished colours, but reach for HSL the moment a colour must be varied: a hover state is the same hue with lightness raised, a muted version is saturation lowered — adjustments that are guesswork in RGB and one slider in HSL. RGB is where screens are programmed and where a colour is specified channel by channel. And a professional caution that saves arguments: the same values do not guarantee the same appearance across a cheap monitor and a calibrated one — the numbers are exact, the displays are not.</p>
<p>Picked and converted colours live only in your page; brand palettes and client work are worked on locally and go nowhere.</p></section>
''',
"roman-numeral-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>The number system with no zero and a two-thousand-year career</h2>
<p>Roman numerals are addition wearing a toga: I, V, X, L, C, D, M stand for 1, 5, 10, 50, 100, 500 and 1,000, and a number is built by stacking them — mostly adding left to right, with the famous subtractive twist that a smaller numeral before a larger one subtracts instead (IV is 4, IX is 9, CM is 900). This converter works in both directions across the classical range 1 to 3,999: type a number and read its numerals, or paste numerals and get the number, with the rules applied strictly so malformed sequences are caught rather than flattered.</p>
<p>The range limit is itself a history lesson: classical Latin had no zero and no symbol beyond M, so 3,999 (MMMCMXCIX) is where the standard system honestly runs out — later conventions drew a bar over a numeral to multiply it by a thousand, but that is an extension, not the original game. What survives everywhere is the usage: film and book copyright years (the BBC's closing credits made MCMXCIX briefly famous), monument and foundation inscriptions, clock faces — which traditionally write 4 as IIII, a clockmaker's exception that has outlived every attempt to correct it — regnal numbers, outlines, and the Super Bowl, whose numbering is now more famous than the system that produced it.</p>
<p>Conversions are computed on your device. Two thousand years on, the numerals are still legible; this page just saves the counting on your fingers.</p></section>
''',
"number-to-words-converter": '''<section class="section"><h2>Why cheques make you write the number twice</h2>
<p>On a cheque, a contract or an invoice, the amount appears twice — once in digits and once in words — and the redundancy is the security system: digits are easy to alter (a 1 grows a tail into a 7, a decimal point wanders), while "four thousand five hundred and sixty-two" resists tampering because rewriting it invisibly is nearly impossible. This converter writes any number out in full English words, correctly hyphenated and grouped — thousands, millions, billions — so the written line on a legal or financial document is exact, rather than improvised under pressure at a bank counter.</p>
<p>The conventions are worth the converter's care. Compound numbers under a hundred are hyphenated (forty-seven, never forty seven, and forty is the spelling that eats a u and traps everyone); "and" belongs after the hundred in British usage (one hundred and five) and is omitted in American; large amounts group in threes with the scale named each time. Getting these right matters because the written amount is the one that prevails when the two disagree on a cheque — banks read the words as authoritative — which makes a spelling error in the words the most expensive typo in everyday paperwork.</p>
<p>Your figures are converted on your device and stored nowhere — the amounts on your documents are your business, in every sense. Write the words this page gives you, and the digits and the prose will agree.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Worked examples: writing money in words</h2>
<p>Cheques and invoices are where this converter earns its keep, and money is written in words the same way in every currency — the number first, the currency word after it. These are the exact words the tool above gives for each amount (American style, no "and" after the hundred):</p>
<table>
<tr><td><strong>Amount</strong></td><td><strong>In words</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>$1,250</td><td>One thousand two hundred fifty dollars</td></tr>
<tr><td>£2,500</td><td>Two thousand five hundred pounds</td></tr>
<tr><td>₹75,000</td><td>Seventy-five thousand rupees</td></tr>
<tr><td>₹1,25,000</td><td>One lakh twenty-five thousand rupees (Indian system)</td></tr>
<tr><td>₹75,00,000</td><td>Seventy-five lakh rupees (Indian system)</td></tr>
<tr><td>$99,999</td><td>Ninety-nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine dollars</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two things to get right. First, the Indian-system rows only read that way with the Indian system selected above: ₹1,25,000 in the international system is "one hundred twenty-five thousand rupees", and ₹75,00,000 is "seven million five hundred thousand rupees" — same money, different words, and a cheque written in the wrong system's words can be returned. Second, this tool takes whole numbers only (0 to 999,999,999,999), so an amount with cents or paise is converted in two parts: for $1,234.56, convert 1234 for "one thousand two hundred thirty-four dollars" and 56 for "fifty-six cents", then write them together — "one thousand two hundred thirty-four dollars and fifty-six cents".</p></section>
''',
"celsius-to-fahrenheit": '''<section class="section"><h2>For the metric traveller entering Fahrenheit country</h2>
<p>Almost the whole world runs on Celsius; the United States — and its weather forecasts, ovens and thermostats — runs on Fahrenheit, and the traveller between them needs the conversion in this direction: the forecast says 77, the recipe says 350, the hotel thermostat says 68, and each needs translating into the scale your instincts understand. The formula is °F = °C × 9⁄5 + 32, applied exactly here in both directions — but the anchors are what make you fluent: water freezes at 0°C = 32°F, boils at 100°C = 212°F, the body runs at 37°C = 98.6°F, and at −40 the two scales meet, the one temperature that needs no converting.</p>
<p>The kitchen is where this direction earns its keep. An American recipe's 350°F is 177°C — gas mark 4, a moderate oven — and the full ladder (325°F ≈ 165°C, 400°F ≈ 200°C, 450°F ≈ 230°C) is worth having in front of you rather than derived mid-recipe with flour on your hands. Weather intuition converts more loosely and this page's table covers it: 20°C is a pleasant 68°F room-temperature day, 30°C is a properly hot 86°F, and a forecast of 100°F is 38°C — stay inside. Precision here is free; second-guessing a converted oven temperature is how cakes die.</p>
<p>The dedicated reverse converter, Fahrenheit to Celsius, serves the opposite journey. Everything computes on your device — the only thing being converted is the weather, not your data.</p></section>
''',
"fahrenheit-to-celsius": '''<section class="section"><h2>Translating American weather into the world's scale</h2>
<p>The United States reports its world in Fahrenheit — its forecasts, its ovens, its fevers — and the rest of the planet, plus all of science, reads Celsius. If your instincts are metric, an American forecast is a foreign language with familiar-looking numbers, which is the dangerous kind: 90 sounds mild and is a fierce 32°C; 32 sounds like a warm day in Celsius terms and is literally freezing. This converter applies the exact formula, °C = (°F − 32) × 5⁄9, in both directions, and its table builds the instinct: 32°F = 0°C where water freezes, 212°F = 100°C where it boils, 98.6°F = 37°C where you run, and −40 is −40 — the scales' one meeting point.</p>
<p>Two domains dominate this direction. Weather, where the feel of the numbers matters more than their precision: 50°F is a jacketed 10°C, 68°F a perfect 20°C, 86°F a hot 30°C, and a fever of 101°F is a concerning 38.3°C — the medical conversion being the one to get right at 3 a.m. with a thermometer in hand. And cooking, travelling the other way into metric kitchens: the American recipe's 350°F is 175–177°C, gas mark 4. For the metric-to-American journey, the twin Celsius to Fahrenheit page is the specialist; this one exists for the American number in front of a metric reader.</p>
<p>Conversions run on your device and nothing is stored. The temperature gets translated; nothing else travels.</p></section>
''',
"kg-to-lbs": '''<section class="section"><h2>Kilograms, translated for the pound-speaking world</h2>
<p>The kilogram is the world's unit of mass; the pound is the unit of the United States, of British bathroom scales and of most gym small talk — and the traffic between them is constant: body weight for an American form, luggage against an airline limit quoted in pounds, a baby's birth weight announced to family in two countries at once. The conversion is a definition, not an estimate: one kilogram is exactly 2.20462 pounds (the pound itself is defined as 0.45359237 kg), and this page applies it exactly, in both directions.</p>
<p>The numbers people actually convert have landmarks worth memorising. A 20 kg gym barbell is 44 lbs, and the standard plates stack in both dialects — 60 kg is 132 lbs, 100 kg is 220. Luggage: the common 23 kg checked-bag allowance is a whisker over 50 lbs, which is why airlines quote the two side by side and why a bag at 23.4 kg is an argument at check-in. Body weight: 70 kg is 154 lbs, 80 kg is 176, 90 kg is 198 — the figures that turn a metric medical record into an American insurance form without a calculator's hesitation. For ounces, stones and the full unit zoo, the general Unit Converter alongside handles them; this page is the specialist for the one conversion half the planet makes weekly.</p>
<p>Computed on your device, stored nowhere — your weight, in either dialect, is your own affair.</p></section>
''',
"lbs-to-kg": '''<section class="section"><h2>Pounds into the unit the rest of the world weighs in</h2>
<p>America weighs itself in pounds; medicine, science and most other countries weigh in kilograms — and the records constantly cross: the US medical chart that must become a metric dose calculation, the American product specification headed for an international buyer, the wrestler or athlete whose class is set in kilos, the parcel priced by a carrier that thinks in kg. The conversion rests on an exact definition: one pound is 0.45359237 kilograms — since 1959, by international agreement, precisely that — and this page applies it exactly, in both directions.</p>
<p>Direction matters because the errors reverse too. Going this way, the landmark figures are the American ones: 100 lbs is 45.4 kg, 150 lbs is 68 kg, 200 lbs is 90.7 kg — and the shipping threshold everyone meets, 50 lbs, is 22.7 kg, sitting just under the 23 kg allowance airlines publish, because the two systems' round numbers quietly interlock. The dosage direction deserves its standing warning: drug doses are prescribed per kilogram worldwide, so a weight recorded in pounds that is fed into a per-kilo formula unconverted is a tenfold-class error — the existence of dedicated converters for exactly this pair of units is not an accident of search traffic but of safety.</p>
<p>Weights are converted on your device and nothing is recorded. Kilograms out, confidence intact — in this direction, accuracy is not pedantry.</p></section>
''',
"cm-to-inches": '''<section class="section"><h2>Centimetres into the inches that products are sold by</h2>
<p>The inch is exactly 2.54 centimetres — defined so in 1959, no approximation anywhere in this page — yet the conversion stays stubbornly necessary because the world's goods are specified in both dialects: screens and tyres and waistbands in inches, bodies and rooms and most countries' paperwork in centimetres. This converter works both ways and shows the result the way people actually use it: not only decimal inches but the feet-and-inches breakdown for heights, because 170 cm is a number while 5 feet 7 inches is a person.</p>
<p>Height is the conversion that dominates: 160 cm is 5′3″, 170 is 5′7″, 180 is 5′11″ and 190 is 6′3″ — the figures that populate dating profiles, medical forms, clothing charts and doorframe pencil marks across two measurement cultures. The second great use is screens, sold by the diagonal inch everywhere on earth: a "55-inch" television is 140 cm corner to corner, and converting your wall's centimetres this way is how you learn whether it fits before it arrives. Fractions are the honest remainder: rulers and tape measures speak in eighths and sixteenths of an inch, so the decimal this page gives (say 4.72″) is read on a tape as the nearest mark — a hair over 4¾″ — which for woodwork is the difference between a conversion and a measurement.</p>
<p>Everything computes on your device. Your height, in whichever dialect the form demands, stays yours.</p></section>
''',
"inches-to-cm": '''<section class="section"><h2>The American measurement, made legible to the metric world</h2>
<p>Products designed in the United States arrive with their dimensions in inches — the laptop that is 13.3 across, the photo printed at 8×10, the lumber sold as a two-by-four, the baby announced at 21 inches long — and the metric majority of humanity needs those numbers in centimetres before they mean anything. The conversion is exact by definition: one inch is 2.54 centimetres, agreed internationally in 1959, and this page multiplies it precisely, in both directions — because a result you will cut, buy or build from should never rest on a remembered approximation like "about two and a half".</p>
<p>This direction has its own landmark cases. The screen diagonal: a 13-inch laptop is 33 cm, a 27-inch monitor 68.6 cm, a 65-inch television 165 cm corner to corner — the figures that decide furniture and wall mounts. Photographs: the classic 4×6 print is 10×15 cm, 8×10 is 20×25. Heights reverse the usual flow too — an American form's 5′10″ is 178 cm. And fractions are the live edge of this conversion: American measurements arrive as ¾ and ⅞, and the honest centimetre values (1.9 cm, 2.2 cm) are what a metric tape or a machine shop works from — precision here is a courtesy to the person holding the saw.</p>
<p>Conversions are computed on your device and stored nowhere. Inches in, centimetres out, exactly — the way standards are supposed to behave.</p></section>
''',
"mm-to-inches": '''<section class="section"><h2>Millimetres are the workshop's unit; inches are the catalogue's</h2>
<p>Millimetres are how the metric world measures small things — screws, drill bits, jewellery, phone screens, the thickness of a sheet. Inches are how the American catalogue sells them. The two meet constantly in online shopping: a 25 mm lens filter, a 6 mm drill bit, a watch case 40 mm across — and the listing, the review or the tutorial quotes inches. The conversion is exact by definition, because the inch is defined as 25.4 millimetres: divide by 25.4 and you have the inches, with no approximation hiding anywhere in the maths.</p>
<p>The landmarks are worth learning because they recur. 25 mm is just under an inch (0.98 in) — the classic near-miss that catches people ordering parts. 50 mm is 1.97 in, 100 mm is 3.94 in, 150 mm is 5.91 in, and 300 mm is 11.81 in — just under a foot. In the other direction, the numbers people quote in conversation — a 1-inch pipe, a 2-inch screen bezel — are 25.4 mm and 50.8 mm, the figures a metric workshop actually works from.</p>
<p>Where precision matters, use the exact figure and not the folk one. "About 25 mm to the inch" is fine for estimating a shelf, but a 25 mm hole drilled at 1 inch is 0.4 mm oversize — enough for a bearing to rattle. This converter divides by exactly 25.4 in both directions, so the number you cut or order from is the true one. For longer lengths, the <a href="/cm-to-inches/">cm to inches</a> converter covers the same ground at centimetre scale, and the <a href="/unit-converter/">Unit Converter</a> handles everything else.</p></section>
''',
"inches-to-mm": '''<section class="section"><h2>The American inch, measured in the world's millimetres</h2>
<p>American products arrive with their dimensions in inches — the screen that is 6.1 across, the pipe that is ¾, the bolt that is ⅜ — and the metric majority of the world needs those numbers in millimetres before they mean anything in a workshop. The conversion is exact: one inch is 25.4 millimetres, by international definition since 1959, so multiplying by 25.4 is not an estimate but the definition itself. A 6-inch screen is 152.4 mm; a 12-inch ruler is 304.8 mm; a 24-inch monitor is 609.6 mm.</p>
<p>Fractions are where this conversion earns its keep. American hardware is sold in fractions — ¼, ⅜, ½, ¾ — and each has a clean millimetre value: 6.35, 9.53, 12.7 and 19.05 mm. A spanner marked ⅜ fits a 9.5 mm nut near enough to be dangerous and wrong enough to round it off, which is why the exact figures matter more than the rounded ones. The table above gives the whole numbers; the converter gives any value, fractions included, to the tenth of a millimetre.</p>
<p>The same inch feeds the other pair converters on this site. A height in feet and inches becomes centimetres through the <a href="/inches-to-cm/">inches to cm</a> converter, a length in feet through <a href="/feet-to-meters/">feet to metres</a>, and the <a href="/unit-converter/">Unit Converter</a> covers the rest. Millimetres are simply the finest scale of the same system — the one machinists, jewellers and 3D-printer owners live in.</p></section>
''',
"meters-to-feet": '''<section class="section"><h2>Metres are the map; feet are the room</h2>
<p>Metres measure the world outside — the race is 100 metres, the building is 30 metres tall, the room is 5 metres long — and feet measure the room you are standing in, at least if you are standing in America. One metre is 3.28084 feet, and the conversion is a division away from exact: the foot is defined as 0.3048 metres, so the factor is 1 ÷ 0.3048, a number with no rounding in it until you choose how many decimals to show.</p>
<p>Heights are the conversion people actually make. 1.8 metres is 5.91 feet — 5 foot 11 in the way people say it — and 1.75 metres is 5 foot 9. The table above covers the landmarks: 2 metres is 6.56 feet, 5 metres is 16.4 feet, 10 metres is 32.81 feet. Distances follow the same rule, which is why a "10-metre" diving board is a 32.8-foot drop and a 100-metre sprint is 328 feet of running — the same race, two different announcements.</p>
<p>Feet alone rarely tell the whole story: a height of 5.91 feet is usually wanted as 5 feet 11 inches, not as a decimal. Take the whole feet, then multiply the leftover decimal by 12 — 0.91 × 12 is 10.9 inches. For the reverse direction, the <a href="/feet-to-meters/">feet to metres</a> converter does the same job backwards, and the <a href="/cm-to-inches/">cm to inches</a> page covers the finer scale.</p></section>
''',
"feet-to-meters": '''<section class="section"><h2>Feet into metres: the height conversion, done properly</h2>
<p>Feet are how the English-speaking world still measures people, rooms and ceilings — 6 feet tall, a 9-foot ceiling, a 100-foot drop — and metres are how the rest of the world reads those same numbers. One foot is exactly 0.3048 metres, defined internationally, so multiplying by 0.3048 is exact: 6 feet is 1.8288 metres, 10 feet is 3.048 metres, 100 feet is 30.48 metres. The only rounding is in how many decimals you show.</p>
<p>Heights are the case that trips people up, because a height is almost never a whole number of feet. Five foot eleven is not 5.11 feet — it is 5 feet plus 11 inches, which is 71 inches, which is 1.8034 metres. The decimal-feet mistake (reading 5′11″ as 5.11 ft) is the most common conversion error in this pair, and it puts a person 8 centimetres shorter than they are. Convert inches to feet properly first — 11 inches is 0.9167 feet, so 5′11″ is 5.9167 feet — and the metres come out right.</p>
<p>For the other direction, the <a href="/meters-to-feet/">metres to feet</a> converter reverses the same maths, and the <a href="/inches-to-cm/">inches to cm</a> page covers the finer scale. The <a href="/unit-converter/">Unit Converter</a> gathers every pair in one place — but for a height on a form, one pair, done exactly, is the tool that earns its bookmark.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-chinese-new-year": '''<section class="section"><h2>The festival that moves with the moon</h2>
<p>Chinese New Year — the Spring Festival — is the largest annual human migration on Earth, and its date is set by the moon, not by a fixed calendar square. It falls on the first day of the first lunar month: the second new moon after the winter solstice, which lands somewhere between 21 January and 20 February every year. That is why the date wanders — 17 February in 2026, 6 February in 2027, 26 January in 2028 — while the season it opens stays fixed: the turn from winter toward spring.</p>
<p>The celebration runs fifteen days, from New Year's Eve — the reunion dinner, when families travel home whatever the distance — through to the Lantern Festival on the fifteenth day. In between come the red envelopes for children, the fireworks, the lion dances, and the year's animal: 2027 is the Year of the Goat, following 2026's Year of the Horse. The animals cycle every twelve years, so the animal you were born under returns on your twelfth, twenty-fourth and every twelfth birthday after.</p>
<p>The date on this page follows the Hong Kong Observatory's published lunar tables, the same tables governments and calendars use. One honest wrinkle: the new moon is an instant, and the calendar day it falls on can differ by a day on the far side of the world from China — the dates here are the Chinese calendar dates. If you are counting down to a different festival, the <a href="/days-until/">Days Until Any Date</a> builder counts to any date you choose.</p></section>
''',
"days-until-summer": '''<section class="section"><h2>The longest day is an astronomical fact, not a feeling</h2>
<p>The first day of summer is not a date someone chose — it is a moment in the Earth's orbit. At the June solstice the North Pole tilts furthest toward the sun, the sun reaches its highest point in the northern sky, and the Northern Hemisphere gets its longest day of the year: about sixteen hours of daylight in London, nearly nineteen in Edinburgh, and twenty-four hours of sun above the horizon inside the Arctic Circle. The countdown above ticks toward that exact instant, not just toward the date on the calendar.</p>
<p>The solstice falls on 20 or 21 June, and which day it is depends on where you stand: the instant is the same everywhere, but the calendar date it lands on shifts with time zone. In 2028 and 2029 the solstice falls on the evening of 20 June in the Americas while it is already 21 June in Europe and Asia — the table above lists the UTC time so the difference is visible rather than hidden. Meteorologists, for their part, keep it simple and call June, July and August summer regardless; the solstice is the astronomical start, and the two definitions disagree by three weeks.</p>
<p>People have marked this day for thousands of years — sunrise over the Heel Stone at Stonehenge still draws a crowd every June — and modern summer still runs on it: school terms end, festivals begin, and the long evenings arrive. The southern half of the planet gets the mirror image: the June solstice is the first day of winter there, and its summer begins at the December solstice instead.</p></section>
''',
"add-text-to-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>Typing on a PDF without retyping the PDF</h2>
<p>Most PDFs arrive finished — a form, a contract, a certificate — and the job is to add a few words to a page that already exists: a name on a form, "APPROVED" across a draft, a date and a signature line on a letter. Rebuilding the whole document for a dozen words is the wrong job; adding the words to the finished page is the right one. This tool does exactly that: open the PDF, click where the text should start, type it, and download a new copy with the words stamped onto the page.</p>
<p>Two honest limits are worth knowing before you rely on the result. First, the added text is a new overlay in Helvetica — it is not the document's original font, and it cannot be, because the fonts embedded in a PDF are usually subsetted and cannot grow new characters. For a form field, a stamp or a note that is fine; for matching a document's body text exactly it is not the tool. Second, the whiteout option covers old text with a white rectangle and writes over it — it hides the words underneath, it does not delete them from the file. A careful reader with a PDF editor can still find what was covered, so whiteout is for tidiness, not for redaction: truly sensitive text should be removed with a proper redaction tool.</p>
<p>Everything happens in your browser: the PDF is opened with the same PDF.js engine Firefox uses, the text is stamped with pdf-lib, and the file never leaves your device — which matters, because the documents people most need to annotate are usually the ones they least want on a stranger's server. The original file is never changed; the download is a new copy. For a signature rather than typed text, <a href="/sign-pdf/">Sign PDF</a> stamps a drawn or typed signature instead, and for a claim across every page at once, <a href="/add-watermark-to-pdf/">Add Watermark to PDF</a> is the companion tool.</p></section>
''',
"add-watermark-to-pdf": '''<section class="section"><h2>A watermark is a claim, not a decoration</h2>
<p>A watermark says, on every page at once, what a document is: DRAFT, CONFIDENTIAL, SAMPLE, PAID — or whose it is. It is the cheapest honest signal a document can carry, and it is also the most misused: a watermark set too dark ruins the page it was meant to protect, and one set too faint is invisible to the person it was meant to warn. The settings on this page exist to get that balance right — diagonal across the page at 30% grey is the classic for a reason: visible to anyone reading, invisible to anyone actually using the words.</p>
<p>The diagonal angle does real work. A horizontal watermark sits on one line of text and can be read around; a diagonal one crosses every line on the page, so the document cannot be cropped or copied into a clean version without the mark travelling with it. The opacity is the other half of the trick: 20–30% is the working range for "seen but not in the way", 50% and above starts fighting the text, and below 15% it only shows up on screen, not on paper. This tool stamps every page identically, because a watermark on page one and clean pages after it is worse than none at all — it tells the reader the mark was an accident.</p>
<p>One honest limit: a watermark deters casual copying; it does not stop a determined person with a PDF editor, who can remove an overlay stamp. It is a label and a deterrent, not a lock — for genuinely sensitive documents, proper access control beats any watermark. The stamp is added to a new copy of your file with pdf-lib, in your browser, and the original is never changed or uploaded. For writing on one page only, use <a href="/add-text-to-pdf/">Add Text to PDF</a>; for a signature, <a href="/sign-pdf/">Sign PDF</a>.</p></section>
''',
"passport-photo-maker": '''<section class="section"><h2>The photo a form accepts is a specification, not a portrait</h2>
<p>A passport photo is not judged on how good you look — it is judged on measurements. The face must fill a set share of the frame, the background must be plain white or off-white, the head must be straight and unshadowed, and the file must be exactly the size the form asks for: 35 × 45 mm in much of Europe and India, 2 × 2 inches in the United States, 51 × 51 mm for an Indian passport. A beautiful photo at the wrong size is rejected as surely as a blurry one, and a photo at the right size but the wrong shape is rejected too. The specification is the whole job.</p>
<p>This tool does the mechanical half of that specification: it takes any photo, crops it from the centre to the exact ratio of the size you picked, and resizes it to the exact pixel dimensions that size means at 300 dpi — 413 × 531 pixels for 35 × 45 mm, 600 × 600 for both square sizes. It does not do the other half, and it says so plainly: it cannot check where your head sits in the frame, whether the lighting is even, or whether you are smiling (most authorities require a neutral expression). Frame your face in the centre of the shot, against a plain light wall, in even daylight, and the crop will land where the rules expect it.</p>
<p>Two practical notes. First, if your photo has a transparent background — a cut-out from another tool — tick the white-background box, or the transparency becomes black in a JPG. Second, always check the current official specification for the document you are applying for: sizes are stable, but rules on glasses, head coverings and photo age change, and the issuing authority's page is the only source that counts. For the file-size side of the same forms, the <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a> shrinks the finished photo to the exact KB cap a portal demands.</p></section>
''',
"xml-formatter": '''<section class="section"><h2>XML is strict on purpose — and the error is the help</h2>
<p>XML looks like HTML's fussier cousin, and the fussiness is the point. Where HTML forgives a missing closing tag, XML refuses the whole document: one root element, every tag closed, attributes quoted, case exact. That strictness is what makes XML trustworthy as a data format — a config file, an RSS feed, a sitemap, a data export — because a file that parses is a file whose structure is exactly what it claims to be. The cost is that a single stray character breaks everything, and the error message is usually where the parser gave up, not where you made the mistake.</p>
<p>This formatter is built around that reality. It validates first and formats second: paste the document, and the first error is shown with its line and column, so the fix starts at the right place instead of at the top. Pretty-printing then rewrites the document with two-space indentation, one element per line, so the structure is visible at a glance — nested data becomes a readable outline instead of a single unreadable line. Minify does the reverse for shipping: every unnecessary space removed, the data identical. The XML declaration at the top, if you pasted one, is carried through both operations.</p>
<p>One boundary to know: this tool works on well-formed XML — documents that follow the syntax rules. A document that is valid against a schema (an XSD or DTD) is a different, stricter question, and this page does not check it; a file can be perfectly well-formed and still wrong for the system that consumes it. For its sibling format, the <a href="/json-formatter/">JSON Formatter</a> does the same job — validate, pretty-print, minify — with JSON's own line-and-column errors.</p></section>
''',
}

def _seo_desc(desc):
    low = desc.lower()
    needs_free = "free" not in low
    needs_trust = "sign-up" not in low and "signup" not in low
    if needs_free and needs_trust:
        suffix = " Free to use — no sign-up, and it runs 100% in your browser, so nothing leaves your device."
    elif needs_trust:
        suffix = " No sign-up — runs 100% in your browser, so nothing leaves your device."
    elif needs_free:
        suffix = " Free to use online."
    else:
        return desc
    if len(desc) + len(suffix) <= 225:
        return desc + suffix
    return desc

