         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The most-asked calendar question in the world</h2>
<p>"What date is 90 days from today?" might be the single most common date question on the internet, and it is asked for serious reasons. Visa rules are written as 90 days in a rolling period. Probation reviews land 90 days after a start date. Warranties expire 180 or 365 days after purchase; return windows close 30 days after delivery; contracts demand action "within 14 days of this notice". Every one of those phrases hides the same job: start at a date, move a fixed number of days, and name the date you land on — weekday included, because a deadline that lands on a Sunday behaves differently in most offices.</p>
<h2>Why guessing goes wrong</h2>
<p>The natural guess treats 90 days as "three months" and jumps to the same date three months later. But months are not 30 days each: a 90-day span can cross months of 28, 30 and 31 days in any order, so the true landing date is usually a few days before or after the three-month guess. Across a February in a leap year it moves again. The difference is small — and completely decisive when a border officer, an HR system or a court is doing the counting. Rules written in days must be answered in days, on the real calendar.</p>
<h2>The method</h2>
<p>By hand, you move month by month: from the start date, count the days left in the current month, subtract them from your total, then walk whole months at their true lengths until the remainder fits inside one month — that remainder is your landing day. Going backwards works identically in reverse, which is how you answer "what date was 45 days ago?" for refund windows and backdated deadlines. It is honest arithmetic but slow and easy to fumble under pressure. The <a href="/date-calculator/">Date Calculator</a> does exactly this walk instantly, in both directions, and shows the weekday of the date you land on.</p>
<h2>Calendar days or working days?</h2>
<p>Check the wording before you celebrate an answer. "Within 90 days" in a visa or contract almost always means calendar days — every day counts, weekends included. "Within 10 working days" or "business days" excludes Saturdays and Sundays (and is the job of our <a href="/business-days-calculator/">Business Days Calculator</a>). Mixing the two is the expensive error: a working-day deadline counted as calendar days makes you late; the reverse makes you needlessly early. The phrase in the document is the instruction — read it literally.</p>
<h2>A worked example</h2>
<p>A warranty runs 180 days from a purchase made on 3 January. January has 31 days, so 28 days remain in January after the 3rd. February (non-leap) takes 28, March 31, April 30, May 31 — that is 148 of the 180 days by the end of May, leaving 32, which runs 30 days through June and lands on 2 July. The calculator returns the same answer in a blink, with the weekday attached: 2 July, ready to diary. Try your own dates — the tool runs in your browser, and the dates never leave your device.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Rolling windows: when the deadline moves every day</h2>
<p>Some day-count rules do not stand still. The famous one governs short stays in Europe's Schengen area: 90 days within any 180-day period. There is no fixed start date — every single day, the window looks back 180 days and counts your days inside it. A traveller who spent 90 straight days in the zone must stay out for 90 days before the window frees up enough room, and partial trips free up day by day as old days fall out of the window's back edge. The same rolling pattern appears in some insurance waiting periods, tax-residency tests and platform rate limits. The working method is identical in all of them: for any day you care about, count the days between that day and the day 180 (or 90, or 365) before it, and check how many of your used days sit inside. Our <a href="/days-between-dates/">Days Between Dates</a> calculator does that gap count; the date calculator finds the window's edges.</p>
<p>Recurring deadlines are the friendly version of the same idea: a 90-day probation review, a quarterly report "within 45 days of quarter end", a subscription that renews 365 days after it starts. Write the rule as its day count and anchor date, not as a date you calculated once in your head — anchors survive staff changes and lost emails, while a date with no visible reasoning gets re-derived wrong by the next person. And when the answer really matters — a border, a court, a contract — calculate it twice by two routes (add forward from the anchor; count the gap back) and make the two answers agree before you act.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-calculate-your-age-exactly",
