         + """<section class="section">
<h2>Why "exact" age is a different number</h2>
<p>Subtract your birth year from the current year and you have an age that is right for part of the year and wrong for the rest. Anyone whose birthday is still ahead of them this year is a year younger than the subtraction claims. For conversation that hardly matters; for the places exact age is demanded — school admissions, pension and benefit eligibility, insurance, sports categories, medical forms — it is the whole question. Those settings want your age as a completed count: full years lived, then the months since your last birthday, then the days since that month turned.</p>
<h2>The borrowing method</h2>
<p>Officials and our calculator use the same procedure. Start with today's date and your birth date. If today's day-of-month is smaller than your birth day-of-month, borrow a month: the days component becomes the difference counted across the true length of the previous month. If today's month is smaller than your birth month, borrow a year the same way. Walk through an example: born 25 June 1994, today is 12 November 2026. Days: 12 is smaller than 25, so borrow October's 31 days — 12 + 31 − 25 = 18 days, and the month count starts from October. Months: 10 (October) − 6 (June) = 4 months. Years: 2026 − 1994 = 32. Exact age: 32 years, 4 months, 18 days. Every step used the real calendar — which is exactly where hand arithmetic slips, around short months and leap days.</p>
<h2>The totals underneath</h2>
<p>An exact-age calculation also yields your total days lived, and that number has a life of its own. Milestone culture runs on it: your 10,000th day is a real, findable date — for most people it falls in their late twenties — and "days old" parties have become a small tradition precisely because the number feels more personal than a birthday. Total weeks explain baby ages (a 14-week-old is "three months" only loosely), and retirement and pension planning quietly counts in total days more often than brochures admit. The <a href="/age-calculator/">Age Calculator</a> shows years-months-days plus totals in weeks, days and hours from one date of birth.</p>
<h2>Ages on other dates</h2>
<p>Many exact-age questions are not about today at all: how old will a child be on the school cut-off date, how old were you when a photograph was taken, will you be 60 by a retirement date? Those need the same borrowing method aimed at a different target date — our <a href="/age-on-date/">Age on a Specific Date</a> calculator does that version, backwards and forwards. Whichever you use, the calculation happens in your browser: a date of birth is personal data, and here it is never transmitted or stored.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Where the exact method meets real forms</h2>
<p>Insurance runs on a variation worth knowing: many policies price by "age last birthday" — your age in completed years at the policy's start — while others use "age nearest birthday", rounding to whichever birthday is closer. Two quotes for the same person can therefore legitimately use ages a year apart, and a premium difference that looks like an error is sometimes just two conventions at work. Pensions and state benefits are stricter: entitlement usually begins on the exact birthday a law names, which is why official letters ask for a date of birth rather than an age — the date never drifts, while a stated age is stale the moment it is written down.</p>
<p>School admissions are where parents meet the method first. Systems phrase the rule as an age on a fixed date ("four years old on or before 31 August"), which means the operative calculation is not age today but age on that date — the job of the <a href="/age-on-date/">Age on a Specific Date</a> calculator. A child born on 2 September can be a full school year behind a child born on 30 August, eleven months of development compressed into a three-day difference in birthdays; the exact method is how you see that coming years in advance instead of at the application deadline.</p>
<p>And the leap-day question, because it always comes up: someone born on 29 February has a birthday the calendar only prints every four years. In non-leap years, many legal systems treat 1 March as the day they turn a year older; families, sensibly, celebrate on whichever of 28 February or 1 March they prefer. For exact-age calculations in a non-leap year, the calculator counts by the calendar as it stands — if an official form is involved, the official's convention is the one that decides.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>Why does my age in days keep changing its weeks figure?</strong> Because weeks are unforgiving: every seven days the count turns, so the same total can read as 1,400 weeks one day and 1,401 the next midweek. Both are right; the day count underneath is the stable figure.</p>
<p><strong>Is a person "in their 40th year" at 39 or 40?</strong> At 39. Your first year of life ends on your first birthday, so your 40th year runs from your 39th birthday to your 40th. Insurance documents and horoscopes use the phrase differently, which is why exact dates beat phrases everywhere in this guide.</p>
<p><strong>Do time zones or birth times matter?</strong> For legal age, no — the date governs, and you come of age at the start of your birthday in the place whose law applies. Birth times matter for the total-hours figure and for twins born either side of midnight, not for your age in years.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-work-out-an-age-gap",
         title="How to Work Out an Age Gap Between Two People | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to work out the exact age difference between two people — siblings, couples, parents and children — in years, months and days, and why birth-year subtraction misleads.",
         body=page_head("How to Work Out an Age Gap Between Two People", "The true gap between two birthdays is rarely the difference between two birth years.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The two-birthday problem</h2>
<p>"How much older is she than her brother?" sounds like a subtraction, and the subtraction everyone reaches for — birth year minus birth year — is wrong for most of the calendar. Two people born in 1990 and 1992 can be barely a year apart (December 1990 and January 1992) or nearly three years apart (January 1990 and December 1992). Birth months and days decide which, and they decide real things: school cohorts, sibling spacing, eligibility windows, and the quiet arithmetic behind family trees.</p>
<h2>The exact method</h2>
<p>Work the gap the way you work an exact age: line up the two full birth dates and subtract with borrowing. Full years first, then the months left over, then the days, borrowing across real month lengths as you go. Example: 12 March 1990 and 25 June 1994. Years: 1994 − 1990 = 4. Months: June comes after March, so 3 full months. Days: 25 − 12 = 13. Gap: 4 years, 3 months, 13 days — 1,566 days in total. When the later date's month or day is the smaller one, the borrowing walks the other way, which is where hand calculation traditionally drops a month. The <a href="/age-difference-calculator/">Age Difference Calculator</a> does the borrowing both ways, tells you which person is older, and shows each person's current age alongside the gap.</p>
<h2>Where gaps actually decide things</h2>
<p>School placement is the heavyweight: systems that admit children by a cut-off date ("five by 1 September") mean an eleven-month gap can share a classroom while a two-week gap across the cut-off separates children by a full year. Youth sports leagues split by birth date for the same reason. In family history, the gap is a checking tool: a claimed mother-child gap of twelve years or a sibling gap of four months flags a record that needs a second look, while the total-days figure exposes two sources disagreeing by days that a years-only comparison hides.</p>
<h2>Couples, generations and curiosity</h2>
<p>For couples the exact gap is usually pure curiosity — studies put the average gap between married partners at roughly two to three years — but curiosity deserves an exact answer too. Generations are the same maths at family scale: measure the gap between a grandparent's and a grandchild's birth dates and divide by the generations between them, and you have your family's true generation length, which is rarely the round 25 or 30 years people assume. Both birth dates are crunched in your browser and never sent anywhere — family arithmetic stays in the family.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Reading a family tree with gaps</h2>
<p>Genealogists use age gaps as a lie detector, and the tests are simple arithmetic. Work down a tree measuring the gap between each parent's birth and each child's. Gaps in the twenties and thirties are the ordinary rhythm of most families across most of history. A gap under about fifteen years between a mother and a child asks for a second look at the records — someone has been attached to the wrong generation, a common error when names repeat. A gap over sixty between a father and a child is possible but rare enough to double-check. Sibling gaps tell stories too: a run of two-year gaps interrupted by a nine-year gap often marks a remarriage, a war, or a child who died young and was never spoken of; twins, of course, show a gap of zero days — or one, when the second twin arrives after midnight, a detail families treasure and records garble.</p>
<p>The generation length ties it together. Take a grandparent born in 1931 and a grandchild born in 1998: the gap is 67 years across two generations, a generation length of about 33.5 years. Measure several lines of the same family and the average is a real fact about that family's history — families that married late run long generations and compress more history into fewer names, which is why one person's great-grandparent can be another's contemporary. All of it falls out of two dates and the borrowing method; the calculator at the top of this page exists so the arithmetic is never the part that goes wrong.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>Can an age gap be negative, or zero?</strong> The gap itself is a distance, so it is never negative — the tool reports which person is older instead of using a sign. Zero happens exactly once in nature: twins born minutes apart share a gap of 0 years, 0 months and a handful of minutes, which the day-level gap honestly reports as 0 days.</p>
<p><strong>What is the biggest age gap ever recorded between siblings?</strong> Verified cases exceed 40 years — a parent having children across four decades. It is rare enough that a gap like that in a family record deserves a source check, which is precisely the genealogist's test described above.</p>
<p><strong>Does the order I enter the dates matter?</strong> No. Older-first or younger-first, the gap and the "who is older" answer come out identical — the borrowing simply runs in whichever direction the dates demand.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-compress-a-pdf",
         title="How to Compress a PDF to 200KB or 100KB | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to compress a PDF under the 200KB or 100KB limit that forms demand — why PDFs get heavy, what compression changes, and how to keep the result readable.",
         body=page_head("How to Compress a PDF to 200KB or 100KB", "The form said no. Here is how to make the same document fit — without making it unreadable.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The message everyone has seen</h2>
<p>"Please upload a PDF under 200 KB." Exam boards, job portals, visa and government forms all say some version of it, and they say it to people holding a 4 MB scan of a two-page certificate. The limit is not spite: portals store millions of uploads and set the ceiling near what a clean document should weigh. The problem is that a phone scan is not a clean document — it is a set of photographs, and photographs are heavy. Understanding that single fact makes the whole job make sense.</p>
<h2>Why your PDF is heavy</h2>
<p>Text weighs almost nothing; images weigh a great deal. A born-digital PDF — typed, exported from Word — is mostly text and stays small. A scanned or photographed document is images all the way down: each page a full-colour picture, often at the camera's full resolution, most of it blank margin and paper texture that no reader needs at photographic fidelity. Compression earns its keep by re-encoding those page images at a sensible size, efficiently — the pages themselves are never re-drawn, so the text layer stays exactly as it was. The words still read identically on screen and in print; the megabytes were mostly air.</p>
<h2>The job, step by step</h2>
<p>First, check the actual limit on the form — 200KB and 100KB are the common pair, and aiming for the wrong one wastes a round trip. Second, run the file through the <a href="/compress-pdf/">PDF Compressor</a> with that target selected; the tool works down through gentler settings first, so a file that only needs a light squeeze keeps the most sharpness. Third, open the result and read a page at full size. Text should be crisp, stamps and signatures legible. If a very tight limit has softened a page that must be read closely — a marksheet, a certificate with fine print — compress the pages as separate smaller PDFs if the form allows multiple uploads.</p>
<h2>What compression changes — honestly</h2>
<p>A PDF compressed this way keeps its pages as pages: the text inside stays selectable and searchable, because only the images were re-encoded. (Compressors that flatten pages into pictures do destroy the text layer — that is the older method, and it is why we do not use it.) Keep your original untouched as the master anyway, name the compressed one clearly ("certificate-200kb.pdf"), and you will never wonder which file is which. If the document began life as a digital export and is somehow still huge, the weight is usually embedded images or fonts — re-exporting with images downsampled, then compressing, beats either step alone.</p>
<h2>The privacy point</h2>
<p>Documents that need compressing for forms are, almost by definition, your most sensitive files: ID, certificates, bank proofs, signed agreements. Uploading them to a random server to shave off kilobytes is a bad trade. Our compressor runs entirely in your browser — the file is read on your device, shrunk on your device, and downloaded back to your device. Nothing is transmitted, stored or "deleted within 24 hours", because nothing ever left.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>If it still does not fit: the escalation ladder</h2>
<p>Occasionally a file resists — a long scan at a strict 100KB limit, where maximum compression makes the text mush. Work down this ladder before giving up. First, split the document: many portals accept several files, and three 100KB parts are legal where one 300KB file is not; the <a href="/split-pdf/">Split PDF tool</a> does the dividing cleanly. Second, fix the source rather than the symptom: re-scan at 200 DPI in greyscale instead of full colour at 600 DPI, and the file is often born under the limit — colour is the single heaviest ingredient in a scan, and most official documents carry no information in their colour at all. Third, if the PDF was exported from an office app rather than scanned, re-export with images downsampled; embedded full-size photographs are the usual hidden weight. Fourth, merge discipline: compress after merging several files, not before, so the squeeze is applied once to the final weight that the portal actually measures.</p>
<p>What not to do: do not screenshot the pages and paste them into a document to shrink it — the result is a worse image in a heavier wrapper. And do not keep re-compressing a compressed file hunting for the target; each round-trip costs quality without teaching the file anything new. One clean pass from the original, at the form's actual limit, with a full-size read of the result — that is the whole professional method.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>Will the office accept a compressed PDF?</strong> Yes — a compressed PDF is an ordinary PDF; nothing in the file marks it as compressed, and it opens identically everywhere. What the office checks is the size limit and whether the pages read clearly, and both are under your control before you submit.</p>
<p><strong>Why did my file land just over the limit?</strong> A kilobyte on a form usually means 1,000 bytes to the portal's counter and 1,024 to your computer, and image-heavy final pages resist the last squeeze. Aim a few kilobytes under the stated limit — target 195KB for a 200KB form — and the rounding never bites.</p>
<p><strong>Does compressing change page size or order?</strong> No. Pages keep their dimensions and sequence; only the weight of the images inside them changes.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-convert-pdf-pages-to-jpg-images",
         title="How to Convert PDF Pages to JPG Images | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to convert PDF pages into JPG images — when a document needs to become pictures for slides, social posts or image-only forms, and how to keep the results sharp.",
         body=page_head("How to Convert PDF Pages to JPG Images", "For slides, posts and stubborn forms that accept pictures but not documents.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>Documents travel as PDFs; screens speak images</h2>
<p>PDF is where documents live, but much of the modern world will not take one. Presentation software wants a picture of the chart, not the report it came from. Social platforms, messaging apps and most form builders accept images only. A client will open a photo instantly and let a PDF sit for a week. Converting each page of a PDF into a JPG solves the mismatch in the most robust way possible: a picture cannot reflow, lose its fonts, or break on an old device. The page looks exactly as designed, everywhere, forever.</p>
<h2>Step by step</h2>
<p>Open the <a href="/pdf-to-jpg/">PDF to JPG converter</a>, choose your PDF, and convert. Every page becomes its own JPG, in order, rendered at 2× the page's natural size. That double rendering is the step most converters skip and the reason their output looks soft: a page rendered at exactly screen size has no spare pixels when you zoom, project or print it. At 2×, text stays crisp on a slide and a photo page stays honest on a phone. Download the pages, keep the ones you need, discard the rest — page 7 as its own image was the point of the exercise.</p>
<h2>JPG or PNG? Choosing honestly</h2>
<p>JPG is the right destination for photographs, scanned pages, slides and anything headed for a screen or a message: small files, universal support, quality that holds up at sensible settings. PNG wins in two cases — pages that are mostly sharp text and line diagrams where you want every edge lossless, and pages with transparency, which JPG cannot hold at all (it fills transparent areas with white). Our <a href="/pdf-to-png/">PDF to PNG converter</a> handles that version. Converting to JPG will not improve a poor PDF, either: a blurry scan becomes a faithful picture of a blurry scan.</p>
<h2>Where the images go next</h2>
<p>A page-image drops straight into PowerPoint or Google Slides as a full-bleed slide, attaches to a WhatsApp message without a document icon, uploads to the form that demanded "JPG only", and prints as a poster with its layout frozen. One caution: a page converted to an image carries its text as pixels, so it cannot be searched or copied from afterwards — if the reader needs the text, send the PDF as well. And the conversion runs on your device: contracts and statements become images without ever being uploaded to become them.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Sizes, names and order — the practical details</h2>
<p>A converted A4 page at 2× rendering lands around 1,650 × 2,340 pixels — sharp enough to print at A4, big enough for a full-screen slide, and typically a few hundred kilobytes as a JPG: light enough to message, heavy enough to read. A 30-page report therefore becomes 30 images totalling perhaps 6–10 MB. That is normal; images are simply what pages weigh when they travel individually, which is why the PDF remains the better format for sending whole documents and the images are for using pages.</p>
<p>Naming is where converted pages succeed or fail as a set. Keep the converter's page order in the filenames — page-01, page-02, page-03, never page-1, page-2, page-10, which sorts itself into nonsense in every file browser — and prefix the set with the document's name ("contract-page-03.jpg") if pages from several documents will share a folder. It takes ten seconds at download time and saves the archaeology later.</p>
<p>One more working habit: convert once, at the generous 2× size, and treat those images as masters. Need a smaller copy for a chat message? Shrink the master with the <a href="/image-resizer/">Image Resizer</a>. Re-converting the PDF for every use, or shrinking and then re-enlarging a small copy, both end in the same soft place. Masters stay big; copies are disposable. That rule, plus the privacy of converting on your own device, is the whole of the professional method for this small job.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>Can I convert just one page?</strong> The converter renders every page, each as its own download named in order — converting the whole file and keeping page 7 is faster than any page-picking workflow, and the spare images cost nothing but a delete. If the document is enormous, split out the chapter first with the <a href="/split-pdf/">Split PDF tool</a>, then convert the small piece.</p>
<p><strong>Why does my converted page look slightly different in colour?</strong> PDFs can carry print-oriented colour (CMYK) that screens approximate as RGB; the conversion is faithful to how the page displays, and any shift arrived with the source file, not the converter. On-screen documents — the vast majority — show no shift at all.</p>
<p><strong>What if the PDF is password-protected?</strong> A protected PDF must be unlocked first — the converter cannot, and should not, bypass a document's password. Open it with the password in a PDF reader, save an unlocked copy, and convert that.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-merge-pdf-files-into-one",
         title="How to Merge PDF Files Into One Document | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to merge several PDFs into one file — ordering pages properly, preparing for application portals, size limits, and doing it without uploading your documents.",
         body=page_head("How to Merge PDF Files Into One Document", "One portal, one upload button, five files. This is the fix.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The one-upload-button problem</h2>
<p>Application portals, HR systems and university admissions all share a design decision: one field, one file. Meanwhile your evidence lives as several — a passport scan here, a bank statement there, a signed form somewhere else. Merging is the bridge: several PDFs combined into a single document, in the order the reader expects, uploaded once. The same job appears at work in friendlier clothes — four exported reports becoming one board document, chapters becoming a book, invoices becoming a month's bundle for the accountant.</p>
<h2>Order is the whole craft</h2>
<p>Joining the files is mechanical; the skill is sequence. Readers judge a merged document by its flow, and the expected flow is the order the checklist lists the documents in: identity first, then qualifications, then financials, signature where it certifies. In the <a href="/merge-pdf/">Merge PDF tool</a>, arrange the files in that order before you merge, then read the arrangement once more the way a stranger would — because a stranger is exactly who will open it. Remember that each source file contributes its pages in its own order, so a source document that is itself scrambled will be faithfully scrambled in the result; fix it at the source first.</p>
<h2>Sizes and limits</h2>
<p>Merging stacks weight as well as pages: five files of 2 MB become one file of roughly 10 MB, and portals that demand one file often also cap its size. The correct order of operations is merge first, then compress the combined document once with the <a href="/compress-pdf/">PDF Compressor</a> to the portal's limit — 200KB and 100KB are the usual ceilings. Compressing each file separately first also works, but one pass on the final bundle is simpler and treats the document evenly. If the total page count looks surprising after a merge, count the sources: merged output is the sum of its parts, and a stray duplicate file in the list is the usual explanation.</p>
<h2>Doing it without uploading your life</h2>
<p>A merged bundle is a concentration of your most sensitive documents in one place — precisely the file you least want on a stranger's server. This merger assembles the document in your browser: the sources are read on your device, the combined file is built on your device, and nothing is transmitted in either direction. Merge, download, and the bundle exists only where you put it.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Before you send it: the two-minute check</h2>
<p>Merged documents fail in predictable ways, and a short ritual catches all of them. <strong>Open the merged file and flick through every page.</strong> Not a sample — every page, at thumbnail speed. You are looking for the four classics: a document included twice because it was added twice; a file that was itself scanned upside down, now faithfully upside down in the bundle; pages from the wrong version of a form; and the blank page that some scanners append, sitting in the middle like a shrug. <strong>Check the page count against your expectation</strong> — the sum of the sources' pages. A mismatch means something was added or left out, and it is far better discovered by you than by an admissions officer. <strong>Check the total file size</strong> against the portal's cap while you can still compress calmly, not at the deadline. <strong>Check the signature page specifically</strong> — signatures are the page most often scanned at an angle, cropped, or swapped for the unsigned version.</p>
<p>Then name the file like a professional: "hamza-basra-application-documents.pdf" tells a recipient what it is before it is opened; "merged.pdf" tells them nothing and joins a folder of identical strangers. None of this is pedantry. A merged bundle is usually the single file on which an application, a claim or a hiring decision turns, and the people who open them form their first impression from exactly these details — order, completeness, and a name that respects their time.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>Can I merge a PDF with a Word document or a photo?</strong> Not directly — merging joins PDFs to PDFs. Convert the other pieces first: a photo becomes a PDF with the <a href="/jpg-to-pdf/">JPG to PDF converter</a>, and a Word document exported or printed to PDF joins the set the same way. Once everything speaks PDF, one merge finishes the document.</p>
<p><strong>Does merging change the quality of the pages?</strong> No. Merging copies pages as they are — no re-encoding, no compression, no quality loss. What you put in is exactly what the reader gets out, which is why the order check matters more than any setting.</p>
<p><strong>Is there a page or size limit?</strong> The practical limit is your device's memory, since the work happens in your browser — ordinary application bundles of a few dozen pages merge in seconds. Very large bundles (hundreds of pages, hundreds of megabytes) are happier on a desktop browser than an old phone.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-turn-photos-into-a-pdf",
         title="How to Turn Photos Into a Single PDF | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to turn phone photos of contracts, receipts and ID into one clean PDF — order, orientation, file size, and sending copies that offices accept.",
         body=page_head("How to Turn Photos Into a Single PDF", "Your phone is a scanner. This is how its pile of photos becomes a document.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The camera roll is not a document</h2>
<p>You photographed the contract — all four pages, plus the signed one twice because the first shot was blurry. The office now wants "the document, as a PDF". Between those two facts sits this job: selecting the right photos, in the right order, and binding them into one PDF where each photo becomes one page. It is the most common document task in modern life, and the difference between a submission that sails through and one that gets returned is almost never the tool — it is the two minutes of care before converting.</p>
<h2>Prepare the photos first</h2>
<p>Pick the sharpest shot of each page and delete its duplicates from the set before you start. Check orientation: a page photographed sideways becomes a sideways PDF page, and rotating your phone while shooting does not always register; rotate stragglers upright with any photo editor first. Flatten the visual field if you can — pages shot flat on a table in decent light, filling the frame, convert into PDFs that look scanned. Pages shot at an angle on a bed convert into PDFs that look like evidence photos. The <a href="/jpg-to-pdf/">JPG to PDF converter</a> binds what you give it faithfully, so give it the good set.</p>
<h2>Bind in reading order</h2>
<p>Add the photos in the sequence the reader needs: page 1, page 2, and so on; ID front before ID back; the summary before the receipts that prove it. The tool's list is the document's order — read it once before converting, exactly as you would flick through a stapled stack before handing it over. Convert, then open the PDF and flick through it again. Two flicks, thirty seconds, and you have caught every error class this job has: a missing page, a doubled page, a wrong order, a sideways page.</p>
<h2>Weight and privacy</h2>
<p>Camera photos are heavy, and a ten-page photo-PDF can outweigh the portal's limit by twenty times. If the form caps uploads at 200KB or 100KB, shrink the finished PDF with the <a href="/compress-pdf/">PDF Compressor</a> after binding — one pass on the complete document. Photographs of documents are also sensitive by nature, and this binding happens in your browser: the photos are assembled on your device and the file never passes through a server to become a document.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Five habits that make phone scans look professional</h2>
<p><strong>Light from the side, not the flash.</strong> Daylight from a window beats a camera flash, which blows out the middle of the page and leaves the corners in shadow. If the page shines — laminated IDs are the worst — tilt the document slightly rather than the phone, until the glare slides off the text. <strong>All four corners in frame, with a small margin.</strong> A page cropped to its edges looks tampered with; a page shown whole, with a breath of table around it, reads as a faithful copy. <strong>Shoot from directly above.</strong> Angles make trapezoids, and while a mild keystone is readable, a strong one whispers "photographed in a hurry" to everyone who opens it. Stand up, align the page's edges with the frame, then shoot. <strong>Flatten the page.</strong> Curled receipts and book spines bend text into waves; thirty seconds under a heavy book, or a hand holding one edge out of shot, straightens the lines the reader's eye has to follow. <strong>One session per document.</strong> Photograph all pages of one document together, in order, before starting the next. Camera rolls interleave life with paperwork; a single session keeps the set contiguous, in sequence, and findable — the three things you will beg for at binding time.</p>
<p>Later, the same habits pay off again: a clean set of page photos converts into a PDF that looks like it came from a scanner, and offices accept clean PDFs without the follow-up email asking for a clearer copy. The camera was never the problem. The two unhurried minutes were always the product.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>Can I add one more page to a PDF I already made?</strong> Yes — merge it. Make the extra page into its own small PDF the same way, then join the two with the <a href="/merge-pdf/">Merge PDF tool</a>, new PDF after the old one. The result is the original document plus its missing page, with no re-photographing of anything.</p>
<p><strong>Why does my PDF look less sharp than the photos?</strong> Almost always the viewer, not the file: PDF readers soften pages at fit-to-screen zoom. Zoom to 100% before judging — if the photo was sharp, the page is sharp. If it is soft at full size too, the original shot was soft, and reshooting that one page beats any setting.</p>
<p><strong>What about HEIC photos from an iPhone?</strong> Convert them to JPG first with the <a href="/heic-to-jpg/">HEIC to JPG converter</a> — one quick pass — and then bind them like any other photos.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-resize-an-image-for-an-online-form",
         title="How to Resize an Image for an Online Form | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to resize a photo for an online form — the difference between pixel size and file size, aspect ratio, passport-photo dimensions, and hitting KB limits exactly.",
         body=page_head("How to Resize an Image for an Online Form", "Two different limits, one confused internet. Pixels and kilobytes are not the same problem.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The form is asking two different questions</h2>
<p>Upload forms speak two languages and rarely say which one they mean. Some specify dimensions: "image must be 600×600 pixels", "photo should be 35×45 mm". Others specify weight: "file must not exceed 200KB". Many of the most frustrating — passport, visa and exam portals — specify both. A photo can pass one test and fail the other in either direction: a tiny 300×300 image exported carelessly can weigh more than a huge one saved well. Before touching any tool, read the form's wording once and identify which limits are actually yours.</p>
<h2>Dimensions: pixels and proportions</h2>
<p>For a dimension limit, the <a href="/image-resizer/">Image Resizer</a> takes the photo to the exact pixel size the form demands. The discipline is the aspect ratio: 600×600 from a rectangular photo means something has to give — either the frame crops to a square, or the picture stretches. Stretching is always wrong; faces widen, logos warp, and officials reject distorted ID photos on sight. Resizers here keep the ratio locked by default, so a 600-pixel-wide result keeps the photo's true proportions. If the form demands an exact rectangle your photo is not shaped like, crop first with the <a href="/image-cropper/">Image Cropper</a> — cropping chooses what to keep; stretching chooses to lie about all of it.</p>
<h2>Weight: kilobytes and the quality dial</h2>
<p>For a weight limit, the lever is encoding quality, and the <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a> turns it to a target you name: 200KB, 100KB, 50KB for the strictest ID forms. Two facts make the job predictable. First, dimensions and weight are linked — halving width and height quarters the pixels and usually cuts the weight far more than half, so a modest resize often lands a photo near its limit before compression finishes it. Second, quality is a dial with a gentle slope: at around 80% most photos are indistinguishable from the original on screen. Preview the result before you submit; on an ID photo the face must stay unmistakably the face.</p>
<h2>The working order</h2>
<p>Crop to the required shape, resize to the required dimensions, then compress to the required weight — in that order, once each. Doing it backwards (compressing, then resizing) wastes quality you will want later, and re-compressing an already-compressed file degrades it further each round. Keep the phone's original untouched as your master, name the finished file for its purpose ("passport-photo-200kb.jpg"), and the next form with the same rules becomes a ten-second job. All of it happens in your browser — which, for photographs of identity documents, is exactly where it should happen.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>The passport-photo special case</h2>
<p>Identity photos are where resizing stops being casual, because the specification is physical: the classic passport photo is 35 × 45 mm — a ratio of 7:9 — and portals variously demand it in millimetres, in pixels, or as a file under a weight cap like 200KB, sometimes all three. Converting between the two languages is one formula: pixels = millimetres ÷ 25.4 × the dots-per-inch the portal assumes, and the common assumption is 300 DPI. At 300 DPI, 35 × 45 mm becomes roughly 413 × 531 pixels — the number behind most portals' "413×531 px" requirement, which looks arbitrary until the millimetres are recognised inside it. If a portal states pixels, use its pixels; if it states millimetres, the ratio is what matters and any size in 7:9 proportion (700×900, say) carries the right shape.</p>
<p>Framing has rules too, and they are about the person, not the file: head and shoulders, face occupying roughly 70–80% of the frame's height, eyes in the upper half, plain light background, no shadows across the face. Crop for those proportions with the cropper first, then resize to the stated pixels, then compress to the stated weight — the same crop → size → weight order as every other form photo, with no step skipped because the checker is a machine that reads the rules literally. A rejected passport photo is almost never a photography failure; it is a specification failure, and specifications can be met exactly, in advance, in your browser.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>The form says "recent photo, 200KB" but gives no size. What do I do?</strong> Weight-only forms are the forgiving kind: keep the photo's proportions natural, resize it to a sensible width (1,000–1,500 pixels suits most profile and application slots), then compress to a little under the cap — 190KB for a 200KB limit — so the portal's rounding never rejects you by a hair.</p>
<p><strong>Can I make a photo bigger to meet a minimum size?</strong> You can set the pixels, but you cannot invent the detail: an enlarged photo passes the machine's dimension check and may fail the human's sharpness check. If a portal sets a minimum size, it expects a photo that was born that big — retake it closer, or use the camera's full resolution rather than a messaging app's shrunken copy.</p>
<p><strong>Why was my photo rejected as "edited"?</strong> Some ID portals screen for filters and heavy retouching. Resizing and compressing are not the edits they mean — they mean altered faces and backgrounds — but keep colours and lighting faithful to the original shot, and never let a "beautify" mode near an identity photograph.</p></section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-calculate-percentages",
         title="How to Calculate Percentages Without Going Wrong | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to calculate percentages correctly — percentage of a number, percentage change, reversing a percentage, stacked discounts and percentage points, with worked examples.",
         body=page_head("How to Calculate Percentages Without Going Wrong", "Three questions, two traps, one habit that protects you from all of it.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>Every percentage problem is one of three questions</h2>
<p>Strip the story off any percentage problem and one of three skeletons falls out. <strong>Share of a whole:</strong> "What is 15% of 240?" — multiply 240 by 0.15 to get 36. Tips, commissions, tax amounts and discount values live here. <strong>Part against whole:</strong> "45 out of 180 is what percent?" — divide 45 by 180 and multiply by 100: 25%. Scores, completion rates and survey results live here. <strong>Change against a start:</strong> "From 80 to 100 is what percent increase?" — the change (20) divided by the start (80), times 100: 25%. Raises, inflation and growth live here. Nearly every percentage error in the wild is one question answered with another's method — most often a change calculated against the new value instead of the start. Our <a href="/percentage-calculator/">Percentage Calculator</a> keeps the three in separate boxes so the method cannot wander.</p>
<h2>Trap one: percentages do not cancel</h2>
<p>A price rises 25%, then falls 25% in a sale. It is not back where it started. The rise acted on 100 and made 125; the fall acted on 125 and removed 31.25, landing at 93.75. Percentages are always of something, and after the first move the something has changed. This asymmetry is not a technicality — it is the engine inside "up 50% this year" fund claims, recovery statistics and sale pricing. Whenever two percentages are chained, ask what each one is a percentage of.</p>
<h2>Trap two: percentage points</h2>
<p>When a rate itself changes — interest from 4% to 5%, unemployment from 6% to 7.5% — the honest unit for the difference is the percentage point: 4% to 5% is a rise of one percentage point. It is also, relative to itself, a 25% increase in the rate. Both statements are true, and headlines pick whichever serves the story. Reading "rates rose 25%" as "rates rose to 25%" — or the reverse — is the single most expensive misreading in news and finance. Check which unit the sentence is using before you react to it.</p>
<h2>Running percentages backwards</h2>
<p>The backwards jobs defeat more people than all the forwards ones: "84 after a 30% discount — what was the original?" The wrong instinct adds 30% of 84, overshooting, because the discount was taken from the original price, not from 84. The right move divides by what remains: after 30% off, 84 is 70% of the original, so 84 ÷ 0.70 = 120. The same reversal recovers a pre-tax price from a tax-inclusive one (divide by 1 + the tax rate as a decimal) and a pre-raise salary from a payslip. The <a href="/percentage-increase-calculator/">Percentage Increase Calculator</a> and <a href="/discount-calculator/">Discount Calculator</a> handle the two backwards jobs people meet most, and every number you type stays in your browser — percentages of your own salary are your own business.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Margin is not markup: the business trap</h2>
<p>One more confusion costs businesses real money, so it earns its place here. A shop buys a jacket for 80 and sells it for 100. The <em>markup</em> is the gain measured against cost: 20 ÷ 80 = 25%. The <em>margin</em> is the same gain measured against the selling price: 20 ÷ 100 = 20%. Same jacket, same 20 of profit, two different percentages — and they diverge further as the numbers grow: a 50% margin requires a 100% markup, and a business that prices by "adding our 30% margin" as a 30% markup is quietly earning a 23% margin and wondering where the money went. Neither figure is dishonest; they answer the share-of-whole question against two different wholes. The discipline is simply to name the whole every time — percent of cost, or percent of price — in your own books and in every quote you sign.</p>
<p>Examination marks are the friendlier everyday case, and they are the part-against-whole question in uniform: 342 marks from a total of 450 is 342 ÷ 450 × 100 = 76%. Where a certificate or a job application then asks for a grade band, the percentage is the number that travels between systems — which is why working it out exactly, rather than rounding your way to a feeling, is worth the ten seconds it takes. The pattern across this whole guide holds: identify which of the questions you are actually being asked, name what the percentage is of, and the arithmetic itself is the easy part.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Questions people ask</h2>
<p><strong>What is the fastest mental check for a percentage?</strong> Anchor on 10%. Ten percent of anything is a decimal shift; from it, 5% is half of that, 20% is double, and 15% is the two added together. A tip, a discount estimate, a sanity check on a bill — 10% plus its neighbours covers most of daily life, and it catches calculator typos before they cost you.</p>
<p><strong>Why did the calculator give a different answer to my spreadsheet?</strong> Usually rounding order: spreadsheets that round each intermediate step drift from an exact-then-round calculation by small amounts that compound in long chains. For money, calculate exactly and round once, at the end; for everything else, a difference in the second decimal is rounding, not error.</p>
<p><strong>Can a percentage be over 100?</strong> Whenever the part outgrows the original whole, yes — growth of 150%, a price at 250% of last year's. Percentages over 100 are only wrong when the part is truly a piece of the whole, as in a score or a share.</p></section>"""),
    dict(slug="guides/jpg-vs-png-vs-webp",
         title="JPG vs PNG vs WebP: Which Format Should You Choose? | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="JPG vs PNG vs WebP explained honestly — how each compresses, what happens to quality and transparency, realistic file sizes, and which format is right for forms, screenshots, logos, editing and websites.",
         body=page_head("JPG vs PNG vs WebP: Which Format Should You Choose?", "Three formats, three different bargains. The right choice depends on one question: what is this image for?")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The one-sentence version of each</h2>
<p><strong>JPG</strong> is the photograph format: small files, universal support, quality traded away in a way eyes forgive — and no transparency, ever. <strong>PNG</strong> is the fidelity format: every pixel stored exactly, transparency fully supported, files large enough to feel. <strong>WebP</strong> is the delivery format: noticeably smaller than both at web quality, transparency included, and native to every current browser — but still a stranger to some older desktop software. Almost every "which format?" question is decided by those three sentences plus one fact about your image: is it a photo, or is it a graphic?</p>
<h2>How the compression actually differs</h2>
<p>JPG compresses by discarding: it analyses blocks of the photograph and throws away fine detail the human eye is poor at seeing, mostly in colour rather than brightness. That is why photos survive it beautifully and why text, logos and line art suffer — sharp edges are exactly the high-detail information JPG discards first, so graphics come out speckled and slightly haloed. PNG compresses by describing: it finds patterns in the pixel data and stores them efficiently, losing nothing at all. A screenshot of text is stored perfectly — and a photograph, which has almost no repeatable pattern, is stored at several megabytes. WebP offers both modes: a lossy mode that out-compresses JPG at the same visual quality (typically by 25–35%), and a lossless mode that usually beats PNG's size on graphics while keeping every pixel and the transparency.</p>
<h2>Transparency, animation and metadata</h2>
<p>Transparency is the sharpest dividing line. JPG has none: a see-through area becomes white (or occasionally black) the moment you save. PNG and WebP both carry a full alpha channel, so logos, stickers, product cut-outs and overlays keep their shape against any background. Animation is WebP and GIF territory; JPG and PNG are stills formats for practical purposes. Metadata — camera settings, location, copyright notes stored as EXIF — tends to survive in originals and be stripped by converters and platforms alike, in every format; if the metadata matters, the original file is its only safe home, which is why every workflow below starts with keeping one.</p>
<h2>Realistic sizes, same photograph</h2>
<p>Take a typical modern phone photo, 4000×3000 pixels. As a camera JPG it might be 3–4 MB. Re-saved as PNG it balloons to 10 MB or more, with literally nothing added — the same picture, stored exactly. Encoded as WebP at web quality it lands near 2–2.5 MB, visually a match in ordinary viewing. Now take a screenshot full of text instead: PNG keeps it crisp at perhaps 400 KB, JPG smudges the letters while barely saving anything, and WebP lossless beats both. The format that "wins" flips completely with the image type — which is the entire lesson of this guide in one paragraph.</p>
<h2>The decision, job by job</h2>
<p><strong>Uploading a photo to a form, portal or marketplace:</strong> JPG. Small, accepted everywhere, quality more than sufficient. If the form also imposes a KB cap, compress to the exact size rather than hoping — the <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a> fits a photo under a stated limit. <strong>Screenshots, diagrams, anything with text:</strong> PNG (or WebP lossless for a website). Crispness is the content. <strong>Logos and graphics with transparency:</strong> PNG as the master and for desktop destinations; WebP for the same graphic on a web page. <strong>Images for your own website:</strong> WebP by default — the weight saving is real money in load time — with JPG the fallback for the rare visitor on ancient software. <strong>Anything you will edit again:</strong> PNG, because every lossy re-save spends quality you cannot get back; convert the finished edit to its delivery format last, never first. <strong>A format your recipient cannot open</strong> (the WebP a browser saved, the AVIF a phone produced): convert to JPG for people and forms, PNG for editing desks — that is what the converters in the next paragraph exist for.</p>
<h2>Converting between them, honestly</h2>
<p>Every conversion in our toolbox runs in your browser: <a href="/png-to-jpg/">PNG to JPG</a> and <a href="/jpg-to-png/">JPG to PNG</a> for the classic pair, <a href="/jpg-to-webp/">JPG to WebP</a> and <a href="/png-to-webp/">PNG to WebP</a> going web-ward, <a href="/webp-to-jpg/">WebP to JPG</a> and <a href="/webp-to-png/">WebP to PNG</a> coming back. Two honest limits apply to all of them. Converting up cannot restore quality: a JPG widened into a PNG is a big file holding a JPG's worth of picture. And converting down is a one-way spend: keep the best original you have, convert copies for destinations, and the question "which format?" stays a choice instead of becoming a regret.</p>
</section>
<section class="section">
<h2>And the neighbours: GIF, AVIF, SVG, HEIC</h2>
<p>The three formats above cover most decisions, but four neighbours complete the map. <strong>GIF</strong> survives for one reason — simple animation — and is otherwise outclassed: 256 colours, huge for photos, stills better taken as PNG. <strong>AVIF</strong> is WebP's stronger, younger rival: smaller again at the same quality, growing browser support, but patchier acceptance in tools and uploads, so it is a format you convert <em>from</em> more often than you choose it as a destination today. <strong>SVG</strong> is not a photograph format at all but a drawing — infinitely scalable, ideal for logos and icons in their master form, and rendered to PNG or WebP at the size a destination needs. <strong>HEIC</strong> is the iPhone's storage format: excellent on the phone, unwelcome almost everywhere else, and handled by its own guide in this series. The pattern across all seven: keep the best master you have, and let the destination choose the delivery format.</p>
</section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-compress-a-photo-to-an-exact-size",
         title="How to Compress a Photo to an Exact KB Size | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to compress a photo to an exact size — 20KB, 50KB, 100KB or 200KB — for passport, visa, exam and job forms: resize first, then quality, then check the face is still clear.",
         body=page_head("How to Compress a Photo to an Exact KB Size", "The form does not want a smaller photo. It wants a photo under an exact limit — and still recognisably you.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>Why forms demand a number, not a look</h2>
<p>Passport and visa portals, exam registrations, job applications and government forms across the world state their photo rules as two numbers: dimensions in pixels and a ceiling in kilobytes — commonly 200KB, often 100KB, sometimes a strict 50KB or even 20KB for a signature. The ceiling exists because these systems store millions of photographs and print identity documents from them; the floor exists because a photo compressed into mush cannot identify anyone. Your job is to land between them: under the stated size, at the stated dimensions, with a face that still looks like a face. It is a three-variable problem, and it has a correct order of operations.</p>
<h2>Step one: dimensions before everything</h2>
<p>File size is mostly pixel count. A phone photo at 4000×3000 carries twelve million pixels; a passport slot asking for 600×600 needs less than a twentieth of them. No amount of quality adjustment makes an oversized canvas light, so resize first: crop to the form's exact shape (the <a href="/image-cropper/">Image Cropper</a> handles the passport rectangle and squares), then set the pixel dimensions the form specifies, or a modest size comfortably above them if it only states a KB limit. Resizing to the destination is not losing quality — it is removing pixels the destination was going to throw away itself, badly, after upload.</p>
<h2>Step two: quality, aimed at a target</h2>
<p>With dimensions sane, size is governed by the JPG quality setting, and the honest way to hit an exact ceiling is to aim at it rather than guess: this is precisely what the <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a> does — type the target (50, 100, 200, or a custom figure), and it works down through quality steps until the photo fits under your limit at the best quality that fits. Working downward from high quality matters: it finds the clearest version of your photo that satisfies the form, instead of a fixed low setting that sacrifices faces it did not need to sacrifice. If the result still cannot fit, the dimensions — not the quality — are what to reduce next; a slightly smaller photo at decent quality beats a large photo smeared to fit.</p>
<h2>Step three: check like an official</h2>
<p>Before you upload, open the compressed photo at 100% and look at it the way the receiving human or system will. Is the face clear, evenly lit, unmistakably you? Are the eyes sharp — the first thing both people and face-matching systems read? Is the background the plain colour the form asked for, without compression blotches swarming in it? Flat backgrounds are where hard compression shows first, as visible banding and speckle; if the background is dirty but the face is fine, you have usually still passed — but if the face itself has gone soft or blocky, go back a step: shave dimensions a little and let the quality rise. Also confirm the mundane killers: correct orientation, the exact pixel dimensions in the file properties, and the size in KB as the form counts it.</p>
<h2>The common failures</h2>
<p>Compressing before cropping — wasting the size budget on background the form will cut away. Screenshotting the photo as a shortcut, which destroys resolution and adds interface artefacts. Saving as PNG for a photo slot, guaranteeing a file several times too heavy. And the panic error: shrinking to a fraction of the requested dimensions "to be safe", producing a thumbnail the system then stretches into a blur on an ID card. The limit is a ceiling, not a target to dive under — the best file is the largest, clearest one that still fits. Your photo is processed entirely in your browser throughout: the picture on an identity document is the last image that should ever be uploaded to a stranger's server to make it smaller.</p>
</section>
<section class="section">
<h2>The specs people actually meet</h2>
<p>The numbers differ by country and institution, but the shapes repeat. Digital passport and visa photos commonly ask for a square or near-square image — the US digital passport photo, for instance, is specified at 600×600 pixels minimum in JPG at no more than 240KB — generous by form standards. Indian exam and recruitment portals (the SSC, UPSC and banking families) are stricter and smaller: photographs often capped between 20KB and 50KB at modest dimensions, with a separate, smaller signature image sometimes capped under 20KB — which is why a signature should be cropped tight to the writing before it is compressed, since most of its canvas is blank paper the budget should not pay for. University and job portals across many countries cluster at 100KB or 200KB for the photograph. Two habits survive all of them: read the limit off the actual form in front of you rather than a memory of a similar one (they change quietly, year to year), and keep an uncompressed master of the same photo, so next season's different numbers are a fresh conversion rather than a rescue job on an already-squeezed file.</p>
</section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-convert-heic-photos-for-windows-and-the-web",
         title="How to Convert iPhone HEIC Photos for Windows and the Web | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="Why iPhone photos are HEIC, why Windows, forms and websites refuse them, and how to convert HEIC to JPG, PNG or WebP for each destination — plus the iPhone settings that prevent the problem.",
         body=page_head("How to Convert iPhone HEIC Photos for Windows and the Web", "Your iPhone shoots a format half the world cannot open. Here is the translation guide, by destination.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>Why your photos are HEIC in the first place</h2>
<p>Since the iPhone 7 era, Apple has saved photos in HEIC — the still-image flavour of the HEIF standard — because it stores the same photograph in roughly half the space of a JPG, with better handling of colour and depth data besides. On Apple devices this is invisible and entirely beneficial. The bill arrives when a photo leaves the garden: Windows PCs without the (separately installed, occasionally paid) HEIF codec show blank icons; Android phones vary; web upload forms, CMSs and most websites refuse or ignore the files outright; and plenty of professional and legacy software has never heard of the format. The photo is fine. Its passport is not.</p>
<h2>Choose the destination format by where the photo is going</h2>
<p><strong>Windows, email, forms, other people: JPG.</strong> The <a href="/heic-to-jpg/">HEIC to JPG converter</a> produces the file every PC, portal and person on earth can open, at a size close to the HEIC original. This covers nine out of ten real cases — sharing, uploading, attaching, printing at a shop. <strong>Editing, retouching, archiving: PNG.</strong> <a href="/heic-to-png/">HEIC to PNG</a> decodes the photo once into a lossless file, so later edits and saves never degrade it, and it will open in every tool for decades. The files are big — that is fidelity's price, paid deliberately for photos that matter. <strong>Your own website: WebP.</strong> <a href="/heic-to-webp/">HEIC to WebP</a> lands the photo at web weight, smaller than the JPG equivalent, native to every browser — the right shipping format when the audience is a page load.</p>
<h2>Converting in batches, and what survives</h2>
<p>Nobody converts one photo; the camera roll holds hundreds. Work through them in batches, keep the original HEIC files as your archive (storage is cheap, reshooting is impossible), and convert copies per destination. Know what a conversion carries: the full resolution and the picture itself, faithfully. What typically does not travel is the metadata wrapped around it — camera settings, location, depth maps and the Live Photo's motion are HEIC-container features that a JPG does not inherit — and Apple's HDR colour can render slightly flatter in a plain JPG on a non-HDR screen. For ordinary photographs in ordinary light, the converted JPG simply looks like the photo. For the one irreplaceable shot, keep the HEIC beside whatever you convert.</p>
<h2>Stop the problem at the source (optional)</h2>
<p>If your life is mostly Windows and web, the iPhone can simply stop shooting HEIC: Settings → Camera → Formats → Most Compatible makes every new photo a JPG — larger on the phone, welcome everywhere else. The gentler setting lives in Settings → Photos: "Transfer to Mac or PC — Automatic" makes the phone hand over JPGs when you connect it to a computer, while the originals stay HEIC on the device. Neither helps the existing library — for that, conversion is the only road — but they decide whether next year's photos arrive pre-translated.</p>
<h2>Privacy, briefly, because these are your photos</h2>
<p>Every converter named in this guide runs in your browser: the photos are decoded and re-encoded on the device in your hand, never uploaded to a server. For a camera roll — the most personal dataset most people own — that is not a feature detail, it is the whole point. Convert, download, close the tab; nothing of yours remains anywhere.</p>
</section>
<section class="section">
<h2>Live Photos, videos and screenshots: the edge cases</h2>
<p>A Live Photo is not one file but two: an HEIC still and a short video clip bound together, and converting the still leaves the motion behind — the converted JPG is the key moment, frozen, which is usually what was wanted, but if the value was the movement, export the clip as video from the Photos app instead of converting the picture. iPhone screenshots, note, are already PNG, and screen recordings are MP4 video; neither needs this guide. And photos that arrive from an iPhone through some apps come pre-converted without telling you — if a ".jpg" from an Apple device refuses to open on an old Windows machine, suspect a HEIC wearing a JPG's name from a careless transfer, and run it through the converter anyway: a genuine JPG passes through unchanged in substance, a disguised HEIC comes out honest.</p>
<h2>If Windows must open them natively</h2>
<p>Conversion is the reliable road, but Windows can be taught HEIC directly: Microsoft's HEIF Image Extensions, available from the Microsoft Store (free on most systems, occasionally a small charge for the video extension sibling), let Explorer and the Photos app open HEIC files as if they were JPGs. That solves viewing on your own machine. It does nothing for uploads, forms, other people's computers or the web — the four places this guide exists for — which is why conversion, not codecs, remains the working answer for any photo that has to travel.</p>
</section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/pdf-compression-trade-offs",
         title="PDF Compression Trade-offs: Smaller Files vs Clarity and Selectable Text | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="What PDF compression actually changes — image resolution, clarity, print quality — what it must never change (selectable text, form fields, signatures), and how to verify the result before you send it.",
         body=page_head("PDF Compression Trade-offs: Smaller Files vs Clarity and Selectable Text", "Compression is a bargain, not a spell. This guide itemises what you are trading, so the bargain is a good one.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>Where a PDF's weight actually lives</h2>
<p>A PDF's text weighs almost nothing — a dense page of type is a few kilobytes of character data. The weight is images: a scanned page is a photograph; an exported report carries charts, logos and screenshots; a camera-made PDF is photographs wearing a document's coat. A five-page scan at full camera resolution can outweigh a five-hundred-page novel in PDF form by fifty to one. This is why compression works at all — there is almost always a large, compressible image layer sitting on top of a featherweight text layer — and it is also where every trade-off in this guide lives: compressing a PDF means negotiating with its images, and the terms of that negotiation decide whether the result is a triumph or a quietly ruined document.</p>
<h2>Trade-off one: image resolution vs clarity</h2>
<p>Compression's first lever is re-rendering page images at lower resolution. The honest scale is dots per inch relative to the final use. For a document that will only ever be read on screens, images at around 150 DPI look clean — screens cannot show more. For office printing, 200–300 DPI keeps text in scanned pages and fine lines crisp. Below roughly 100 DPI the cost becomes visible: small print softens, signatures lose their character, stamps and seals blur, and diagrams with fine lines start to dissolve. The craft is matching the level to the destination: the application form headed for a screen review can take the deep compression its 200KB limit demands (our <a href="/compress-pdf/">Compress PDF</a> works down through gentler settings toward the target you set); the contract archive or the print-bound report deserves the mildest setting that satisfies the requirement, and its original should be kept regardless.</p>
<h2>Trade-off two: the text layer — the one that must not be spent</h2>
<p>A born-digital PDF carries its text as text: selectable, searchable, copyable, readable by screen readers and by the systems that index and process documents. That layer is worth more than size. Some aggressive "flattening" compression routes re-render whole pages as pictures — the file may shrink, but its text becomes pixels: unsearchable, uncopyable, inaccessible, and subtly softer to read. Proper compression leaves the text layer alone and negotiates only with the images. The verification takes ten seconds and should be a reflex after any compression: open the result, try to select a line of text with the mouse, and run a word search. If both work, the document survived whole. (If a document's text refuses to extract even before compression, it was a scan — a photograph all along; the <a href="/pdf-to-text/">PDF to Text</a> tool shows you exactly what text layer exists.) Form fields and placed signatures deserve the same suspicion: check that fields still accept typing and a signature still sits where it was put.</p>
<h2>Trade-off three: colour, and the small print of small print</h2>
<p>Deep compression also re-encodes images more aggressively, and its artefacts appear first where documents are most fragile: in flat tints (logos and charts develop banding), around sharp edges (small text in screenshots grows halos), and in photographs of pages, where paper texture turns to noise that then wastes the very bytes you were saving. This is why "smallest possible" is the wrong goal: each step down buys fewer bytes at a rising quality price, and the curve's knee — where the document still looks like itself — is usually one or two settings above the floor. Choose the target by the rule that imposed it (a form's KB limit, an email ceiling) and stop there; compressing beyond the requirement spends quality on nothing.</p>
<h2>When not to compress at all</h2>
<p>Keep the untouched original whenever the document is evidence, archive or master: the signed contract, the certificate, the thesis submission, the accounts. Compress copies for destinations — the upload, the email, the form — and let the original sit at full weight in your files. Storage is cheap; regeneration is impossible. And do the whole job where the document stays yours: our compressor runs in your browser, so the contract, statement or application being squeezed never leaves your device. A document important enough to compress carefully is important enough not to upload carelessly.</p>
</section>
<section class="section">
<h2>A sixty-second pre-send checklist</h2>
<p>Before the compressed file goes anywhere, run the checks that catch every failure mode in this guide. One: the file size and page count are what the destination demands — compression never changes the page count, and a changed count means something else went wrong. Two: select a line of text and search for a word, proving the text layer survived. Three: view the document's most demanding page at 100% — the smallest print, the densest table, the official stamp — and judge its clarity against the original side by side. Four: if it carries form fields or a signature, confirm they are alive and in place. Five: the untouched original still exists in your files under its own name. A compressed PDF that passes all five is not a compromise; it is the same document, deliberately packed. One that fails any of them is a draft, and the fix is always the same — go back to the original and pack it again, one setting gentler.</p>
</section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/how-to-create-a-qr-code-and-test-it",
         title="How to Create a QR Code and Test It Before Printing | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="How to create a QR code that actually scans in the real world — short links, error correction levels, print size, contrast and quiet zones — and the two-minute print test that prevents a useless print run.",
         body=page_head("How to Create a QR Code and Test It Before Printing", "Making the code takes ten seconds. Making one that still scans on a rainy night, at arm's length, on a curved bottle — that is the guide.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>Start with what the code carries</h2>
<p>A QR code is a container, and everything about its real-world performance follows from what you put in it. The governing law: more data means a denser code — more, smaller modules packed into the same square — and denser codes are harder for cameras to read at small sizes, at angles and in poor light. So the first craft decision is to shorten the payload. A long URL full of tracking parameters makes a fragile code; the same destination behind a short, clean link (<em>yoursite.com/menu</em> rather than a hundred-character string) makes a bold one. Use our <a href="/qr-code-generator/">QR Code Generator</a> with the shortest honest destination you have, and let the code stay coarse and confident.</p>
<h2>Error correction: buy insurance with density</h2>
<p>QR codes carry Reed–Solomon error correction, which lets a code survive damage — a scratch, a sticker's corner, a coffee splash — and still scan. It comes in four levels: L restores about 7% of the code, M about 15%, Q about 25% and H about 30%. The price of higher correction is, again, density. The working choices: for a code that will live on screens or clean indoor paper, M is plenty. For anything printed, handled, outdoors or partly covered by design elements, Q or H is the professional default — H is also the level that tolerates a small logo placed in the code's centre, since the correction is precisely what reconstructs the covered modules. Never pay for H density on a long payload at a tiny size, though: insurance and cargo compete for the same square.</p>
<h2>Size, contrast and the quiet zone</h2>
<p>Three physical rules decide most failures. <strong>Size:</strong> the dependable minimum for a printed code is about 2 × 2 cm for near scanning (menus, packaging), scaled up with viewing distance — a poster meant to be scanned from two metres needs a code nearer 15–20 cm; the rule of thumb is a tenth of the intended scanning distance. <strong>Contrast:</strong> dark modules on a light background, as dark and light as you can make them — black on white remains undefeated. Inverted codes (white on black) scan on many modern phones and fail on enough older ones to be a gamble; brand-coloured codes work only when the contrast stays severe. <strong>The quiet zone:</strong> the code needs a margin of empty space around it — roughly four modules wide — because scanners locate the code by that silence. Cropping tight against artwork is the most common self-inflicted QR failure in professional print.</p>
<h2>The two-minute test that saves the print run</h2>
<p>Print one copy, at final size, on the final material if you can. Then test like a stranger: scan it with at least two different phones (an older one if the audience will have them), at the distance and in the lighting where it will actually live — the table tent under restaurant light, the poster across the pavement, the label on a curved bottle (curvature distorts edge modules; sleeves and bottles deserve their own test). Tilt the phone; let a shadow fall on it; scan it quickly, the way people actually do. Then follow the code all the way: does it land exactly where promised, on a page that loads fast and reads well on a phone? A code that scans into a broken or desktop-only page is a failure wearing a success's clothes. Only when the single printed copy survives all of that is it a code — until then it is a picture of one.</p>
<h2>One last decision: the destination's future</h2>
<p>A printed QR code is permanent in a way its destination may not be: posters outlive campaigns, packaging outlives promotions. Point the code at an address you control and can redirect or update — your own domain, not a temporary link — because the code on ten thousand leaflets will faithfully send people wherever that address leads for years. Generate it on your device (the generator here sends nothing anywhere), print it after testing, and the smallest square in your design will also be the hardest-working.</p>
</section>
<section class="section">
<h2>Beyond links: Wi-Fi, contacts and text</h2>
<p>A URL is the commonest payload, not the only one. A Wi-Fi QR code — encoding the network name, password and security type in a standard format — lets guests join a network by pointing a camera, and has become standard in rentals, cafés and offices precisely because it removes the dictated-password ritual (and the password on a wall in plain text). A vCard code turns a poster or business card into a contact saved in one tap. Plain-text codes carry serial numbers, seat details and instructions where no network exists at all. The same laws govern all of them: more payload means more density, so a fat vCard with every field filled needs a bigger print size than a slim one; and a Wi-Fi code deserves one extra test — join your own network with it, on a phone that has never saved the password, before the guests arrive to do it for you.</p>
</section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/why-whatsapp-compresses-your-photos",
         title="Why WhatsApp Compresses Your Photos — And How to Send Them in Full Quality | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="Why WhatsApp compresses photos, what it does to their resolution and quality, and the two ways to send pictures in full quality — plus how to shrink a photo to an exact KB size when smaller is what you actually want.",
         body=page_head("Why WhatsApp Compresses Your Photos — And How to Send Them in Full Quality", "You sent a sharp photo and they received a soft one. Here is what WhatsApp did to it on the way — and how to send the real thing instead.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>What actually happens to your photo</h2>
<p>When you attach a photo to a chat the normal way, WhatsApp does not send your file. It builds a smaller copy of it: the resolution is cut down (typically to around 1,600 pixels on the long edge), the copy is re-encoded as a JPEG at a modest quality setting, and the metadata your camera recorded — location, settings, timestamps — is stripped away. A crisp 5 MB photo from a modern phone commonly arrives as a file of 100–300 KB. At chat size, on a phone screen, it looks fine. The moment the recipient zooms in, crops it, prints it or uses it in a document, the missing detail is gone for good — the softness was baked in before the message ever left your phone. Videos are squeezed harder still.</p>
<h2>Why WhatsApp does this</h2>
<p>It is a deliberate design choice, not a bug. WhatsApp is built for the whole world: budget phones, slow networks and places where mobile data is expensive. If every photo travelled at full size, messages would crawl, backups would balloon, and sending a holiday album would eat a week's data allowance. Compression keeps chat instant and cheap for billions of people — a fair trade for conversation. The mistake is treating a chat app as a photo-delivery service. It is a photocopier tuned for speed, and sometimes you need the original document, not the photocopy.</p>
<h2>Fix one: switch on HD quality</h2>
<p>WhatsApp offers a middle path. In <strong>Settings → Storage and data → Media upload quality</strong>, choose <strong>HD quality</strong> (on some phones you can also tap the HD button while sending a photo). HD sends a much higher-resolution version that keeps most of the visible detail — faces stay sharp, text in photos stays readable, and zooming in no longer turns everything to watercolour. The honest caveat: HD is still a re-encode, not your original file, and it uses more data than standard quality. For most people, most of the time, it is the right balance.</p>
<h2>Fix two: send the photo as a document</h2>
<p>The only route that delivers the exact original — every pixel, byte for byte — is sending the photo as a document instead of as a picture. On Android: open the chat, tap the paperclip, choose <strong>Document</strong>, then browse to the photo and send it. On iPhone: open the photo in Photos, tap Share → <strong>Save to Files</strong>, then in WhatsApp tap <strong>+</strong> → <strong>Document</strong> and pick the file from Files. The recipient receives the true file, at full resolution, with nothing re-encoded. The trade-offs are real but mild: the download is bigger, and the photo arrives as a file to open rather than a picture in the chat flow. For wedding photos, work shots, scans and anything destined for print, it is worth it.</p>
<h2>When you actually want it smaller — on your terms</h2>
<p>Sometimes compression is the goal: an application form demands a photo under 50KB or 200KB, a marketplace caps image sizes, or you are on a slow connection and would rather choose the compromise yourself than let WhatsApp choose it for you. That is a job for our <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a>: choose the photo, pick the exact target size in KB, and compress — you see the result and its quality before you download, and you can aim for the largest size your limit allows instead of the smallest possible mush. Two relatives help nearby jobs: iPhone photos are HEIC files that some devices and websites refuse to open, so run them through <a href="/heic-to-jpg/">HEIC to JPG</a> before sending; and when a site wants exact pixel dimensions for a profile or ID photo, the <a href="/image-resizer/">Image Resizer</a> sets them precisely. All of it happens in your browser — the photo never leaves your device.</p>
<h2>And if someone sends you a blurry photo?</h2>
<p>Compression cannot be undone at your end. No app can restore detail that was stripped before the photo reached you — anything that claims to "enhance" it is guessing at what the missing pixels probably looked like. The only real fix is at the source: ask the sender to send it again as a document, or with HD quality switched on. It takes them thirty seconds, and it is the difference between a photo you can keep and one you can only glance at.</p>
<h2>The rule to remember</h2>
<p>Chat for talking, documents for delivering. If the photo is a moment in a conversation, standard or HD quality is fine. If the photo itself is the point — it will be printed, edited, archived or submitted — send it as a document, or compress it deliberately to the size the job requires. Either way, the choice is yours, made before the file leaves your phone, instead of WhatsApp's, made silently after.</p>
</section>"""),

    dict(slug="guides/pdf-too-big-to-email",
         title="PDF Too Big to Email? How to Shrink Any PDF Under 25 MB in Seconds | ToolPixi Guides",
         desc="Gmail bounces attachments over 25 MB — and email encoding quietly makes the real limit lower. Why PDFs get so heavy, and how to compress one under the limit in seconds, free, in your browser, without uploading it anywhere.",
         body=page_head("PDF Too Big to Email? How to Shrink Any PDF Under 25 MB in Seconds", "The bounce-back says your attachment is too large. Here is why PDFs get heavy, what the limit really is — and the fastest honest way under it.")
         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The limits — including the hidden one</h2>
<p>The headline numbers are easy to find: Gmail allows attachments up to 25 MB, Outlook.com caps them at 20 MB, and Yahoo Mail allows 25 MB. The hidden catch is that email attachments do not travel as-is. They are encoded into the message (in a format called Base64), and that encoding inflates the attachment by roughly a third. A 24 MB PDF can become a message of more than 30 MB and bounce even though the file itself looked "under the limit". The practical rule used by people who send documents all day: <strong>aim for 20 MB or smaller</strong>, and smaller still if the recipient's provider is stricter than yours. Under that line, everything just works.</p>
<h2>Why your PDF is so heavy</h2>
<p>It is almost never the text. A dense page of type weighs a few kilobytes; a five-hundred-page novel in PDF form can be smaller than one phone photo. The weight is images. A scanned page is a photograph of a page. A report carries screenshots and charts as full-resolution pictures. A PDF exported from a camera or a scanning app is photographs wearing a document's coat — a ten-page scan at full camera resolution easily reaches 20–50 MB. That is good news: it means nearly all of the size is compressible, because the same page with its images re-encoded at a sensible resolution looks identical on screen and in print at a fraction of the weight. Our guide to <a href="/guides/pdf-compression-trade-offs/">PDF compression trade-offs</a> explains the mechanics in depth; the short version is that shrinking a PDF means negotiating with its images.</p>
<h2>Why not just zip it?</h2>
<p>Because it barely works, and it is worth knowing why. A PDF is already a compressed container — its text and much of its structure are packed efficiently when the file is created. Putting it in a ZIP archive squeezes what is left, which for a typical document is a few percent at best. A 40 MB scan zips down to perhaps 38 MB: still far too big, and now the recipient has an extra step before they can even open it. Re-rendering the pages at a sensible size, the way real PDF compression works, routinely removes 80–95% of a scan's weight instead.</p>
<h2>The fast fix: compress it in your browser</h2>
<p>Open the <a href="/compress-pdf/">PDF Compressor</a>, choose your PDF, and pick a target size. The tool re-encodes the pictures inside the PDF at the highest quality that fits the target, stepping down only as far as it must — the text and the pages themselves are never touched. For email, the largest target — 500 KB — is already far below every provider's limit, and pages stay perfectly readable for contracts, certificates, statements and reports. If a document genuinely cannot reach a target without turning unreadable, the tool tells you plainly and still hands you the smallest clean result rather than a blurry file that wastes everyone's time. Check the result's size in the summary, open it, zoom into one dense page to confirm it reads clearly, and attach it. Because the compression runs in your browser, the document is never uploaded to a stranger's server — which matters, because files too big to email are usually the private ones.</p>
<h2>The trade-off we state openly</h2>
<p>This style of compression re-encodes only the pictures inside the PDF. The file looks the same and prints the same, and its text stays selectable, searchable and copyable, because the pages themselves are never re-drawn. (Some older compressors flatten pages into images, which does kill the text layer — avoid those for anything you may edit later, or keep the original.) If a file is still too large after compression, share it through a cloud-drive link instead, or use our <a href="/split-pdf/">Split PDF</a> tool to send the document in two parts across two emails. And whatever route you choose, keep your own original untouched — the compressed copy is for sending, not for keeping.</p>
<h2>If it still will not fit</h2>
<p>A very long, photo-dense PDF occasionally refuses every reasonable target. Three exits remain, in order of preference. First, compress to the smallest clean result the tool offers and check its actual size — if it lands under your 20 MB safety line, the problem is already solved. Second, split the PDF into halves with <a href="/split-pdf/">Split PDF</a> and send two emails, each safely under the limit; recipients reassemble nothing — two attachments read in order work fine. Third, when a document is simply huge by nature (a print-ready portfolio, a photo book), email was the wrong vehicle from the start: upload it to a drive and send the link.</p>
<h2>Before you press send</h2>
<p>Twenty seconds of checking saves the bounce-and-apologise cycle: open the compressed PDF once, on the device you are sending from; zoom into the smallest text on any page and confirm it is crisp; confirm the file size shown is the size of the file you are attaching, not the original sitting beside it; and only then send. The recipient gets a document that arrives the first time, opens instantly, and reads exactly as you intended.</p>
</section>"""),

