         + """<section class="section">
<h2>The countdown, in plain words</h2>
<p>Christmas Day is 25 December every year, and in 2026 it falls on a <strong>Friday</strong> — so the countdown ends at the start of a long weekend for most people, with Boxing Day on Saturday, 26 December. Advent, the season churches and advent calendars count from, begins on Sunday, 29 November 2026, the fourth Sunday before Christmas Day.</p>
<p>The number that matters is live, not printed: our <a href="/christmas-countdown/">Christmas Countdown</a> ticks down in days, hours, minutes and seconds, recalculated every second from your own device clock, so it is exact in your timezone. The plainer <a href="/days-until-christmas/">Days Until Christmas</a> counter shows the same live figure. Open either page on Christmas Day and it celebrates the day; from 26 December both roll over and start counting down to next year's Christmas, so the page is never out of date.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Days, weeks, sleeps and weekends</h2>
<p>One number spawns four questions, and all four come from the same live days figure. <strong>Sleeps:</strong> one sleep per night, so the number of sleeps until Christmas is simply the days figure — if the counter says 12 days, there are 12 more nights of sleep first. Children ask in sleeps; the answer is the same number, in a friendlier unit.</p>
<p><strong>Weeks:</strong> divide the days by seven — 35 days left is exactly 5 weeks; 40 days is 5 weeks and 5 days. <strong>Weekends:</strong> roughly one full weekend in every block of seven days, so divide by seven and round down — 35 days out, five full weekends remain, the honest count of how many shopping weekends you really have left.</p>
<p>The weekday does quiet work of its own. A Friday Christmas means most people finish work on the Thursday or Friday and wake up to Christmas morning with no working day in between, and the long weekend stretches through Boxing Day on the Saturday. A Christmas that lands midweek leaves ordinary working days on either side — the date is the same, the shape of the holiday is not.</p>
</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Every countdown on ToolPixi</h2>
<p>Christmas is the countdown everyone asks for, but it is only one of sixteen event countdowns on the site — each one a live timer in days, hours, minutes and seconds, each with its own page explaining the date, the weekday pattern and the questions people ask about it. The full set:</p>
<ul>
